; SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
;
; binder — the symbol half of the reference's internal/binder/binder.go.
;
; ── What this module is FOR, and what a slice of it is ──────────────────────
;
; The binder walks the parsed tree, decides which nodes are DECLARATIONS, makes a
; Symbol for each and files it in a table — the enclosing container's `locals`, or
; the container symbol's `members` or `exports` — merging declarations that share
; a name and reporting a diagnostic where they may not merge. Its output is what
; the fourth yardstick compares (tests/oracle/symbols.go, TESTPLAN.md's *The
; fourth yardstick*), and it is compared as SYMBOL IDENTITY rather than as shape:
; two `var x` are one symbol with two declarations, and a port that made two
; symbols would produce the identical picture at every node.
;
; ── What is NOT ported, and why each omission is a decision ─────────────────
;
; ★★★ THE FLOW GRAPH IS PORTED AS OF SLICE 92, AND THE SENTENCE THAT STOOD HERE
; FOR SIXTY SLICES WAS HALF WRONG IN THE HALF THAT MATTERED. It read: *"THE FLOW
; GRAPH IS NOT PORTED AT ALL … and NONE of it is reachable through an exported
; accessor, so the symbols dump cannot see it and neither can any dump this suite
; has."* The first clause was a fact about this port; the second was a claim about
; the REFERENCE, and it is false — `Node.FlowNodeData()`, `BodyBase.EndFlowNode`,
; `SourceFile.EndFlowNode`, the four `ReturnFlowNode` slots and
; `CaseOrDefaultClause.FallthroughFlowNode` are all exported, and an oracle in
; tests/oracle is BUILT INSIDE the reference's own module. So what blocked the
; largest missing mechanism in this port — the head of the stage-2 work list twice
; over — was not its size. It was a dump that nobody had written, and §3.5be
; forbids machinery no instrument can reach. tests/oracle/flow.go is ten lines of
; walk over those accessors; the fifth yardstick is the slice.
;
; ★★ WHAT THE FLOW HALF ALSO DECIDES, and what slices 33–36 ported for that reason
; alone, is the TRAVERSAL ORDER: `bindChildren` routes a dozen statement and
; expression kinds through flow-shaped helpers, and two of them visit children in
; an order ForEachChild does not — bindParameterFlow binds a parameter's NAME
; last, after its type and initializer, and bindBindingElementFlow does the same.
; The order decides the order symbols are created in and therefore the order
; DIAGNOSTICS come out in, both of which are compared. Those readings all survived
; slice 92 unchanged, which is the check on them: the graph arrived and not one
; traversal moved.
;
; ★★ THE ONE PIECE OF THE FLOW HALF THAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE is `b.emitFlags`
; — the HasAsyncFunctions bit, three writers upstream and one reader, a SourceFile
; node flag no dump in this directory carries. bindContainer's source-file arm ORs
; it into the file's flags and this port does not; the flow dump's `r` section
; masks to the three REACHABILITY bits, so the omission is stated rather than
; hidden behind a green run.
;
; ★★ AND WHAT IS NOT PORTED IS REPORTED. A kind the reference's `bind` switch or
; `bindChildren` switch has an arm for and this slice does not stops the bind and
; records `unported` with the KIND as its detail — the same mechanism the parser
; uses, and the same reason: an incomplete port that answered a well-formed dump
; would be reporting agreement it has not earned. tests/triage.py groups the
; column by tag, so the histogram names the arm that unlocks the most units next.
;
; ── The slice line ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; Slice 27 ports the SKELETON and the arms a file of variables and functions is
; made of: the container walk with its locals, declareSymbol with the duplicate
; diagnostics, the source file (including its module-ness), the variable
; declaration, the parameter, the function declaration and the type parameter.
; Slice 28 adds the MODULE SYMBOL. Slice 29 adds the import/export ALIAS family:
; the four kinds the reference binds through one line (an import-equals, a
; namespace import, an import specifier and an export specifier), plus the four
; arms with a body of their own — bindImportClause, bindExportDeclaration,
; bindExportAssignment and bindNamespaceExportDeclaration. Slice 30 adds the
; CLASS-LIKE family and the ten member kinds that declare into the tables those
; containers own. Slice 31 adds the MODULE DECLARATION: bindModuleDeclaration,
; declareModuleSymbol and ast.GetModuleInstanceState, which is the first ported
; function in this package that has to WALK a body to answer a question about
; the node above it. Slice 32 adds the OBJECT LITERAL family: the literal
; itself, its two property kinds, and the shared assignment-pattern case label
; of bindChildren — whose whole content beyond bindEachChild is one assignment
; to a flow field nothing ported can read. Slice 33 opens the FLOW chapter with
; the two heads of the histogram, the BINARY EXPRESSION and the CALL: three
; traversals that are not ForEachChild's (a destructuring assignment's operand
; swap, a binary expression's four-of-five slots, an IIFE's arguments before its
; callee), the binary arm's strict-mode diagnostic, and the optional-chain family
; — which brings the property access, the element access and the non-null
; expression with it, because those three arms ARE that one branch. What it does
; not bring is the JavaScript ASSIGNMENT DECLARATIONS both arms open on: those
; are classified, reported by kind, and belong to a slice of their own.
; Slice 34 adds the four function-like heads — whose yield is almost entirely the
; WALK, since each of them used to report and return over a whole subtree. Slice
; 35 closes the `bind` switch, and adds the six bindChildren arms without which
; five of its own checks could not be measured. Slice 36 closes the bindChildren
; switch with the statement-flow chapter: thirteen kinds, ten of them
; ForEachChild's own order and three a reorder the bind-diagnostic section can
; see. Slice 37 opens the JavaScript assignment declarations from the end that has
; ONE piece of machinery behind it — the three kinds that share
; setCommonJSModuleIndicator, plus the `require` call under a marker of its own.
; Slice 38 adds the other half of that family, and it is a second PASS rather than
; an arm: every `f.a = 1` and `Object.defineProperty(f, "a", …)` is REMEMBERED with
; the container registers of its own moment and bound after the whole walk, because
; an expando attaches to a symbol that must already carry its value declaration,
; and a host that is not a function declaration is bound in SOURCE ORDER.
; Slice 92 adds the FLOW GRAPH: the node constructors and the label bookkeeping,
; bindContainer's control-flow arm with the reachability bits it writes,
; bindChildren's unreachable head and its per-statement slot, the seven other slot
; writes — two of which live in DECLARATION arms where no reading of the switch
; finds them — and the seventeen statement and expression arms slices 33–36 left
; as traversals. Nothing in either switch moves; what arrives is the graph.
;
; ★★★ SO NOTHING IN EITHER SWITCH REPORTS ITSELF ANY MORE, and as of slice 42 no
; assignment-declaration KIND does either — the family is complete, `this.a = x`
; included. What is left of the `unported` column is markers INSIDE ported arms:
; an `infer` type parameter, a numeric declaration name, and an error range with no
; name to point at. Both inventory functions stay, empty, as the place §2's step 3
; re-reads after a pin bump.

use scaly.memory.Page
use scaly.containers.Array
use scaly.containers.String
use tscaly.scanner.Scanner
use tscaly.parser.Parser
use tscaly.ast.AstNode
use tscaly.ast.Symbol
use tscaly.ast.SymbolTable
use tscaly.ast.NodeData
use tscaly.ast.FlowNode
use tscaly.ast.FlowList
use tscaly.ast.FlowSwitchClauseData
use tscaly.ast.FlowReduceLabelData
use tscaly.Diagnostics.DiagnosticList

; ── ContainerFlags ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's own enum, hand-ported: eleven bits, no composites, and the
; comments are upstream's because each one says what the bit COSTS at the call
; site (a container becomes the current container, a block-scoped container only
; the current block-scope, and only HasLocals gets a table).

define ContainerFlagsNone: int 0
define ContainerFlagsIsContainer: int 1 << 0
define ContainerFlagsIsBlockScopedContainer: int 1 << 1
define ContainerFlagsIsControlFlowContainer: int 1 << 2
define ContainerFlagsIsFunctionLike: int 1 << 3
define ContainerFlagsIsFunctionExpression: int 1 << 4
define ContainerFlagsHasLocals: int 1 << 5
define ContainerFlagsIsInterface: int 1 << 6
define ContainerFlagsIsObjectLiteralOrClassExpressionMethodOrAccessor: int 1 << 7
define ContainerFlagsIsThisContainer: int 1 << 8
define ContainerFlagsPropagatesThisKeyword: int 1 << 9

; ── ModuleInstanceState ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ast.ModuleInstanceState, and the NUMBERS are load-bearing rather than
; incidental: getModuleInstanceStateWorker takes a MAXIMUM over an export
; clause's specifiers with `>`, and upstream's iota order puts ConstEnumOnly
; ABOVE Instantiated — so a const-enum-only specifier wins the max while only an
; Instantiated one ends the loop early. Renumbering these would be a silent
; change of answer on a mixed clause.
define ModuleInstanceStateUnknown: int 0
define ModuleInstanceStateNonInstantiated: int 1
define ModuleInstanceStateInstantiated: int 2
define ModuleInstanceStateConstEnumOnly: int 3

; ── the three side structures GetModuleInstanceState needs ──────────────────
;
; ★★★ `ancestors []*Node` is a stack of VIRTUAL parents, and it exists because
; the function is called DURING the bind: a module's own Parent chain is set, its
; body's children's is not, and getModuleInstanceStateForAliasTarget walks
; UPWARDS out of the node it was handed. Upstream pushes with `append` and pops
; with a re-slice, so the value is only ever truncated from the end — which is a
; CONS LIST with the aliasing question removed. Two appends onto one base slice
; would write the same index twice in Go; here two pushes onto one link cannot
; interfere, and no reader can tell the difference because every reader reads at
; its own length.
define AncestorLink
(
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    prev: ref[AncestorLink]?
)

; `visited map[NodeId]ModuleInstanceState`, as an association LIST. The map is a
; memo over a deterministic function, so it is unobservable — EXCEPT for the one
; thing it is really there for: an entry is written as Unknown BEFORE the worker
; runs, so a cycle through an export alias answers NonInstantiated instead of
; recursing forever. A linear scan answers exactly that, over the statements of
; one module body.
;
; ★ The lookup is by NODE POINTER where upstream keys by GetNodeId. Those are the
; same identity here and the pointer is the cheaper spelling of it — §3.10 gives
; this port its ids at PARSE time precisely so that identity is not a mutation of
; the tree.
define VisitedLink
(
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    state: int
    next: ref[VisitedLink]?
)

define VisitedSet (head: ref[VisitedLink]?)

; `b.notConstEnumOnlyModules collections.Set[*Symbol]`, as a list for the same
; reason: it holds one entry per module symbol that has been observed NOT to be
; const-enum-only, and it is asked once per module declaration. A field on Symbol
; would be invisible to the dump and would work; the reference keeps the question
; out of the symbol, and so does this.
define SymbolLink
(
    symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    next: ref[SymbolLink]?
)

; `binder.ExpandoAssignmentInfo`, slice 38: one entry per JavaScript expando
; assignment met during the walk, holding the node and the two container
; registers as they stood AT THAT MOMENT.
;
; ★★★ THE REGISTERS ARE THE WHOLE POINT OF THE RECORD, and they are why the
; deferred pass is a pass rather than an arm. `f.a = 1` has to look `f` up in the
; scope the assignment is WRITTEN in, and by the time the second pass runs the
; walk is over and both registers point at whatever the last container was. So
; the reference captures them per entry and restores them per entry — and it
; captures TWO of the three, which is a fact with a consequence: see
; bind_deferred_expando_assignments.
;
; ★ An Array of the record rather than a cons list, because the order is the
; walk's and the pass reads it front to back — `append` in Go, and the same
; growth here. Nothing truncates it, so the aliasing question the AncestorLink
; comment above answers does not arise.
define ExpandoAssignmentInfo
(
    node: ref[AstNode]
    container: ref[AstNode]?
    block_scope_container: ref[AstNode]?
)

; binder.ActiveLabel — one entry of the label stack a `break outer` / `continue
; outer` is resolved against. A LIST rather than an Array, because the reference's
; is a stack whose links are saved and restored by bindContainer: a container
; saves the head, binds, and puts the head back, so the entries below it must
; survive being detached and reattached.
;
; ★★ `referenced` is the field slice 36 named and deferred with its reason —
; *"the content is a NODE FLAG, and no dump in this suite carries a node's flags
; after a bind"*. This slice is the dump, so the field is real and its reader is
; bindLabeledStatement, which turns a false into NodeFlagsUnreachable on the LABEL
; node for the checker to report as TS7028.
;
; ★ The name is a (data, length) RANGE, the way every name in this port is: an
; identifier's text is a slice of the source and a String would copy it.
define ActiveLabel
(
    next: ref[ActiveLabel]?
    name: Slice[char]
    break_target: ref[FlowNode]?
    continue_target: ref[FlowNode]?
    referenced: bool
)
{
    init(nx: ref[ActiveLabel]?, nd: Slice[char], bt: ref[FlowNode]?)
    {
        set next: nx
        set name: nd
        set break_target: bt
        set continue_target: null
        set referenced: false
    }
}

define Binder
(
    host: ref[Page]

    ; The SourceFile node. The reference's `b.file` is the *ast.SourceFile; here
    ; every accessor takes the node, so the node is what is kept.
    file: ref[AstNode]?

    ; A SCANNER over the same source, and it is here for exactly one question:
    ; `SkipTrivia(text, pos)`, which every bind diagnostic's start position goes
    ; through (`GetErrorRangeForNode`). The reference's SkipTrivia is a free
    ; function over the text; this port's lives on the Scanner because the text
    ; does, so the binder holds one.
    ;
    ; ★ Slice 31: it SCANS now, at one site. errorOnFirstToken reports at
    ; `GetRangeOfTokenAtPosition(file, node.Pos())`, which is a fresh scanner, one
    ; Scan and the token's own [start, end) — see range_of_token_at_position for
    ; why the shared one is the same answer.
    trivia: ref[Scanner]

    ; `ast.IsExternalModule(b.file)`, computed ONCE at create because the reference
    ; computes it once too — its parser stores the indicator NODE on the SourceFile
    ; and IsExternalModule is a null test on it. This port answers the question
    ; instead of storing the node (§3.5q), so the answer is cached here rather than
    ; re-walked at every declaration.
    ;
    ; ★ `b.file.IsDeclarationFile` is NOT carried, and the reason is the rule this
    ; file's own header states from the other side: its only reader in binder.go is
    ; the emitFlags/HasAsyncFunctions bit, a SourceFile node flag that no dump here
    ; can see. A field nothing reads is only DECLARED (§3.5be), so the slice that
    ; makes that flag observable is the slice that adds it back.
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 37 SPLIT IT, and until this slice the two readings were one field
    ; because they could not disagree. `ast.IsExternalModule(file)` is
    ; `ExternalModuleIndicator != nil`; `ast.IsExternalOrCommonJSModule(file)` is
    ; that OR `CommonJSModuleIndicator != nil` — and the second indicator is
    ; written only by setCommonJSModuleIndicator, which this slice is the first to
    ; port. So `is_external_module` keeps the EXTERNAL half exactly and the
    ; disjunction moves to is_external_or_common_js_module, which is asked at the
    ; four sites the reference asks it at and at no other. Reading the wrong one
    ; is not a crash: at three of the four EXTERNAL sites it is a strict-mode
    ; diagnostic's MESSAGE NUMBER (`… Modules are automatically in strict mode`
    ; against the plain one), i.e. a well-formed wrong answer.
    is_external_module: bool

    ; `b.file.CommonJSModuleIndicator`, and it is the NODE rather than a bool
    ; because setCommonJSModuleIndicator's own guard is a null test on it —
    ; "the first `require` or `module.exports` wins" is the whole content of the
    ; function, and a bool says it just as well; what decides is that the field
    ; must be settable from the arms and readable by three of them. Kept as a node
    ; for the reference's shape, since it costs the same word.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference's OTHER indicator is a node too, and this port answers it as
    ; a bool (§3.5q) — the difference is that the parser computes that one before
    ; the bind and nothing during a bind can move it, while this one is written
    ; mid-walk and every reader after the write sees a different answer.
    common_js_module_indicator: ref[AstNode]?

    ; b.seenThisKeyword — set by the `this` keyword and the `this` type, read by
    ; bind_container's interface branch and turned into NodeFlagsContainsThis
    ; there. See the arms in bind_worker for why only that branch is ported.
    seen_this_keyword: bool

    ; `b.file.IsDeclarationFile`, and it is back — the paragraph above says a field
    ; nothing reads is only DECLARED (§3.5be), and slice 29 is the reader:
    ; bindNamespaceExportDeclaration reports
    ; Global_module_exports_may_only_appear_in_declaration_files on exactly this
    ; question. It is answered from the file NAME, the way the parser answers it,
    ; and cached for the same reason is_external_module is.
    is_declaration_file: bool

    ; `b.file.GlobalExports`, made on FIRST USE the way a symbol's members and
    ; exports tables are (ast.GetSymbolTable(&b.file.GlobalExports)). One writer,
    ; bindNamespaceExportDeclaration's default arm.
    ;
    ; ★ It is NOT in the symbols dump, and that is a statement about the ORACLE
    ; rather than an omission here: tests/oracle/symbols.go numbers the tables it
    ; reaches through the walk (locals) and through a symbol (members, exports),
    ; and a SourceFile's GlobalExports is neither. What IS compared is everything
    ; the table's existence changes — the symbol declared into it appears on the
    ; declaration's own `n` line and in the `s` section, its parent is the file
    ; symbol, and a second `export as namespace X` MERGES into it, which is
    ; visible as one symbol with two declarations. So the table has to be real; it
    ; just has no id.
    global_exports: ref[SymbolTable]?

    ; `len(b.file.Diagnostics()) == 0`, which guards the contextual-identifier
    ; check. The parse is over, so the count cannot move during the bind.
    file_has_no_parse_errors: bool

    ; `b.file.FileName()`, and it is here for ONE question: an external module's
    ; symbol is named after it (bind_source_file_as_external_module). It arrives
    ; as a parameter rather than off the node for the same reason `source` does —
    ; the reference's SourceFile carries both, this port's carries neither — and
    ; it must be the name the CALLER normalized, because upstream's
    ; `opts.FileName` is `GetNormalizedAbsolutePath(arg, cwd)` and the symbol name
    ; is that string character for character (tspath.scaly's absolute-path
    ; section). Kept as a RANGE, since a Symbol's name is one.
    file_name: Slice[char]

    ; The four container registers. `last_container` is the head of the
    ; reference's container CHAIN (addToContainerChain) — the chain's links are
    ; NextContainer fields this port does not carry, since the only reader is the
    ; checker's getLocalNameOfContainer, so what is kept is the register the chain
    ; is threaded through and nothing else.
    container: ref[AstNode]?
    this_container: ref[AstNode]?
    block_scope_container: ref[AstNode]?
    last_container: ref[AstNode]?

    ; `b.symbolCount`, and it is not len(symbols): it counts every newSymbol call,
    ; including the ones a conflict throws away, so it is a statement about the
    ; path taken. The dump compares it, which makes it a check on the path.
    symbol_count: int

    ; ★ `ast.nextSymbolId`, slice 41 — the counter behind Symbol.id. Upstream's is
    ; a package-global atomic; this one is a field, i.e. per file, which is the
    ; divergence Symbol.id's comment argues and §3.5cl records. It is NOT
    ; symbol_count: that one counts every newSymbol call, this one is drawn from
    ; only when somebody ASKS for an id, and in the whole reference binder the only
    ; asker is the private-name minter.
    next_symbol_id: int

    ; `b.classifiableNames`, a SET of names. A SymbolTable stands in for it — the
    ; entries carry a null symbol and only the key matters — because a second
    ; container type would buy nothing and the dump reads it as a sorted name list.
    classifiable_names: ref[SymbolTable]

    ; `SetBindDiagnostics`: a THIRD list, which is why the yardstick has a section
    ; of its own for it. Appended RAW (add_direct), because the reference's
    ; addDiagnostic is a plain append with neither the parser's dedup nor its
    ; has_error flag.
    bind_diagnostics: ref[DiagnosticList]

    ; `b.notConstEnumOnlyModules`, slice 31. Written by two sites and read by
    ; one, all three inside the const-enum-only question: a module symbol that has
    ; once been seen with a Function, Class or RegularEnum declaration can never
    ; go back to being const-enum-only, and the set is what remembers it ACROSS
    ; the declarations of one merged symbol.
    not_const_enum_only_modules: ref[SymbolLink]?

    ; `b.inAssignmentPattern`, slice 33. The one flow field this port carries, and
    ; it is here because it is not a flow field at all: its only reader,
    ; bindDestructuringAssignmentFlow, uses it to decide which ORDER to bind a
    ; destructuring assignment's operands in, and the order decides the sequence
    ; symbols are created in. Slice 32 measured it as free precisely because that
    ; reader was unported (§3.5be — a flag whose only reader is unported is
    ; DECLARED, not ported); this slice is the reader, so the field is real.
    in_assignment_pattern: bool

    ; `b.expandoAssignments`, slice 38. The DEFERRED list: every `f.a = x` and
    ; `Object.defineProperty(f, "a", …)` the walk met, each with the container
    ; registers of its own moment, bound after the walk is over.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHY THE REFERENCE DEFERS AT ALL — and it is not the usual "the indicator
    ; must be known first" reason the JSTypeAlias deferral has. An expando
    ; declaration attaches to the symbol of the thing it extends, and that symbol
    ; must already carry its VALUE DECLARATION, so a single pass would look the
    ; host up before its declaration had been bound and find nothing.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE OBVIOUS EXAMPLE OF THAT IS NOT THE ONE THAT NEEDS THIS PASS,
    ; which is worth writing at the line because it cost a control. `f.a = 1` above
    ; `function f() {}` is bound correctly with NO deferral:
    ; bind_each_statement_functions_first walks a statement list twice and binds
    ; every function declaration on the first walk, so the hoisting of a FUNCTION is
    ; handled one mechanism earlier. What this pass is for is every OTHER host —
    ; `g.b = 2` above `const g = () => {}`, where the arrow's symbol does not exist
    ; until the declaration is reached in source order. The battery's c4 was UNGATED
    ; against the function shape and is RED 1 against the const one.
    expando_assignments: ref[Array[ExpandoAssignmentInfo]]

    ; ── the FLOW registers (slice 92) ────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ ELEVEN FIELDS AND ONE OF THEM IS NOT NULLABLE, which is the whole shape
    ; of the reference's design in one line: `unreachableFlow` is a SENTINEL, made
    ; once per binder, and every test in this file that asks *is this code
    ; reachable* is an identity comparison against it. A nullable spelling would
    ; make `currentFlow == b.unreachableFlow` answer FALSE for a null current flow
    ; rather than reporting, and the difference is a graph that quietly keeps
    ; building past a `return`.
    ;
    ; ★★ `current_flow` IS nullable and it must be: bindContainer leaves it null
    ; for a container that is not a control-flow container, and `bind`'s
    ; QualifiedName and access arms test it for null before writing the slot —
    ; upstream's `b.currentFlow != nil` at two of the five slot writes and not at
    ; the other three, which is a distinction this port keeps rather than
    ; normalises.
    current_flow: ref[FlowNode]?
    current_break_target: ref[FlowNode]?
    current_continue_target: ref[FlowNode]?
    current_return_target: ref[FlowNode]?
    current_exception_target: ref[FlowNode]?
    current_true_target: ref[FlowNode]?
    current_false_target: ref[FlowNode]?
    pre_switch_case_flow: ref[FlowNode]?
    active_label_list: ref[ActiveLabel]?

    ; `b.hasExplicitReturn` — read once, by bindContainer, to decide whether a
    ; function whose end is reachable also has a `return` somewhere. Its product
    ; is NodeFlagsHasExplicitReturn, which the checker reads for TS2378 and TS2366.
    has_explicit_return: bool

    ; `b.hasFlowEffects` — set by every construct that can divert control (a
    ; return, a throw, a break, a mutation, a call) and read by the three places
    ; that decide whether a junction label was worth making: a logical expression,
    ; a conditional expression and an optional chain each keep the flow they
    ; STARTED with when nothing under them had an effect.
    has_flow_effects: bool

    ; The unreachable sentinel. One per binder, and compared by IDENTITY.
    unreachable_flow: ref[FlowNode]

    ; SourceFile.ECMALineMap, lazily. The reference computes the line map on
    ; first ask and keeps it on the SourceFile; this port keeps it here, because
    ; the Binder is what holds the scanner the text lives in. Null until the
    ; first caller — today error_range_for_arrow_function, which is the only
    ; place in the checker's whole span machinery that asks for a LINE.
    ecma_line_map: ref[Array[int]]?

    ; The unported report, in the parser's shape: the first construct this slice
    ; does not implement stops the bind and names itself.
    unported_pos: int
    unported_tag: Slice[char]
    unported_detail: int
)
{
    ; ── entry ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The reference's BindSourceFile/bindSourceFile pair, minus the pool and the
    ; once-guard: a Binder here is one object on the caller's page, used once.
    function bind_source_file(host: ref[Page], file: ref[AstNode], source: String, file_name: String, is_external_module: bool) returns ref[Binder]
    {
        let b host.allocate(sizeof Binder, alignof Binder) as pointer[Binder]
        set b.host: host
        set b.file: file
        let sc &Scanner^host(source)
        ; ★ Slice 31: the scanner is told the file's LANGUAGE VARIANT, because
        ; since error_on_first_token it SCANS. GetScannerForSourceFile takes the
        ; variant off the SourceFile; here the file name is what carries it, the
        ; same question `is_declaration_file` is answered from and through the
        ; parser's own clamped table (a `.js` unit is NOT the JSX variant even
        ; though upstream's raw table says so, and a `.json` unit IS).
        sc.set_language_variant_jsx(Parser.script_kind_is_jsx_variant(file_name))
        set b.trivia: sc
        set b.is_external_module: is_external_module
        set b.common_js_module_indicator: null
        set b.seen_this_keyword: false
        set b.is_declaration_file: is_declaration_file_name(file_name)
        set b.global_exports: null
        set b.file_name: Slice[char](file_name.get_length() as int, file_name.get_buffer() as pointer[char])
        set b.file_has_no_parse_errors: AstNode.diagnostic_count(file) = 0
        set b.container: null
        set b.this_container: null
        set b.block_scope_container: null
        set b.last_container: null
        set b.symbol_count: 0
        set b.next_symbol_id: 0
        set b.classifiable_names: SymbolTable.create(host)
        set b.not_const_enum_only_modules: null
        set b.in_assignment_pattern: false
        let ea &Array[ExpandoAssignmentInfo]^host()
        set b.expando_assignments: ea
        let dl &DiagnosticList^host(host)
        set b.bind_diagnostics: dl
        set b.ecma_line_map: null
        set b.unported_pos: 0 - 1
        set b.unported_tag.data: null
        set b.unported_tag.length: 0
        set b.unported_detail: 0

        ; The flow registers. `unreachableFlow` is made first, because
        ; `current_flow` starts AS it — upstream's `b.currentFlow = nil` at this
        ; point is a Go zero value that bindContainer overwrites before anything
        ; reads it, and this port starts from the sentinel so that no reader can
        ; meet a null current flow at all outside the two arms that test for one.
        let uf FlowNode.alloc(host)
        set uf.flags: FlowFlagsUnreachable
        set uf.node: null
        set uf.antecedent: null
        set uf.antecedents: null
        set b.unreachable_flow: uf
        set b.current_flow: null
        set b.current_break_target: null
        set b.current_continue_target: null
        set b.current_return_target: null
        set b.current_exception_target: null
        set b.current_true_target: null
        set b.current_false_target: null
        set b.pre_switch_case_flow: null
        set b.active_label_list: null
        set b.has_explicit_return: false
        set b.has_flow_effects: false

        b.bind(file)

        ; bindSourceFile's second line, and slice 38 is what puts it there:
        ; `b.bindDeferredExpandoAssignments()` runs after the whole walk.
        ;
        ; ★ It is guarded on the unported flag while the reference has no such
        ; thing, and the guard is about the BINDER's state rather than about the
        ; report: a report stops the walk mid-container, so the three registers are
        ; not restored past it and every entry in the list was captured before it.
        ; The unit's whole dump is the marker either way, so running the pass could
        ; only change which marker.
        if b.is_unported() = false
            b.bind_deferred_expando_assignments()
        b
    }

    ; ── the unported report ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; FIRST one wins and the bind stops walking: the same rule the parser's
    ; `unported` follows, for the same reason — a tree bound past a construct
    ; nobody implemented has meaningless tables after it, so reporting the second
    ; one would be reporting about a state the reference never reaches.
    procedure record_unported(this, tag: Slice[char], detail: int)
    {
        if unported_pos < 0
        {
            set unported_pos: 0
            set unported_tag: tag
            set unported_detail: detail
        }
    }

    function is_unported(this) returns bool
        unported_pos >= 0

    function unported_at(b: ref[Binder]) returns int
        b.unported_pos

    ; ast.GetAssignmentTarget, whole. Its `for` is a CLIMB and not a search: the
    ; four kinds in the middle arm hand the question to the parent, the rest answer.
    function get_assignment_target(node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        var n: ref[AstNode]? node
        while n <> null
        {
            let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(n as ref[AstNode])
            if parent = null
                return null
            let pn parent as ref[AstNode]
            let pk AstNode.kind_of(pn)
            if pk = KindBinaryExpression
            {
                if Parser.is_assignment_operator(AstNode.binary_operator_kind(pn))
                {
                    if AstNode.binary_left_of(pn) = n
                        return pn
                }
                return null
            }
            if pk = KindPrefixUnaryExpression
            {
                let op AstNode.prefix_unary_operator_kind(pn)
                if op = KindPlusPlusToken
                    return pn
                if op = KindMinusMinusToken
                    return pn
                return null
            }
            ; ★★ THE POSTFIX ARM'S TEST IS CONSTANT AND THE PORT SAYS SO RATHER
            ; THAN ASKING. `PostfixUnaryExpression` shares WrappedTypeData with a
            ; dozen one-child nodes (§3.5bb) and stores no operator kind — and it
            ; needs none: the grammar admits exactly `++` and `--` after an
            ; operand, so the reference's `op == ++ || op == --` is true for every
            ; node that can reach this line. ★If a third postfix operator is ever
            ; added, the record grows a slot and this is the line that has to ask.
            if pk = KindPostfixUnaryExpression
                return pn
            var for_in_or_of false
            if pk = KindForInStatement
                set for_in_or_of: true
            if pk = KindForOfStatement
                set for_in_or_of: true
            if for_in_or_of
            {
                if AstNode.initializer_of(pn) = n
                    return pn
                return null
            }
            var climbs false
            if pk = KindParenthesizedExpression
                set climbs: true
            if pk = KindArrayLiteralExpression
                set climbs: true
            if pk = KindSpreadElement
                set climbs: true
            if pk = KindNonNullExpression
                set climbs: true
            if climbs
            {
                set n: pn
                continue
            }
            if pk = KindSpreadAssignment
            {
                set n: AstNode.parent_node_of(pn)
                continue
            }
            if pk = KindShorthandPropertyAssignment
            {
                if AstNode.name_of(pn) <> n
                    return null
                set n: AstNode.parent_node_of(pn)
                continue
            }
            if pk = KindPropertyAssignment
            {
                if AstNode.name_of(pn) = n
                    return null
                set n: AstNode.parent_node_of(pn)
                continue
            }
            return null
        }
        null
    }

    ; ── the FLOW GRAPH's constructors (slice 92) ─────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; internal/binder/binder.go's newFlowNode block, whole. Every one of these is
    ; three lines upstream and three here; what is worth reading is which of them
    ; SHORT-CIRCUIT, because those are the arms that decide how big the graph is.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference allocates out of a per-binder ARENA (`b.flowNodeArena.New()`)
    ; and this port allocates out of the binder's page, which is the same lifetime
    ; said in this language: a flow node dies with the bind that made it, and the
    ; checker that reads one is inside that region.

    procedure new_flow_node(this, flags: int) returns ref[FlowNode]
    {
        let f FlowNode.alloc(host)
        set f.flags: flags
        set f.node: null
        set f.antecedent: null
        set f.antecedents: null
        f
    }

    procedure new_flow_node_ex(this, flags: int, n: ref[AstNode]?, antecedent: ref[FlowNode]?) returns ref[FlowNode]
    {
        let f this.new_flow_node(flags)
        set f.node: n
        set f.antecedent: antecedent
        f
    }

    procedure create_loop_label(this) returns ref[FlowNode]
        this.new_flow_node(FlowFlagsLoopLabel)

    procedure create_branch_label(this) returns ref[FlowNode]
        this.new_flow_node(FlowFlagsBranchLabel)

    ; createReduceLabel. The synthetic node carries the pair the checker needs to
    ; substitute a label's antecedents while it walks past this point.
    procedure create_reduce_label(this, target: ref[FlowNode]?, antecedents: ref[FlowList]?, antecedent: ref[FlowNode]?) returns ref[FlowNode]
        this.new_flow_node_ex(FlowFlagsReduceLabel, this.new_synthetic_node(NodeData.FlowReduceLabel(FlowReduceLabelData(target, antecedents))), antecedent)

    ; The two synthetic nodes' shared constructor. It is the parser's new_node
    ; without the parser: KindUnknown, an empty range, and every slot cleared —
    ; and the clearing is not optional, because `allocate` hands back raw region
    ; memory and the flow dump reads three of those slots on every node it meets.
    ;
    ; ★ These nodes are never REACHED by a walk — nothing parses them, ForEachChild
    ; has no arm — so their range and their parent are inert by construction. The
    ; reference's own NewFlowSwitchClauseData leaves both at zero too.
    procedure new_synthetic_node(this, data: NodeData) returns ref[AstNode]
    {
        let d AstNode.alloc(host)
        set d.kind: KindUnknown
        set d.flags: NodeFlagsNone
        set d.pos: 0
        set d.end: 0
        set d.data: data
        set d.parent: null
        set d.symbol: null
        set d.local_symbol: null
        set d.locals: null
        set d.flow_node: null
        set d.end_flow_node: null
        set d.return_flow_node: null
        set d.fallthrough_flow_node: null
        d
    }

    ; ★★★ createFlowCondition IS FOUR EARLY ANSWERS AND ONE CONSTRUCTION, and the
    ; four are what keep a flow graph from having a node per `if`. In order:
    ; unreachable stays unreachable; a MISSING condition is the true branch for a
    ; true-condition and unreachable for a false one (that is `for (;;)`); a
    ; literal `true` on the false side and a literal `false` on the true side are
    ; unreachable, EXCEPT under an optional chain root or a `??`; and a condition
    ; that narrows NOTHING is not worth a node at all.
    procedure create_flow_condition(this, flags: int, antecedent: ref[FlowNode]?, expression: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[FlowNode]?
    {
        if antecedent = null
            return antecedent
        let a antecedent as ref[FlowNode]
        if (a.flags & FlowFlagsUnreachable) <> 0
            return antecedent
        if expression = null
        {
            if (flags & FlowFlagsTrueCondition) <> 0
                return antecedent
            return unreachable_flow
        }
        let e expression as ref[AstNode]
        let ek AstNode.kind_of(e)
        var literal_kills false
        if ek = KindTrueKeyword
        {
            if (flags & FlowFlagsFalseCondition) <> 0
                set literal_kills: true
        }
        if ek = KindFalseKeyword
        {
            if (flags & FlowFlagsTrueCondition) <> 0
                set literal_kills: true
        }
        if literal_kills
        {
            if Binder.is_expression_of_optional_chain_root(e) = false
            {
                if Binder.is_nullish_coalesce(AstNode.parent_node_of(e)) = false
                    return unreachable_flow
            }
        }
        if Binder.is_narrowing_expression(e) = false
            return antecedent
        Binder.set_flow_node_referenced(a)
        this.new_flow_node_ex(flags, e, antecedent)
    }

    procedure create_flow_mutation(this, flags: int, antecedent: ref[FlowNode]?, n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[FlowNode]?
    {
        if antecedent <> null
            Binder.set_flow_node_referenced(antecedent as ref[FlowNode])
        set has_flow_effects: true
        let result this.new_flow_node_ex(flags, n, antecedent)
        if current_exception_target <> null
            this.add_antecedent(current_exception_target as ref[FlowNode], result)
        result
    }

    procedure create_flow_switch_clause(this, antecedent: ref[FlowNode]?, switch_statement: ref[AstNode]?, clause_start: int, clause_end: int) returns ref[FlowNode]?
    {
        if antecedent <> null
            Binder.set_flow_node_referenced(antecedent as ref[FlowNode])
        this.new_flow_node_ex(FlowFlagsSwitchClause, this.new_synthetic_node(NodeData.FlowSwitchClause(FlowSwitchClauseData(switch_statement, clause_start, clause_end))), antecedent)
    }

    procedure create_flow_call(this, antecedent: ref[FlowNode]?, n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[FlowNode]?
    {
        if antecedent <> null
            Binder.set_flow_node_referenced(antecedent as ref[FlowNode])
        set has_flow_effects: true
        this.new_flow_node_ex(FlowFlagsCall, n, antecedent)
    }

    procedure new_flow_list(this, head: ref[FlowNode]?, tail: ref[FlowList]?) returns ref[FlowList]
    {
        let l FlowList.alloc(host)
        set l.flow: head
        set l.next: tail
        l
    }

    ; combineFlowLists — the head list is rebuilt onto the tail, so the TAIL is
    ; shared between the two results. That sharing is what makes a `try`/`finally`
    ; affordable and it is why a FlowList is a list rather than an Array here.
    procedure combine_flow_lists(this, head: ref[FlowList]?, tail: ref[FlowList]?) returns ref[FlowList]?
    {
        if head = null
            return tail
        let h head as ref[FlowList]
        this.new_flow_list(h.flow, this.combine_flow_lists(h.next, tail))
    }

    ; setFlowNodeReferenced. First reference sets Referenced, every one after that
    ; sets Shared — so the two bits together are a saturating counter, and the
    ; checker reads Shared to decide what is worth memoising.
    function set_flow_node_referenced(f: ref[FlowNode])
    {
        if (f.flags & FlowFlagsReferenced) = 0
            set f.flags: f.flags | FlowFlagsReferenced
        else
            set f.flags: f.flags | FlowFlagsShared
    }

    ; ★★ addAntecedent DEDUPLICATES and finishFlowLabel COLLAPSES, and between
    ; them they are why the graph has no label per junction: a label with one
    ; antecedent IS that antecedent, and a label with none is unreachable.
    ; ★The dedup is a linear scan of the list, upstream's own shape.
    ; ★ `label` is a RESERVED WORD in Scaly (it introduces a loop label), so the
    ; reference's parameter name cannot be transcribed. `flow_label` throughout.
    procedure add_antecedent(this, flow_label: ref[FlowNode], antecedent: ref[FlowNode]?)
    {
        if antecedent = null
            return
        let a antecedent as ref[FlowNode]
        if (a.flags & FlowFlagsUnreachable) <> 0
            return
        var last: ref[FlowList]? null
        var list: ref[FlowList]? flow_label.antecedents
        while list <> null
        {
            let l list as ref[FlowList]
            if l.flow = antecedent
                return
            set last: l
            set list: l.next
        }
        if last = null
            set flow_label.antecedents: this.new_flow_list(antecedent, null)
        else
        {
            let lt last as ref[FlowList]
            set lt.next: this.new_flow_list(antecedent, null)
        }
        Binder.set_flow_node_referenced(a)
    }

    procedure finish_flow_label(this, flow_label: ref[FlowNode]) returns ref[FlowNode]?
    {
        if flow_label.antecedents = null
            return unreachable_flow
        let a flow_label.antecedents as ref[FlowList]
        if a.next = null
            return a.flow
        flow_label
    }

    ; ★★★ THE TEST THE WHOLE FILE RESTS ON, AND IT IS THE FLAG RATHER THAN THE
    ; IDENTITY. The reference writes it both ways — `f.Flags&Unreachable != 0` in
    ; createFlowCondition and addAntecedent, `b.currentFlow == b.unreachableFlow`
    ; in bindCaseBlock and bindInitializer — and the two coincide because the bit
    ; has exactly ONE carrier: `newFlowNode(FlowFlagsUnreachable)` is called once
    ; per binder and no other construction ors the bit in. Ported as the flag test
    ; at every site, since a `ref[FlowNode]?` compared against a non-optional
    ; `ref[FlowNode]` is the one spelling this language will not resolve.
    ; ★If a second carrier is ever introduced upstream, the four identity sites
    ; are the ones that change meaning, and they are named in this comment.
    function flow_is_unreachable(f: ref[FlowNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if f = null
            return false
        let n f as ref[FlowNode]
        (n.flags & FlowFlagsUnreachable) <> 0
    }

    function unported_tag_at(b: ref[Binder]) returns Slice[char]
        b.unported_tag

    function unported_detail_at(b: ref[Binder]) returns int
        b.unported_detail

    ; ── what the dumper reads ───────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; Statics over a pointer, for the reason ast.scaly's accessors are statics: a
    ; program root calling a cross-package free function with a concept-pointer
    ; parameter fails resolution, while a concept static resolves.

    function symbol_count_of(b: ref[Binder]) returns int
        b.symbol_count

    function classifiable_count(b: ref[Binder]) returns int
        SymbolTable.length(b.classifiable_names)

    function classifiable_name_at(b: ref[Binder], i: int) returns Slice[char]
        SymbolTable.entry_name_at(b.classifiable_names, i)

    function classifiable_name_len_at(b: ref[Binder], i: int) returns int
        SymbolTable.entry_name_len_at(b.classifiable_names, i)

    function diagnostic_count(b: ref[Binder]) returns int
        b.bind_diagnostics.length()

    function diagnostic_at(b: ref[Binder], i: int, out_pos: ref[int], out_end: ref[int], out_code: ref[int]) returns bool
        b.bind_diagnostics.at(i, out_pos, out_end, out_code)

    ; ── diagnostics ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; errorOnNode → createDiagnosticForNode → **GetErrorRangeForNode**, and the
    ; last step is not a detail: it is what makes a diagnostic point at the
    ; CONSTRUCT rather than at the whitespace before it.
    ;
    ; ★★★ Leaving it out was worth 31 units of corpus stage 2, in FOUR apparent
    ; classes (TS1212, TS1100, TS2300, TS1359) that were ONE cause — every start
    ; position was one byte early, because a node's `pos` is its FULL start,
    ; leading trivia included, and the reference skips that trivia before
    ; reporting. Four signatures, one fix: a histogram groups by symptom, and the
    ; count of causes is smaller (§3.5bi's lesson, one phase later).
    procedure error_on_node(this, n: ref[AstNode]?, code: int)
    {
        if n = null
            return
        var start 0
        var stop 0
        if this.error_range_for_node(n, &start, &stop) = false
            return
        bind_diagnostics.add_direct(start, stop, code)
    }

    ; b.errorOnFirstToken, slice 31. NOT error_on_node with another span: it does
    ; not ask GetErrorRangeForNode at all, so no declaration name and no
    ; trivia-skip is involved — the span is the FIRST TOKEN at the node's own pos,
    ; which for a `declare module` is the first modifier and for a string name is
    ; the literal.
    procedure error_on_first_token(this, n: ref[AstNode]?, code: int)
    {
        var start 0
        var stop 0
        this.range_of_token_at_position(AstNode.pos_of(n as ref[AstNode]), &start, &stop)
        bind_diagnostics.add_direct(start, stop, code)
    }

    ; scanner.GetRangeOfTokenAtPosition: a scanner at `pos`, ONE scan, and the
    ; token's own [TokenStart, TokenEnd) — the start is past the trivia because
    ; the scan skipped it, so this is not skip_trivia_at with an end bolted on.
    ;
    ; ★★ The reference builds a FRESH scanner (GetScannerForSourceFile) and this
    ; uses the binder's, which is the same answer for three reasons, each of which
    ; would otherwise be a way to differ: the shared one carries no diagnostic sink
    ; (so a scan reports nothing anywhere), skip_trivia_at does not touch scanner
    ; state (so no caller of it can be disturbed by a scan, and no scan can inherit
    ; a position from one), and the variant is set from the file name at
    ; construction — a fresh scanner would be told the same thing.
    ;
    ; ★ The scanner may also report UNPORTED, and it is deliberately not consulted:
    ; the position handed to it is a node's own start, which the PARSER already
    ; scanned successfully with this same variant, so the token here has been
    ; answered once before.
    procedure range_of_token_at_position(this, pos: int, out_start: ref[int], out_end: ref[int])
    {
        trivia.reset_pos(pos)
        trivia.scan()
        set out_start: trivia.token_start()
        set out_end: trivia.token_end()
    }

    ; GetErrorRangeForNode's CONSTRUCTOR arm — the scan LOOP the deferral note on
    ; error_range_needs_rescan named, and slice 83 is the reader it predicted.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS THE SECOND INSTALMENT OF THAT LIST AND THE NOTE PRICED IT EXACTLY:
    ; *Constructor needs a scan LOOP with the token kind read back, to the
    ; `constructor` keyword or a string literal or the end of file.* What makes the
    ; loop necessary rather than one scan is the MODIFIERS: `private constructor()`
    ; starts at `private`, and the span the reference reports is the keyword's.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE START IS THE FIRST TOKEN'S AND THE END IS THE LAST ONE'S, which is the
    ; reference's own shape and not a range over the keyword alone — `scanner
    ; .TokenStart()` is read BEFORE the loop and `TokenEnd()` after it, so a
    ; constructor with modifiers reports from the first modifier to the end of
    ; `constructor`. Transcribed rather than tidied.
    ;
    ; ★ EndOfFile is the loop's guard against a malformed tree and is the
    ; reference's own third terminator; without it a node whose text holds neither
    ; terminator would scan past the buffer.
    procedure range_of_constructor_keyword(this, pos: int, out_start: ref[int], out_end: ref[int])
    {
        trivia.reset_pos(pos)
        trivia.scan()
        set out_start: trivia.token_start()
        var k trivia.token()
        while k <> KindConstructorKeyword
        {
            if k = KindStringLiteral
                set k: KindConstructorKeyword
            else
            {
                if k = KindEndOfFile
                    set k: KindConstructorKeyword
                else
                {
                    trivia.scan()
                    set k: trivia.token()
                }
            }
        }
        set out_end: trivia.token_end()
    }

    ; scanner.ScanTokenAtPosition — the same one scan as above, answering the
    ; token's KIND rather than its range. Slice 61's checkTypeReferenceNode asks it
    ; whether the gap between a type name and its type arguments is a DOT, which is
    ; the JSDoc spelling `Foo.<T>`; the reference builds a fresh scanner through
    ; GetScannerForSourceFile and reads `s.token`, which is what this returns.
    ;
    ; ★ The three reasons the shared scanner is the same answer are
    ; range_of_token_at_position's, unchanged: no diagnostic sink, skip_trivia_at
    ; touches no scanner state, and the variant is fixed at construction. ★And the
    ; position handed here is NOT one the parser has already scanned — it is the
    ; type name's END — so unlike the range function above this one can meet a
    ; token the parse never asked for. It is still a token in a file that parsed,
    ; and the caller compares the answer against ONE kind, so anything else
    ; (including a scanner report) leaves the branch untaken.
    function token_kind_at_position(this, pos: int) returns int
    {
        trivia.reset_pos(pos)
        trivia.scan()
        trivia.token()
    }

    ; scanner.SkipTrivia(file.Text(), pos), exposed for the CHECKER (slice 51).
    ; Two of its grammar checks report at a span that ends past a bracketed list's
    ; own end — `SkipTrivia(text, list.End()) + len(">")` — and the scanner that
    ; can answer it is the one this object already owns, for the three reasons
    ; range_of_token_at_position gives above: no diagnostic sink, no scanner state
    ; touched, and the variant fixed at construction.
    ;
    ; ★ It takes no text, unlike the reference: this scanner was constructed over
    ; the file's buffer, so a caller passing one would be handing back what the
    ; callee already has and would be a second thing to keep in step.
    function skip_trivia_at(this, pos: int) returns int
        trivia.skip_trivia_at(pos)

    ; scanner.GetTextOfNodeFromSourceText's slice — the raw source between two
    ; offsets. ★★★ ITS FIRST READER IS SLICE 95's PRINTER, and it is the first
    ; place in the CHECKER that reads source text at all: `DeclarationNameToString`
    ; answers the text as WRITTEN, which for an identifier carrying a unicode
    ; escape is not the same string as its scanned `Text()`. Handing back a Slice
    ; rather than the buffer keeps the length with the pointer, which is the whole
    ; reason a caller cannot walk off the end of it.
    function source_slice(this, start: int, stop: int) returns Slice[char]
    {
        if stop <= start
            return Slice[char]()
        trivia.buffer.subslice(start as size_t, stop as size_t)
    }

    ; ── the ECMA line map, and its two lookups ──
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE SPAN MACHINERY ASKED FOR A LINE FOR THE FIRST TIME IN THIS SLICE.
    ; Everything above works in OFFSETS — pos, end, skip_trivia, a re-scanned
    ; token — and getErrorRangeForArrowFunction is the one arm of
    ; GetErrorRangeForNode that does not: *the arrow function spans multiple
    ; lines, make the error span be the first line, inclusive*. That question
    ; cannot be answered from two offsets, so the map is a new machine rather
    ; than a call, which is why the arm outlived the other seven.
    ;
    ; ★ Lazily, as upstream: a unit whose diagnostics never touch an arrow
    ; function never walks its text a second time, and the lib file — bound
    ; through a Binder of its own — never does at all.
    procedure ecma_line_starts(this) returns ref[Array[int]]
    {
        if ecma_line_map = null
            set ecma_line_map: trivia.compute_ecma_line_starts(host)
        ecma_line_map as ref[Array[int]]
    }

    ; scanner.ComputeLineOfPosition, whole. ★Its answer for a position BEFORE the
    ; first line start is `low - 1` = -1, and that is the reference's own answer
    ; rather than a guard to add: the array's first entry is 0, so only a
    ; negative position can reach it and no caller here has one.
    function compute_line_of_position(starts: ref[Array[int]], pos: int) returns int
    {
        var low 0
        var high (starts.get_length() as int) - 1
        while low <= high
        {
            let middle low + ((high - low) >> 1)
            let value: int starts.at(middle as size_t)
            if value < pos
                set low: middle + 1
            else
            {
                if value > pos
                    set high: middle - 1
                else
                    return middle
            }
        }
        low - 1
    }

    procedure ecma_line_of_position(this, pos: int) returns int
        Binder.compute_line_of_position(this.ecma_line_starts(), pos)

    ; scanner.GetECMAEndLinePosition — the position of the character BEFORE the
    ; line's terminator, or before the end of the text. ★The `size == 0` half of
    ; the reference's test is the end of the string and is written as the bound
    ; check: DecodeRuneInString of an empty remainder answers size 0, and this
    ; port's utf8_decode_at answers -1 there, which is not a line break.
    procedure ecma_end_line_position(this, line: int) returns int
    {
        let starts this.ecma_line_starts()
        var pos: int starts.at(line as size_t)
        repeat
        {
            if pos >= trivia.end
                return pos - 1
            if Scanner.is_line_break_rune(trivia.utf8_decode_at(pos))
                return pos - 1
            set pos: pos + trivia.utf8_size_at(pos)
        }
    }

    ; scanner.getErrorRangeForArrowFunction — the FIFTH of the eight deferred
    ; arms to come due, and the first that needed a machine rather than a call.
    ; Its reader is the same one the other three had: an arrow function is what
    ; a great many checker reports point at, and until this arm existed every one
    ; of them was DROPPED with `error-range 220` in its place — 75 units of the
    ; stage-2 corpus, the frontier's third row.
    procedure error_range_for_arrow_function(this, n: ref[AstNode], out_start: ref[int], out_end: ref[int])
    {
        let pos this.skip_trivia_at(AstNode.pos_of(n))
        let body AstNode.body_of(n)
        if body <> null
        {
            let bd body as ref[AstNode]
            if AstNode.kind_of(bd) = KindBlock
            {
                let start_line this.ecma_line_of_position(AstNode.pos_of(bd))
                let end_line this.ecma_line_of_position(AstNode.end_of(bd))
                if start_line < end_line
                {
                    ; "The arrow function spans multiple lines, make the error
                    ; span be the first line, inclusive."
                    set out_start: pos
                    set out_end: this.ecma_end_line_position(start_line) + 1
                    return
                }
            }
        }
        set out_start: pos
        set out_end: AstNode.end_of(n)
    }

    ; scanner.GetErrorRangeForNode. Answers false when it meets the ONE arm that
    ; is still deferred — see error_range_needs_rescan, which carries the reason
    ; and the enumeration behind it. ★The list started at eight: slice 64 took the
    ; two clause arms, slice 83 the constructor, slice 84 the return/yield pair and
    ; this slice the SourceFile and the ARROW FUNCTION, and the count is written
    ; out rather than inherited because the prose said *eight* for two slices after
    ; it was six. It is now ONE: SatisfiesExpression.
    function error_range_for_node(this, n: ref[AstNode], out_start: ref[int], out_end: ref[int]) returns bool
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)

        ; The ONE arm still deferred. Reported rather than approximated: an
        ; approximated SPAN is a wrong answer that looks like a right one, which
        ; is the class §3.5bw records for is_in_top_level_context.
        ;
        ; ★★★ AND SLICE 176 WROTE IT AGAIN AND TOOK IT BACK OUT AGAIN — the same
        ; two units, the same two symptoms, the same cause outside this file.
        ; See error_range_needs_rescan's note: it is a MEASUREMENT and not an
        ; aged premise, and re-doing it cost a stage-1 round. §3.5hf.
        if Binder.error_range_needs_rescan(k)
        {
            this.record_unported("error-range-rescan", k)
            return false
        }

        ; `case ast.KindSourceFile:` — the FOURTH of the eight arms to come due,
        ; and it is three lines because the machine it needs (skip_trivia_at and
        ; the re-scan) has been here since slice 31. ★The empty-file answer is
        ; the reference's own and is not a degenerate case to fold away: a file
        ; that is nothing but trivia reports at (0, 0), where the re-scan would
        ; report the END-OF-FILE token's own zero-width span at the text end.
        if k = KindSourceFile
        {
            let sf_pos this.skip_trivia_at(0)
            if sf_pos = trivia.end
            {
                set out_start: 0
                set out_end: 0
                return true
            }
            this.range_of_token_at_position(sf_pos, out_start, out_end)
            return true
        }

        ; `case ast.KindArrowFunction:` — the FIFTH, and the one that needed the
        ; line map; see error_range_for_arrow_function above.
        if k = KindArrowFunction
        {
            this.error_range_for_arrow_function(n, out_start, out_end)
            return true
        }

        ; `case KindCaseClause, KindDefaultClause:` — the first of the eight
        ; deferred arms to come due; see error_range_for_clause below.
        if k = KindCaseClause
            return this.error_range_for_clause(n, out_start, out_end)
        if k = KindDefaultClause
            return this.error_range_for_clause(n, out_start, out_end)

        ; `case ast.KindConstructor:` — the second of the eight deferred arms to
        ; come due. Slice 83's checkConstructorDeclaration hands a ConstructorDeclaration
        ; to error_on_node for TS2390 (*constructor implementation is missing*), and
        ; before this arm existed that report was DROPPED and a stop recorded in its
        ; place: `error-range 177`.
        ;
        ; ★ The Reparsed guard is the reference's and it is the same pair
        ; error_range_uses_declaration_name carries for a function and a method — a
        ; synthesized node has no `constructor` keyword in the text to scan to, so it
        ; reports at itself.
        if k = KindConstructor
        {
            if (AstNode.flags_of(n) & NodeFlagsReparsed) = 0
            {
                this.range_of_constructor_keyword(AstNode.pos_of(n), out_start, out_end)
                return true
            }
        }

        ; `case ast.KindReturnStatement, ast.KindYieldExpression:` — the THIRD of the
        ; eight deferred arms to come due, and its reader arrived exactly where the
        ; deferral note said it would: *the grammar checks report on exactly these
        ; constructs (an arrow function, **a return statement**, a case clause, a
        ; constructor)*. Slice 84's checkReturnStatement hands a ReturnStatement to
        ; error_on_node for TS2408 (*setters cannot return a value*), and before this
        ; arm existed that report was DROPPED with `error-range 254` in its place —
        ; slice 83's TS2390 finding, arriving a second time at the same seam.
        ;
        ; ★★ THE TWO KINDS ARE ONE ROW BECAUSE THEY ARE ONE `case` LABEL, and the
        ; YIELD half has no reader here (checkYieldExpression is the expression
        ; dimension). Splitting them would be a claim the reference does not make;
        ; the arm is two lines and serves both, which is slice 83's reading of the
        ; JSDoc pair.
        ;
        ; ★ The SkipTrivia in front is the reference's and is transcribed rather
        ; than folded away: range_of_token_at_position resets and SCANS, so it skips
        ; the same trivia on its own. Keeping the call makes the two lines the
        ; reference's two lines and costs one pass over whitespace already scanned.
        if k = KindReturnStatement
        {
            this.range_of_token_at_position(this.skip_trivia_at(AstNode.pos_of(n)), out_start, out_end)
            return true
        }
        if k = KindYieldExpression
        {
            this.range_of_token_at_position(this.skip_trivia_at(AstNode.pos_of(n)), out_start, out_end)
            return true
        }

        ; The declaration kinds report at their NAME. The list is the reference's,
        ; and the two Reparsed guards with it: a synthesized function or method has
        ; no name to point at, so it reports at itself.
        var error_node: ref[AstNode]? n
        if Binder.error_range_uses_declaration_name(k)
        {
            var reparsed false
            if (AstNode.flags_of(n) & NodeFlagsReparsed) <> 0
            {
                if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
                    set reparsed: true
                if k = KindMethodDeclaration
                    set reparsed: true
            }
            ; ★★★ THE GUARD THAT STOOD HERE WAS A LATCH, NOT A TEST, AND IT WAS
            ; READ AT CHECK TIME. `is_unported()` asks *has this BINDER stopped at
            ; all* — the deliberate abort of the bind walk — and get_name_of_declaration
            ; has not reported since slice 124 took its last stop out, so the line
            ; could only ever answer about somebody else's report. It is not
            ; harmless there: error_range_for_node's ONE caller is the CHECKER, long
            ; after the bind artifact is written, and the binder still records during
            ; the check (get_module_instance_state, and this very function's
            ; error-range-rescan arm eight lines up). So the FIRST such record turned
            ; every later span in the unit into `error-range <kind>` — a named
            ; VariableDeclaration answering false is the tell, because its arm has no
            ; other way to fail. ★★★§ 3.5gd's shape one file over: slice 148 read the
            ; binder's predicate as a deliberate abort and it is one for the bind
            ; WALK; the two routines the checker calls into are not the bind walk.
            if reparsed = false
                set error_node: this.get_name_of_declaration(n)
        }
        ; ★ The reference asks GetNameOfDeclaration for the list above and plain
        ; `node.Name()` for a CLASS EXPRESSION, one line apart. That is not an
        ; inconsistency to tidy: GetNameOfDeclaration would answer a class
        ; expression's ASSIGNED name (the variable it is bound to), which is in a
        ; different statement, and an error span may not leave the node.
        if k = KindClassExpression
            set error_node: AstNode.name_of(n)

        ; ★★★ THE FALLBACK, AND SLICE 45's WHOLE FINDING IS THAT IT WAS ONE CALL
        ; AWAY FOR FOURTEEN SLICES. `GetRangeOfTokenAtPosition(file, node.Pos())`
        ; — the reference's own comment: *"if we don't have a better node, then
        ; just set the error on the first token of construct"*. The marker that
        ; stood here called it "the re-scan again" and reported
        ; `error-range-no-name`, while `range_of_token_at_position` — the same
        ; re-scan, on the same shared scanner — had been ported in slice 31 for
        ; error_on_first_token and stood in this same concept, sixty lines up. A
        ; marker names a blocker at the moment it is written and nothing re-reads
        ; it when the blocker goes (§3.5ci, the same class one slice-family
        ; earlier), so the deferral outlived its reason.
        if error_node = null
        {
            this.range_of_token_at_position(AstNode.pos_of(n), out_start, out_end)
            return true
        }

        var pos AstNode.pos_of(error_node)
        ; A MISSING node is zero-width, so there is no trivia in front of it to
        ; skip and skipping would move the span into the following token; JSX TEXT
        ; is trivia-free by construction.
        var skip true
        if Binder.node_is_missing(error_node)
            set skip: false
        if AstNode.kind_of(error_node) = KindJsxText
            set skip: false
        if skip
            set pos: trivia.skip_trivia_at(pos)
        set out_start: pos
        set out_end: AstNode.end_of(error_node)
        true
    }

    ; ★★★ THE DEFERRED ARMS — EIGHT UNTIL SLICE 64 AND SIX NOW — AND SLICE 45
    ; REPLACED THEIR STATED REASON WITH THE REAL ONE. The old wording said each of them "needs to RE-SCAN the source
    ; at a position (GetRangeOfTokenAtPosition), which is a scanner call rather
    ; than a range computation" — and that call has been ported since slice 31:
    ; range_of_token_at_position, above error_range_for_node. What actually defers
    ; them is
    ; that NO BINDER DIAGNOSTIC CAN CARRY ONE OF THESE KINDS, so porting the arms
    ; would add code no instrument in this repository can reach.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE ARGUMENT IS AN ENUMERATION, NOT A SAMPLE. error_range_for_node has
    ; exactly one caller — error_on_node — and error_on_node has fourteen call
    ; sites. ELEVEN pass a node of a FIXED kind: four a NamespaceExportDeclaration,
    ; five an Identifier, one a PrivateIdentifier, one a `delete` operand the arm
    ; has already tested for being an identifier. None of those is on this list.
    ; The other THREE pass a SYMBOL'S DECLARATION, which narrows the question to:
    ; can one of these eight kinds be the declaration a duplicate report names?
    ; Five of them — CaseClause, DefaultClause, ReturnStatement, YieldExpression,
    ; SatisfiesExpression — are not declarations at all and get no symbol. The
    ; other three do get one and still cannot arrive: a SourceFile and an
    ; ArrowFunction are bound through bindAnonymousDeclaration, which makes a
    ; symbol with no TABLE and therefore no collision to report, and a Constructor
    ; is declared with excludes NONE, so a second constructor MERGES instead of
    ; colliding. ★The corpus agrees, and that is the cheap half of the evidence
    ; rather than the argument: 0 in 17 807 units.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE READER THAT EARNS THEM IS THE CHECKER. Upstream
    ; GetErrorRangeForNode has two callers: the binder's diagnostic factory and
    ; checker/utilities.go's NewDiagnosticForNode, which every one of the checker's
    ; ~44 createDiagnosticForNode sites goes through — and the grammar checks
    ; report on exactly these constructs (an arrow function, a return statement, a
    ; case clause, a constructor). So this list is the checker dimension's first
    ; bill and not a scanner gap. It joins the other parked readers:
    ; ImportTypeData.is_type_of, the commonJS half of the name resolver, and the
    ; bigint token value.
    ;
    ; ★ WHAT EACH ARM NEEDED BESIDES THE RE-SCAN, kept because the LAST one is
    ; still owed and the record of what the others cost is what dates this note.
    ; SourceFile, ReturnStatement and YieldExpression needed nothing else —
    ; skip_trivia_at plus the re-scan, both ported since slice 31, plus the
    ; SourceFile arm's own empty-file test. CaseClause and DefaultClause needed the
    ; clause's statement list, for `statements[0].Pos()` as the end. Constructor
    ; needed a scan LOOP with the token kind read back. ArrowFunction was named
    ; here as *the only one asking for machinery this port does not have at all:
    ; the ECMA LINE MAP* — this slice built it (ecma_line_starts above), and it is
    ; the smallest machine in the file: one pass over the text, a binary search and
    ; a forward scan to the line's terminator.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT SATISFIES-EXPRESSION STILL OWES IS NOT THE RE-SCAN EITHER. Its
    ; unreparsed half is two lines (`SkipTrivia(text, expression.End())` and the
    ; re-scan). The REPARSED half needs findOriginatingJSDocSatisfiesTag: a climb
    ; through the parents for the first one carrying HasJSDoc, then that node's
    ; EAGER JSDoc list, then the `@satisfies` tag whose type expression is the very
    ; node this expression's type was reparsed from — an identity test on a Loc,
    ; which this port compares as (pos, end). It is one function and it is left
    ; here rather than guessed at, because reporting at the EXPRESSION where the
    ; reference reports at the TAG is a wrong span that looks like a right one.
    ; ★★★ THE ONE KIND LEFT IS KindSatisfiesExpression, AND SLICE 161 WROTE ITS ARM,
    ; MEASURED IT AND TOOK IT BACK OUT — which is worth more than the arm was.
    ; Upstream's shape is two lines (skip trivia from the checked expression's END
    ; and report on the `satisfies` keyword) plus findOriginatingJSDocSatisfiesTag,
    ; which fires only for a REPARSED target type — a `@satisfies {T}` tag in a
    ; JavaScript file — and reports on the tag name instead. That half needs the
    ; PARSER's JSDoc side table (`jsdoc_infos`; see the Checker's `p` field) and a
    ; Binder has no parser, which covers the row's two `.js` units.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE OTHER TWO ARE THE FINDING. With the arm in, `noTypeToStringRecursion`
    ; and `noTypeToStringStackOverflow` — `const f = () => 42 satisfies typeof f;`
    ; — went from UNPORTED to FAIL: TS1360's span came out right, and the reference's
    ; OTHER report, TS7023 at the `f` binding, was missing while the arrow's type
    ; read `() => number` where the reference says `() => any`. The cause is not in
    ; this file at all. Upstream's reportRelationError computes
    ; getTypeNamesForErrorDisplay(source, target) UNCONDITIONALLY as its first line,
    ; and printing the TARGET re-enters getReturnTypeOfSignature for the signature
    ; whose return type is being resolved — so popTypeResolution fails and TS7023 is
    ; that failure. This port formats no diagnostic ARGUMENTS (§3.20, §3.24) and asks
    ; type_to_string only when BOTH types carry a symbol, which a number literal does
    ; not. **A diagnostic argument is not inert: computing it is what closes a
    ; resolution cycle.** The fixture names are the proof — the reference's own test
    ; for this issue is called *noTypeToStringRecursion*.
    function error_range_needs_rescan(k: int) returns bool
        k = KindSatisfiesExpression

    ; ★★★ SLICE 64 PAYS THE FIRST INSTALMENT OF THAT LIST, AND THE NOTE ABOVE NAMED
    ; BOTH THE READER AND THE PRICE. It wrote *the reader that earns them is the
    ; CHECKER … the grammar checks report on exactly these constructs (an arrow
    ; function, a return statement, a case clause, a constructor)*, and
    ; checkSwitchStatement's TS1113 (*a default clause cannot appear more than once*)
    ; is a grammarErrorOnNode on a DefaultClause — so the arm came due exactly where
    ; the deferral said it would, two slices later. ★★It is worth reading beside
    ; slice 64's OTHER deferral finding, which is its mirror image: there a marker
    ; named a blocker that had already been lifted by an unrelated slice, and here a
    ; marker named a reader that arrived on schedule. **A deferral's reason is a
    ; claim with a date on it, and it can expire in either direction.**
    ;
    ; ★★ WHAT IT NEEDED WAS THE CLAUSE'S STATEMENT LIST — the note says so at its
    ; own line — and that is the OTHER half of this slice: statements_of had three of
    ; its four arms until now (ast.scaly), so the price of this arm was paid by a
    ; widening made for checkSwitchStatement's walk. ★And what it did NOT need is
    ; what the deferral's first, wrong wording claimed: there is no re-scan here at
    ; all, only skip_trivia_at.
    ;
    ; ★ CaseClause arrives WITH DefaultClause because they are ONE `case` label
    ; upstream, not because a caller asks for it — §3.5bk's rule, which is the same
    ; reason for_await_modifier_of has a ForIn arm nothing reaches.
    ;
    ; ★ The end is the first STATEMENT's Pos and not its trivia-skipped start, so a
    ; comment between `default:` and the first statement is INSIDE the span. That is
    ; the reference's arithmetic and it is the one asymmetry in this arm: the start
    ; skips trivia and the end does not.
    function error_range_for_clause(this, n: ref[AstNode], out_start: ref[int], out_end: ref[int]) returns bool
    {
        set out_start: this.skip_trivia_at(AstNode.pos_of(n))
        set out_end: AstNode.end_of(n)
        let statements AstNode.statements_of(n)
        if AstNode.list_count(statements) <> 0
            set out_end: AstNode.pos_of((AstNode.child_in_list(statements, 0)) as ref[AstNode])
        true
    }

    function error_range_uses_declaration_name(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindVariableDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindBindingElement
            return true
        if k = KindClassDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindEnumDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindEnumMember
            return true
        if k = KindFunctionExpression
            return true
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindPropertyDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindPropertySignature
            return true
        if k = KindNamespaceImport
            return true
        false
    }

    ; ast.NodeIsMissing: a zero-width node that is not the end-of-file token.
    function node_is_missing(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return true
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindEndOfFile
            return false
        if AstNode.pos_of(n) < 0
            return false
        AstNode.pos_of(n) = AstNode.end_of(n)
    }

    ; ── internal symbol names ───────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The reference's InternalSymbolName* constants: the byte 0xFE — deliberately
    ; not valid UTF-8, so it cannot collide with identifier text — followed by an
    ; ASCII word. Built on the host rather than declared, because Scaly has no
    ; spelling for a string constant carrying that byte and because the name only
    ; has to compare EQUAL by bytes, never by pointer.
    ; ★★★ FOUR OF THE REFERENCE'S CONSTANTS DO NOT CARRY THE PREFIX, and slice 28
    ; found it the way §3.5v predicts these are found — by a construct starting to
    ; produce one. `InternalSymbolNameExportEquals` is the bare string `export=`,
    ; and so are `default`, `this` and `module.exports`; every other one is
    ; `InternalSymbolNamePrefix + word`. Upstream says why in a comment on the
    ; `default` line: those names are "technically not wholly internal" — they are
    ; names a program can actually resolve — so they must NOT be unspellable. Slice
    ; 27 routed all of them through the prefixing helper, which was invisible while
    ; nothing produced an `export=` or a `default` symbol: the JSON arm produces
    ; the first (six units) and an unnamed default export the second, and both
    ; turned up as `%FEexport=` against `export=` in the dump.
    ;
    ; So there are two helpers, and the choice at a call site is a transcription of
    ; which constant the reference names — not a judgement about what the name is
    ; for.
    function internal_name_unprefixed(host: ref[Page], word: Slice[char]) returns Slice[char]
    {
        let n word.length as int
        let buf host.allocate((n + 1) as size_t, 1) as pointer[char]
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            set *(buf + i): word[i]
            set i: i + 1
        }
        Slice[char](n, buf)
    }

    function internal_name(host: ref[Page], word: Slice[char]) returns Slice[char]
    {
        let n word.length as int
        let buf host.allocate(((n + 1) as size_t), 1) as pointer[char]
        set *buf: 0xFE as char
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            set *(buf + 1 + i): word[i]
            set i: i + 1
        }
        Slice[char](n + 1, buf)
    }

    ; ── the walk ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; bind(), and the arm set is this slice's. The kinds the reference handles and
    ; this slice does not report themselves rather than falling through silently:
    ; falling through would bind their CHILDREN into the right tables and leave the
    ; declaration itself out, which is a dump that differs by one line and a defect
    ; that looks like a missing symbol rather than like a missing arm.
    procedure bind(this, n: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        if n = null
            return
        if this.is_unported()
            return

        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)

        ; ── the five FLOW-SLOT writes of `bind`'s own switch (slice 92) ─────
        ;
        ; ★★★ THE ORDER MATTERS AND IT IS THE REFERENCE'S: the slot is written
        ; HERE, before bindChildren runs, so a node in unreachable code is given
        ; the sentinel and then has it taken back out by bindChildren's unreachable
        ; arm. Writing it after the walk would be a different set of nodes.
        ;
        ; ★★★ TWO OF THE FIVE TEST `current_flow` FOR NULL AND THREE DO NOT, which
        ; is upstream's own asymmetry rather than a normalisation. It is
        ; unobservable at this pin — the source file is a control-flow container,
        ; so nothing is bound with a null current flow — and it is kept because a
        ; guard that cannot fire is cheaper to keep than to re-derive.
        if k = KindIdentifier
        {
            set n.flow_node: current_flow
            this.check_contextual_identifier(n)
        }
        if k = KindQualifiedName
        {
            if current_flow <> null
            {
                if Binder.is_part_of_type_query(n)
                    set n.flow_node: current_flow
            }
        }
        if k = KindMetaProperty
            set n.flow_node: current_flow
        ; ★ A property or element access gets a slot only when it is a NARROWABLE
        ; reference — `a.b` yes, `f().b` no — because the slot exists for the
        ; checker to look a narrowing up by, and there is nothing to narrow on an
        ; expression that has no stable identity.
        if k = KindPropertyAccessExpression
        {
            if current_flow <> null
            {
                if Binder.is_narrowable_reference(n)
                    set n.flow_node: current_flow
            }
        }
        if k = KindElementAccessExpression
        {
            if current_flow <> null
            {
                if Binder.is_narrowable_reference(n)
                    set n.flow_node: current_flow
            }
        }
        if k = KindSourceFile
            this.bind_source_file_if_external_module()
        if k = KindTypeParameter
            this.bind_type_parameter(n)
        if k = KindParameter
            this.bind_parameter(n)
        if k = KindVariableDeclaration
            this.bind_variable_declaration_or_binding_element(n)
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            this.bind_function_declaration(n)

        ; ── slice 29: the import/export ALIAS family ────────────────────────
        ;
        ; The reference collapses four kinds into ONE arm, and the collapse is the
        ; statement: an `import x = require("m")`, an `import * as ns`, an
        ; `import { a }` and an `export { a }` are all one alias declaration, and
        ; which TABLE it lands in is decided further down — declareModuleMember's
        ; alias branch, ported in slice 28 and unreachable until this line existed.
        if k = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
            this.declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(n, SymbolFlagsAlias, SymbolFlagsAliasExcludes)
        if k = KindNamespaceImport
            this.declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(n, SymbolFlagsAlias, SymbolFlagsAliasExcludes)
        if k = KindImportSpecifier
            this.declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(n, SymbolFlagsAlias, SymbolFlagsAliasExcludes)
        if k = KindExportSpecifier
            this.declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(n, SymbolFlagsAlias, SymbolFlagsAliasExcludes)
        if k = KindNamespaceExportDeclaration
            this.bind_namespace_export_declaration(n)
        if k = KindImportClause
            this.bind_import_clause(n)
        if k = KindExportDeclaration
            this.bind_export_declaration(n)
        if k = KindExportAssignment
            this.bind_export_assignment(n)

        ; ── slice 30: the CLASS-LIKE family and its MEMBERS ─────────────────
        ;
        ; ★★ The container half and the member half are ONE slice on purpose.
        ; declareSymbolAndAddToSymbolTable's class arm (declareClassMember) and its
        ; members arm can be reached only from a MEMBER's own bind arm, so shipping
        ; the containers alone would park two branches as unreachable — the debt
        ; slice 29 had to pay off for slice 28's c13. The slice that lifts a
        ; condition owns every claim resting on it.
        if k = KindClassDeclaration
            this.bind_class_like_declaration(n)
        if k = KindClassExpression
            this.bind_class_like_declaration(n)
        if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            this.bind_block_scoped_declaration(n, SymbolFlagsInterface, SymbolFlagsInterfaceExcludes)
        if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
            this.bind_block_scoped_declaration(n, SymbolFlagsTypeAlias, SymbolFlagsTypeAliasExcludes)

        ; ★ A JavaScript `@typedef` is bound HERE only when it is NOT at the top
        ; level of the file: a top-level one is DEFERRED to the tail of
        ; bindContainer so that the CommonJS module indicators are known first.
        ; Both halves land in this slice, because either one alone is a typedef
        ; that silently declares nothing.
        if k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
        {
            if AstNode.kind_of(block_scope_container) <> KindSourceFile
                this.bind_block_scoped_declaration(n, SymbolFlagsTypeAlias, SymbolFlagsTypeAliasExcludes)
        }

        ; ── slice 31: the ENUM ──────────────────────────────────────────────
        ;
        ; ★★★ THE ENUM SHIPS WITH THE MODULE, and the argument is slice 30's
        ; verbatim: the module's own const-enum-only bit is set from a state that
        ; only a `const enum` can produce, and a namespace holding a const enum
        ; STOPS at the enum's bind arm — so shipping the module alone would leave
        ; the whole const-enum-only tail, and the reset in
        ; add_declaration_to_symbol, parked as unreachable. The slice that lifts a
        ; condition owns every claim resting on it.
        if k = KindEnumDeclaration
            this.bind_enum_declaration(n)
        if k = KindEnumMember
            this.bind_property_or_method_or_accessor(n, SymbolFlagsEnumMember, SymbolFlagsEnumMemberExcludes)

        ; ── slice 31: the MODULE DECLARATION ────────────────────────────────
        ;
        ; The last of the three container families the reference's switch names
        ; (the source file, the class-like nodes, the module), and the first arm
        ; whose ANSWER depends on the shape of the body below it.
        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
            this.bind_module_declaration(n)

        ; The two anonymous TYPE containers, which share one flag and one name —
        ; and the name is the binder's own rather than the node's.
        if k = KindTypeLiteral
            this.bind_type_literal(n)
        if k = KindMappedType
            this.bind_type_literal(n)

        ; The members. Every one of these routes through
        ; declareSymbolAndAddToSymbolTable, so WHICH table it lands in is the
        ; container's question and not theirs — which is exactly why the arms and
        ; the tables have to ship together.
        if k = KindPropertyDeclaration
            this.bind_property_worker(n)
        if k = KindPropertySignature
            this.bind_property_worker(n)
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            this.bind_method(n)
        if k = KindMethodSignature
            this.bind_method(n)
        if k = KindConstructor
            this.declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(n, SymbolFlagsConstructor, SymbolFlagsNone)
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            this.bind_property_or_method_or_accessor(n, SymbolFlagsGetAccessor, SymbolFlagsGetAccessorExcludes)
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            this.bind_property_or_method_or_accessor(n, SymbolFlagsSetAccessor, SymbolFlagsSetAccessorExcludes)
        if k = KindCallSignature
            this.declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(n, SymbolFlagsSignature, SymbolFlagsNone)
        if k = KindConstructSignature
            this.declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(n, SymbolFlagsSignature, SymbolFlagsNone)
        if k = KindIndexSignature
            this.declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(n, SymbolFlagsSignature, SymbolFlagsNone)

        ; ── slice 32: the OBJECT LITERAL family ─────────────────────────────
        ;
        ; The THIRD of the four containers whose members declare into a `members`
        ; table — the arm that makes the object-literal case of
        ; container_declares_into_members reachable, and with it the two
        ; object-literal predicates the class slice had to write blind
        ; (is_object_literal_method's excludes, and the extra bit
        ; method_container_flags gives a method of an object literal). The fourth,
        ; KindJsxAttributes, is still unported and is the only one left.
        ;
        ; ★ The property and the SHORTHAND property are one case label upstream,
        ; and the pair it passes is BARE. Both nodes carry a postfix-token slot —
        ; the parser fills it for a stray `{ a?: 1 }`, which it accepts and then
        ; reports — and the reference still passes Property without asking
        ; getOptionalSymbolFlagForNode, where a class member's bindPropertyWorker
        ; computes one. Transcribed rather than reasoned about, and c15 breaks it.
        if k = KindObjectLiteralExpression
            this.bind_object_literal_expression(n)
        if k = KindPropertyAssignment
            this.bind_property_or_method_or_accessor(n, SymbolFlagsProperty, SymbolFlagsPropertyExcludes)
        if k = KindShorthandPropertyAssignment
            this.bind_property_or_method_or_accessor(n, SymbolFlagsProperty, SymbolFlagsPropertyExcludes)

        ; ── slice 34: the FUNCTION-LIKE heads ───────────────────────────────
        ;
        ; The two heads of both histograms after slice 33, and they are two arms
        ; rather than one: a function TYPE declares a signature symbol inside an
        ; anonymous type literal, a function EXPRESSION declares one anonymous
        ; symbol named after itself or after nothing.
        ;
        ; ★★★ WHAT THEY UNLOCK IS MOSTLY NOT THEIR OWN CONTENT. Until this slice
        ; every one of the four kinds reported and RETURNED, so the whole subtree
        ; below it — a body's statements, a signature's parameters and type
        ; parameters, every declaration in either — was never walked. That is why
        ; these four kinds sit at the head of a histogram whose entries are counted
        ; per unit: an arrow function anywhere in a file parked the file.
        if k = KindFunctionType
            this.bind_function_or_constructor_type(n)
        if k = KindConstructorType
            this.bind_function_or_constructor_type(n)
        if k = KindFunctionExpression
            this.bind_function_expression(n)
        if k = KindArrowFunction
            this.bind_function_expression(n)

        ; ── slice 35: the LAST fifteen bind arms ────────────────────────────
        ;
        ; ★★★ THE SWITCH IS COMPLETE WITH THIS SLICE, and the fifteen kinds ship
        ; together because kind_has_unported_bind_arm is a LIST and not a
        ; construct: while one entry stands, every unit containing that kind
        ; reports and RETURNS, so the units the other fourteen would unlock stay
        ; parked behind it. Slice 34 measured exactly that from the other side —
        ; its four kinds gained 1 513 units of the shared corpus off 49 lines of
        ; code, and none of the gain was the arms' own content.
        ;
        ; They fall into three groups that have nothing in common except size.

        ; (a) The JSX ATTRIBUTES OBJECT — the FOURTH container declaring into a
        ; `members` table, and the one slice 32 named as the only one left.
        ; `<div a="1" b:c={2}/>` is an object literal whose properties are its
        ; attributes and whose name is the binder's own.
        if k = KindJsxAttributes
            this.bind_jsx_attributes(n)
        if k = KindJsxAttribute
            this.bind_jsx_attribute(n, SymbolFlagsProperty, SymbolFlagsPropertyExcludes)

        ; (b) The STRICT-MODE GRAMMAR CHECKS — six arms, one call each, and every
        ; one of them a DIAGNOSTIC rather than a symbol, so the dump's B section is
        ; the whole of what gates them.
        ;
        ; ★★ NONE of them tests strict mode, and that reads like six missing gates
        ; rather than one. It is the reference: tsgo's binder has no strict-mode
        ; state at all (`grep -c inStrictMode internal/binder/binder.go` = 0, where
        ; Strada's binder branched on it in every one of these arms), so `with (x)
        ; {}` in a plain script reports TS1101 and `delete x` reports TS1102
        ; unconditionally. check_strict_mode_eval_or_arguments already carries this
        ; finding at its own line, measured on a fixture; the six arms below are
        ; the rest of the same fact. Fidelity is to the reference.
        if k = KindCatchClause
            this.check_strict_mode_catch_clause(n)
        if k = KindDeleteExpression
            this.check_strict_mode_delete_expression(n)
        if k = KindPostfixUnaryExpression
            this.check_strict_mode_postfix_unary_expression(n)
        if k = KindPrefixUnaryExpression
            this.check_strict_mode_prefix_unary_expression(n)
        if k = KindWithStatement
            this.check_strict_mode_with_statement(n)
        if k = KindLabeledStatement
            this.check_strict_mode_labeled_statement(n)
        if k = KindPrivateIdentifier
            this.check_private_identifier(n)

        ; b.seenThisKeyword = true, from the two arms that set it. The `this`
        ; KEYWORD shares its case with `super` upstream and only the first of the
        ; two assigns; the `this` TYPE is a case of its own further down the
        ; switch. Both are here because both feed one field.
        if k = KindThisKeyword
        {
            set seen_this_keyword: true
            set n.flow_node: current_flow
        }
        ; ★ `super` shares the case label with `this` upstream and only the flow
        ; slot is common to both — the seenThisKeyword assignment is inside a kind
        ; test there, which is why it is not written twice here either.
        if k = KindSuperKeyword
            set n.flow_node: current_flow
        if k = KindThisType
            set seen_this_keyword: true

        ; (c) The FLOW-ONLY arms. Six kinds whose entire content was one assignment
        ; into a flow graph this port did not build, so each was ported as NOTHING
        ; and what it bought was the walk below it.
        ;
        ; ★★★ SLICE 92 CLOSES THIS LIST: the graph exists, so all six assignments
        ; are written and they stand at the head of this switch, where the
        ; reference puts them. What is left below is the two arms that always had
        ; a second statement — the `this` keyword's seenThisKeyword and the binding
        ; element's declaration — and the paragraphs are kept because the deferral
        ; they record is the shape §3.5be is about, and it EXPIRED on schedule.
        ;
        ; ★★★ ONE of them WAS in this list and is now ported — `b.seenThisKeyword
        ; = true`, on the `this` keyword and on the `this` TYPE — and the reason it
        ; moved is that the paragraph which put it here has EXPIRED. It said the
        ; field would be "a stored field with no reader, i.e. only DECLARED
        ; (§3.5be)", and named a flags column on the binder dump as the reader that
        ; would make it observable. That is still the reader a BINDER yardstick
        ; would need, and it is still blocked on the flow graph — but slice 66 gave
        ; the flag a reader one phase LATER: isThislessInterface asks
        ; `declaration.Flags & NodeFlagsContainsThis` of every interface
        ; declaration, and slice 67 puts getBaseTypes behind that answer. So the
        ; observation route is the CHECKER's unported tag, not a binder dump
        ; column, and the deferral's own condition never mentioned which dump.
        ;
        ; ★★★ WHAT IT COST TO LEAVE IT: for two slices, EVERY interface that
        ; mentions `this` was treated as thisless — so it was denied its `this`
        ; type and its ObjectFlagsReference, silently, because both sides of that
        ; comparison are unported and no dump carries the difference. §3.5dl even
        ; wrote the coincidence down from the other side, saying its `this` fixture
        ; and its plain one "answer the SAME tag on purpose … the difference
        ; between them is a `this` type nothing can print". The truer statement is
        ; that it was a `this` type this port never MADE, and a flag with no writer
        ; and a flag whose difference cannot be printed are not the same finding.
        ;
        ; ★★ ONLY THE INTERFACE HALF OF THE WRITE IS PORTED, and the scoping is
        ; exactly the reader's. bindContainer writes ContainsThis in two branches:
        ; the control-flow one (whose reader is
        ; isContextSensitiveFunctionLikeDeclaration, unported, and which resets and
        ; propagates the flag around a function body) and the INTERFACE one. An
        ; interface's members are signatures, so no function BODY can occur inside
        ; one — which is why the missing control-flow branch cannot make the
        ; interface's answer wrong, and the interface branch's own save/reset/
        ; restore is what keeps a `this` from an earlier statement out of it.
        ;
        ; ★ The BINDING ELEMENT is the one arm here with a second statement, and it
        ; is a call this port has had since slice 27 —
        ; bindVariableDeclarationOrBindingElement, the variable declaration's own
        ; arm. Its FlowNode line was the dropped half and slice 92 restores it.
        if k = KindBindingElement
        {
            set n.flow_node: current_flow
            this.bind_variable_declaration_or_binding_element(n)
        }

        ; ── slice 33: the two heads, and their `bind` arms ──────────────────
        ;
        ; ★★★ BOTH ARMS OPEN WITH THE SAME QUESTION — GetAssignmentDeclarationKind
        ; — and answering it is what lets this slice claim anything at all. The
        ; five kinds it can answer are the JavaScript ASSIGNMENT DECLARATIONS
        ; (`module.exports = x`, `exports.a = x`, `this.a = x`, `f.a = x`,
        ; `Object.defineProperty(f, "a", …)`), and each of them DECLARES A SYMBOL.
        ;
        ; ★★★ AND THE `Property` KIND IS NOT JAVASCRIPT-ONLY. `function f() {}`
        ; followed by `f.x = 1` in a plain .ts file is an expando declaration
        ; upstream — the arm sits outside GetAssignmentDeclarationKind's
        ; IsInJSFile test — which is why this classifier had to be ported here
        ; rather than left to the slice that binds it: a port that assumed "JS
        ; declarations happen in JS files" would answer None for every TS unit and
        ; claim agreement on exactly the ones where the reference makes an extra
        ; symbol.
        ;
        ; ★★★ SLICE 37 SPLIT THE FAMILY IN TWO ALONG THE MACHINERY, not along the
        ; switch: the three kinds below share setCommonJSModuleIndicator and
        ; declare into the FILE symbol's exports, while ThisProperty needs
        ; declareSymbolEx's two extra flags and the this-class table, and
        ; Property/ObjectDefinePropertyValue need the DEFERRED expando pass with
        ; its entity lookup. Both remainders keep reporting, by kind, so the
        ; histogram still prices them.
        if k = KindBinaryExpression
        {
            let adk Parser.get_assignment_declaration_kind(n)
            if adk = JSDeclarationKindModuleExports
                this.bind_module_exports_assignment(n)
            if adk = JSDeclarationKindExportsProperty
                this.bind_exports_or_object_define_property(n)
            if adk = JSDeclarationKindProperty
                this.bind_expando_property_assignment(n)
            if adk = JSDeclarationKindThisProperty
                this.bind_this_property_assignment(n)

            ; checkStrictModeBinaryExpression runs AFTER the switch and outside
            ; it, which is the reference's shape and is now the whole of what this
            ; line says: until slice 42 the ThisProperty arm above reported instead
            ; of binding, and the sentence here had to explain why the diagnostic
            ; was discarded anyway. All four kinds bind now, so there is nothing
            ; left to except.
            this.check_strict_mode_binary_expression(n)
        }

        ; The call arm's own switch has only the two ObjectDefineProperty kinds,
        ; and then bindCallExpression — which exists for ONE question: a
        ; `require(...)` call in a JavaScript file makes the file a CommonJS
        ; module, i.e. gives it a module symbol and binds it as an external one.
        if k = KindCallExpression
        {
            let adk Parser.get_assignment_declaration_kind(n)
            if adk = JSDeclarationKindObjectDefinePropertyValue
                this.bind_expando_property_assignment(n)
            if adk = JSDeclarationKindObjectDefinePropertyExports
                this.bind_exports_or_object_define_property(n)
            if Parser.is_in_js_file(n)
                this.bind_call_expression(n)
        }

        ; Every other arm of the reference's switch, by kind — and as of slice 35
        ; there is no other arm, so this reports nothing. The call stays where it
        ; is because the LIST is the inventory of the reference's switch and a pin
        ; bump re-reads it (the note on the function has the argument).
        if Binder.kind_has_unported_bind_arm(k)
        {
            this.record_unported("bind", k)
            return
        }

        if this.is_unported()
            return

        ; The recursion. `node.Kind > KindLastToken` is the reference's own
        ; optimisation and it is not cosmetic here either: a token has no children
        ; and asking GetContainerFlags of one would answer None anyway.
        if k > KindLastToken
        {
            let container_flags Binder.get_container_flags(n)
            if container_flags = ContainerFlagsNone
                this.bind_children(n)
            else
                this.bind_container(n, container_flags)
        }
    }

    ; The kinds the reference's `bind` switch has an arm for and this port has
    ; not. Read off binder.go's switch, arm for arm.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS EMPTY AS OF SLICE 35, and it stays as a function rather than being
    ; deleted with its call site because it is the INVENTORY: it is where the
    ; reference's switch was read arm for arm, and §2's step 3 — every new
    ; deviation after a pin bump gets a reason or a fix — is a re-reading of
    ; exactly this list against binder.go. A future arm upstream is invisible to
    ; every measurement this suite has (a kind nobody binds simply produces
    ; nothing), so the list is the only place it can be noticed.
    ;
    ; ★ What is NOT on it and never was: an arm whose remainder reports from
    ; INSIDE itself. The binary expression and the call (slice 33) reported by
    ; assignment-declaration kind while the rest of each arm was already ported —
    ; and as of slice 42 neither reports at all, the family being complete; the
    ; four function-like kinds (slice 34) and slice 35's fifteen left WHOLE.
    function kind_has_unported_bind_arm(k: int) returns bool
        false

    ; bindChildren. The flow half is not ported (see the header), so what is left
    ; is the TRAVERSAL, and every arm below either reproduces the reference's order
    ; or argues that ForEachChild's order is the same.
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 33 SPLIT IT IN TWO, and the split is what carries the one flow
    ; FIELD this port has. The reference opens bindChildren by saving
    ; `b.inAssignmentPattern` and clearing it — "most nodes aren't valid in an
    ; assignment pattern" — and closes by restoring it; the arms that ARE valid
    ; there (the four assignment-pattern operands, and a destructuring assignment)
    ; put the saved value back before descending. Written inline that would be a
    ; save at the top and a restore in front of every one of the dozen `return`s,
    ; so the save/restore lives here and the switch is a procedure of its own that
    ; takes the saved value.
    ;
    ; ★★ ONE DIFFERENCE FROM THE REFERENCE FALLS OUT OF THAT AND IS PROVABLY
    ; INVISIBLE: upstream's destructuring arm RETURNS after its call, leaving the
    ; field as bindDestructuringAssignmentFlow left it, where this restores it.
    ; The only reader of the field is that same function, and it is reached only
    ; through the arm below, which ASSIGNS the field immediately before calling —
    ; so no reader can observe a value that was not written in its own arm. (The
    ; reference's own trailing restore rests on exactly the same argument, which
    ; is why the early return is not a third behaviour.)
    procedure bind_children(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let save_in_assignment_pattern in_assignment_pattern
        set in_assignment_pattern: false

        ; ── the UNREACHABLE arm (slice 92) ──────────────────────────────────
        ;
        ; ★★★ THIS IS WHERE TS7027 COMES FROM, and it is the whole of the binder's
        ; contribution to it: past a `return`, a `throw`, a `break` or a
        ; `continue`, the current flow is the unreachable sentinel, and every node
        ; walked from here is MARKED rather than wired. The checker reports the
        ; mark; the binder only sets it.
        ;
        ; ★★★ AND THE FLOW-SLOT CLEAR IS NOT DEAD CODE, which is worth the line
        ; because it looks like it is: `bind`'s own kind switch runs BEFORE this
        ; and writes `current_flow` into an Identifier's slot unconditionally — so
        ; an identifier in unreachable code arrives here holding the sentinel, and
        ; this is what takes it back out. Leave it out and the flow dump prints a
        ; slot the reference does not.
        if Binder.flow_is_unreachable(current_flow)
        {
            set n.flow_node: null
            if Binder.is_potentially_executable_node(n)
                set n.flags: AstNode.flags_of(n) | NodeFlagsUnreachable
            this.bind_each_child(n)
            set in_assignment_pattern: save_in_assignment_pattern
            return
        }

        ; Every STATEMENT is reached by the flow that reaches it, and that is the
        ; only unconditional slot write in the binder. ★The range is the
        ; reference's own [KindFirstStatement, KindLastStatement], which does NOT
        ; include a Block or an EmptyStatement (242 and 243, below the range) even
        ; though both carry a slot — an upstream numbering quirk this port
        ; reproduces rather than tidies, because the flow dump compares the set.
        let k0 AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k0 >= KindFirstStatement
        {
            if k0 <= KindLastStatement
                set n.flow_node: current_flow
        }

        this.bind_children_dispatch(n, save_in_assignment_pattern)
        set in_assignment_pattern: save_in_assignment_pattern
    }

    procedure bind_children_dispatch(this, n: ref[AstNode], save_in_assignment_pattern: bool)
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)

        ; bindEachStatementFunctionsFirst: the function declarations of the list
        ; first, then everything else. NOT ForEachChild's order, and it is
        ; observable — two `function f` and a `var f` between them file their
        ; declarations in a different sequence under the two orders.
        if k = KindSourceFile
        {
            this.bind_each_statement_functions_first(AstNode.statements_of(n))
            this.bind(AstNode.end_of_file_token_of(n))
            return
        }
        if k = KindBlock
        {
            this.bind_each_statement_functions_first(AstNode.statements_of(n))
            return
        }
        if k = KindModuleBlock
        {
            this.bind_each_statement_functions_first(AstNode.statements_of(n))
            return
        }

        ; bindParameterFlow, and this is the arm that makes the order question
        ; real: the reference binds the modifiers, the `...`, the `?`, the type,
        ; the initializer and THEN the name — ForEachChild visits the name third.
        if k = KindParameter
        {
            this.bind_parameter_flow(n)
            return
        }

        ; ★★ THESE FOUR WERE `bind_each_child` UNTIL SLICE 92 AND THE READING THAT
        ; PUT THEM THERE WAS RIGHT: bindVariableDeclarationFlow is bindEachChild
        ; plus flow bookkeeping, and the other three are `bind(node.Expression())`
        ; plus flow — which for those kinds IS the whole child list. So the
        ; traversal does not move here; only the graph arrives.
        if k = KindVariableDeclaration
        {
            this.bind_variable_declaration_flow(n)
            return
        }
        if k = KindReturnStatement
        {
            this.bind_return_statement(n)
            return
        }
        if k = KindThrowStatement
        {
            this.bind_throw_statement(n)
            return
        }
        if k = KindExpressionStatement
        {
            this.bind_expression_statement(n)
            return
        }

        ; ── slice 32: the arm this slice was named after ────────────────────
        ;
        ; ★★★ IT IS bindEachChild, and MEASURING IT IS THE FINDING. The object
        ; literal was postponed twice — slices 30 and 31 both named it the head
        ; and both stepped around it, because "its bindChildren arm is the flow
        ; half" and opening it would be a chapter. Read out, the reference's arm
        ; is `b.inAssignmentPattern = saveInAssignmentPattern` followed by
        ; bindEachChild: the whole of the difference from the DEFAULT arm is that
        ; the flag is restored BEFORE the descent instead of after it, and
        ; `inAssignmentPattern` has exactly one reader in binder.go —
        ; bindDestructuringAssignmentFlow, reached only from the BinaryExpression
        ; arm of bindChildren, which is unported. So the field is §3.5be's shape
        ; (a flag whose only reader is unported is DECLARED, not ported) and the
        ; arm is the default arm. It is written out anyway rather than dropped
        ; into the default, for the reason the four statement arms above are: a
        ; reader looking up the reference's case label has to find the place it
        ; was answered.
        ;
        ; ★★★ AND THE PREDICTION IN THAT PARAGRAPH CAME TRUE ON SCHEDULE, WHICH IS
        ; WHY IT IS KEPT: it ended "the slice that ports the BinaryExpression arm
        ; owns the field, and having this arm already spelled out is what makes
        ; adding it a one-line change here plus the save/restore around the whole
        ; function". Slice 33 is that slice, the line below this comment is the
        ; one line, and the save/restore is the bind_children split above. The
        ; arm's SHAPE was the part that had to be transcribed; its effect arrived
        ; a slice later.
        ;
        ; ★ ONE case label, one body, transcribed whole (§3.5bb, and slice 31's
        ; own line about not splitting one): the four kinds are the operand
        ; positions of a destructuring assignment, which is why upstream groups
        ; them and why they will stay grouped when the flag arrives.
        if Binder.binds_children_as_assignment_pattern_operand(k)
        {
            set in_assignment_pattern: save_in_assignment_pattern
            this.bind_each_child(n)
            return
        }

        ; ── slice 33: the two heads ─────────────────────────────────────────
        ;
        ; ★★ THE BINARY ARM IS THE ONE THAT MOVES SYMBOLS. Both of its halves
        ; depart from ForEachChild: a destructuring assignment binds its operands
        ; in an order that DEPENDS on the flag, and every other binary expression
        ; binds four of its five child slots — the MODIFIERS are not bound at all.
        if k = KindBinaryExpression
        {
            if Binder.is_destructuring_assignment(n)
            {
                ; "Carry over whether we are in an assignment pattern to binary
                ; expressions that could actually be an initializer" — upstream's
                ; own comment, and the assignment is what makes `[a] = b` nested
                ; inside `({x: [a] = b} = c)` read as an assignment PATTERN.
                set in_assignment_pattern: save_in_assignment_pattern
                this.bind_destructuring_assignment_flow(n)
                return
            }
            this.bind_binary_expression_flow(n)
            return
        }

        if k = KindCallExpression
        {
            this.bind_call_expression_flow(n)
            return
        }

        ; ── the optional-chain family ────────────────────────────────────────
        ;
        ; ★★★ THREE ARMS THAT ARE ONE ARGUMENT, and the argument is why they are
        ; in this slice rather than in one of their own: bindAccessExpressionFlow
        ; and bindNonNullExpressionFlow are `IsOptionalChain(node) ?
        ; bindOptionalChainFlow : bindEachChild`, and the CALL arm above needs the
        ; same branch — so porting the call without them would leave `a?.b()`
        ; stopping at the property access, i.e. the chain half of the head unable
        ; to be exercised at all.
        ;
        ; ★★ What the chain path COSTS is nothing, and that is measured rather
        ; than assumed (§3.5v's fourth row): bindOptionalChain visits the
        ; expression and then bindOptionalChainRest visits the remaining slots —
        ; `?.` and the name; `?.` and the argument; `?.`, the type arguments and
        ; the arguments — which is ForEachChild's list of each kind, in
        ; ForEachChild's order. Everything else in those functions is labels and
        ; antecedents. It is written out because the reference has the functions,
        ; and a control that routes it back into bind_each_child is UNGATED by
        ; construction.
        if k = KindPropertyAccessExpression
        {
            this.bind_access_expression_flow(n)
            return
        }
        if k = KindElementAccessExpression
        {
            this.bind_access_expression_flow(n)
            return
        }
        if k = KindNonNullExpression
        {
            this.bind_non_null_expression_flow(n)
            return
        }

        ; ── slice 35: the six arms that make slice 35's OWN arms observable ──
        ;
        ; ★★★ THEY ARE IN THIS SLICE FOR ONE REASON: without them, five of the
        ; seven checks the fifteen bind arms just added produce output NOTHING CAN
        ; SEE. A unit whose bindChildren arm is unported reports and returns, so
        ; its whole dump is the marker — the TS1102 on `delete x`, the TS1100 on
        ; `eval++`, the TS1344 on a labeled declaration and the catch clause's own
        ; check (reachable only under a `try`) would every one of them have been
        ; shipped unmeasured. Same argument slices 30 and 31 make about a container
        ; and its members: the slice that lifts a condition owns every claim
        ; resting on it.
        ;
        ; ★★ FIVE OF THE SIX ARE bindEachChild ONCE THE FLOW IS REMOVED, and that
        ; is read off the reference rather than assumed:
        ;   bindDeleteExpressionFlow      = bindEachChild + bindAssignmentTargetFlow
        ;   bindPostfixUnaryExpressionFlow= bindEachChild + bindAssignmentTargetFlow
        ;   bindPrefixUnaryExpressionFlow = bindEachChild in BOTH branches (the `!`
        ;                                   branch only swaps the two flow targets)
        ;   bindLabeledStatement          = bind(Label), bind(Statement) — which is
        ;                                   ForEachChild's list and order — plus a
        ;                                   label-referenced FLAG the dump cannot see
        ;   bindTryStatement              = bind(TryBlock), bind(CatchClause),
        ;                                   bind(FinallyBlock), in that order
        ; So a control that routes any of these five back into bind_each_child is
        ; UNGATED by construction (§3.5v's fourth row), and each is written out for
        ; the reason the four statement arms above are: a reader looking up the
        ; reference's case label must find the place it was answered.
        if k = KindDeleteExpression
        {
            this.bind_delete_expression_flow(n)
            return
        }
        if k = KindPostfixUnaryExpression
        {
            this.bind_postfix_unary_expression_flow(n)
            return
        }
        if k = KindPrefixUnaryExpression
        {
            this.bind_prefix_unary_expression_flow(n)
            return
        }
        if k = KindLabeledStatement
        {
            this.bind_labeled_statement(n)
            return
        }
        if k = KindTryStatement
        {
            this.bind_try_statement(n)
            return
        }

        ; ★★★ THE SIXTH ONE IS A REORDER, and it is the only observable claim in
        ; this group. bindBindingElementFlow binds the `...`, the property name,
        ; the INITIALIZER and then the NAME, where the record's field order — which
        ; is child_at's order (§3.5s) — is `...`, property name, NAME, initializer.
        ; The reference states its reason in three lines of spec citation: "when
        ; evaluating a binding pattern, the initializer is evaluated before the
        ; binding pattern".
        ;
        ; ★★ IT IS OBSERVABLE THROUGH THE BIND-ORDER APPEND, slice 33's finding
        ; used as a tool for the second time: the `s` section numbers symbols in
        ; WALK order, so a bind ORDER can only be seen in the B section.
        ;
        ; ★★★ AND THE FIRST FIXTURE FOR IT MEASURED NOTHING, which is worth more
        ; than the arm. `var { arguments = ++eval } = src` gives two TS1100s whose
        ; order should flip and does not: the name's diagnostic comes from `bind`'s
        ; OWN arm — bind_variable_declaration_or_binding_element, above — which runs
        ; before bindChildren, so it precedes the initializer's under either order.
        ; A diagnostic that witnesses a CHILDREN order has to be produced BY a
        ; child, and a name checked in the arm is not a child. What works is a name
        ; that reports as an IDENTIFIER: binder_binding_element_order.ts pairs a
        ; future-reserved-word binding name (TS1212, from check_contextual_identifier
        ; while the name CHILD is bound) with an `eval`/`arguments` initializer
        ; (TS1100), and the reference's B section reads the 1100 first.
        ;
        ; ★ bindInitializer, which the reference calls instead of bind() for the
        ; initializer slot, is flow WHOLE: it binds the node and then joins the
        ; entry flow with the exit flow so that an initializer that was not
        ; evaluated does not count as a side effect (GH#49759). Its traversal is
        ; bind().
        if k = KindBindingElement
        {
            this.bind_binding_element_flow(n)
            return
        }

        ; ── slice 36: the statement-flow chapter, and the LAST arms of the
        ;    bindChildren switch ──────────────────────────────────────────────
        ;
        ; ★★★ THIRTEEN KINDS, AND SETTLING WHICH OF THEM CAN BE SEEN WAS THE WORK.
        ; Every one of these arms is a flow arm — a break target, a continue
        ; target, a branch label per clause, an antecedent per edge — so what a
        ; port of them can claim is the TRAVERSAL, and §3.5v asks for "one slice or
        ; thirteen rows of *indistinguishable*" to be answered BEFORE the code is
        ; written. Read arm against ast_generated.go's ForEachChild, one at a time:
        ;
        ;   bindWhileStatement        Expression, Statement            = ForEachChild
        ;   bindDoStatement           Statement, Expression            = ForEachChild
        ;   bindIfStatement           Expr, Then, Else                 = ForEachChild
        ;   bindSwitchStatement       Expression, CaseBlock            = ForEachChild
        ;   bindCaseBlock             the clauses, in order            = ForEachChild
        ;   bindCaseOrDefaultClause   Expression, Statements           = ForEachChild
        ;   bindConditionalExpr.Flow  Cond, ?, WhenTrue, :, WhenFalse  = ForEachChild
        ;   bindBreakStatement        Label                            = ForEachChild
        ;   bindContinueStatement     Label                            = ForEachChild
        ;   bindForStatement          Init, Cond, STATEMENT, INCREMENTOR    ✗
        ;   bindForInOrOfStatement    EXPRESSION, Await, Init, Statement    ✗
        ;
        ; Ten of the thirteen are the default arm; three are a real reorder, and
        ; both of the reorders are the RUNTIME order — a `for` body runs before its
        ; update, `for (x of y)` evaluates y before it assigns to x — which is the
        ; order a flow graph has to be built in and the reason these functions
        ; exist at all.
        ;
        ; ★★★ THEY SHIP AS ONE SLICE BECAUSE THE LIST IS A LIST. While a single
        ; entry stands, every unit containing that kind reports and RETURNS, so the
        ; 1 542 stage-2 units this chapter parks are parked by all thirteen jointly
        ; and unlocked by none of them alone — slice 35's finding, stated as the
        ; reason for a grouping rather than found afterwards.
        ;
        ; ★★ WHERE AN ORDER CAN BE SEEN IS THE B SECTION AND NOWHERE ELSE. The `s`
        ; section numbers symbols in WALK order (slice 33's second finding), so a
        ; bind ORDER shows only in the bind-order append of the bind DIAGNOSTICS —
        ; hence three fixtures that put a REPORTING expression in every slot whose
        ; order is in question. Two are the reorders themselves
        ; (binder_for_statement_order, binder_for_in_of_order); the third is the
        ; FALSIFIABILITY of the ten rows above (binder_statement_child_order), and
        ; it had to be written because before it existed, reversing any of those ten
        ; arms' child walk changed NOTHING in 965 units — so "= ForEachChild" was a
        ; reading rather than a measurement. ★The reference's own B section is
        ; non-monotonic in POSITION on the first two and strictly monotonic across
        ; sixteen diagnostics on the third, which is the same statement with both
        ; signs, and both are read off the ORACLE alone.
        ;
        ; ★★ AND ONE PIECE OF NON-TRAVERSAL CONTENT IS LEFT OUT WITH ITS REASON.
        ; bindBreakOrContinueStatement looks the label up in the active-label list
        ; and sets `referenced` on it; the only reader of that field is
        ; bindLabeledStatement, which turns a false into NodeFlagsUnreachable on the
        ; LABEL node — "the checker will decide whether to report it", upstream's
        ; own comment. So the content is a NODE FLAG, and no dump in this suite
        ; carries a node's flags after a bind: *unmodelled* (§3.5v's fourth row),
        ; not deferred, and the field that would make it observable is the very
        ; flags column slice 35 named — which the flow slice owns, since
        ; bindContainer writes HasImplicitReturn, HasExplicitReturn and the
        ; reachability reset into the same line.
        if k = KindWhileStatement
        {
            this.bind_while_statement(n)
            return
        }
        if k = KindDoStatement
        {
            this.bind_do_statement(n)
            return
        }

        ; bindForStatement — initializer, condition, STATEMENT, incrementor, where
        ; ForEachChild visits the incrementor THIRD. The two flow labels between
        ; them (preBody, preIncrementor) are what the reorder is for upstream.
        if k = KindForStatement
        {
            this.bind_for_statement(n)
            return
        }

        ; bindForInOrOfStatement — ONE reference function over both kinds, and the
        ; kind test belongs to it, so both arms route into one procedure the way
        ; the property/element access pair above route into bind_access_expression_flow.
        if k = KindForInStatement
        {
            this.bind_for_in_or_of_statement(n)
            return
        }
        if k = KindForOfStatement
        {
            this.bind_for_in_or_of_statement(n)
            return
        }

        if k = KindIfStatement
        {
            this.bind_if_statement(n)
            return
        }
        if k = KindBreakStatement
        {
            this.bind_break_statement(n)
            return
        }
        if k = KindContinueStatement
        {
            this.bind_continue_statement(n)
            return
        }
        if k = KindSwitchStatement
        {
            this.bind_switch_statement(n)
            return
        }

        ; ★ bindCaseBlock is the one row of the table above whose "= ForEachChild"
        ; needs an argument rather than a reading, because its loop LOOKS like it
        ; can bind a clause twice: an inner loop binds each clause whose statement
        ; list is EMPTY and advances past it (a fallthrough group shares one flow
        ; label), and the outer body then binds the clause the inner loop stopped
        ; at. It stops either on a non-empty clause or on the LAST one, and both
        ; are bound by the outer body — so every clause is bound exactly once, in
        ; list order, which is visitNodeList(node.Clauses).
        if k = KindCaseBlock
        {
            this.bind_case_block(n)
            return
        }

        ; bindCaseOrDefaultClause, one case label over both kinds: the expression
        ; under the PRE-SWITCH flow, then the statements. A default clause has no
        ; expression, which is the reference's own null test rather than a second
        ; arm.
        if k = KindCaseClause
        {
            this.bind_case_or_default_clause(n)
            return
        }
        if k = KindDefaultClause
        {
            this.bind_case_or_default_clause(n)
            return
        }

        if k = KindConditionalExpression
        {
            this.bind_conditional_expression_flow(n)
            return
        }

        if Binder.kind_has_unported_bind_children_arm(k)
        {
            this.record_unported("bind-children", k)
            return
        }

        this.bind_each_child(n)
    }

    ; The four kinds of bindChildren's shared assignment-pattern case label, in
    ; the reference's own order. A predicate rather than four copies of the body,
    ; because upstream has one body and splitting it would put the same two lines
    ; in four places.
    function binds_children_as_assignment_pattern_operand(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindObjectLiteralExpression
            return true
        if k = KindArrayLiteralExpression
            return true
        if k = KindPropertyAssignment
            return true
        if k = KindSpreadElement
            return true
        false
    }

    ; The kinds bindChildren has an arm for and this port has not. Read off
    ; binder.go's switch, arm for arm.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS EMPTY AS OF SLICE 36, so BOTH of the reference's switches are
    ; complete and nothing in either one reports itself any more. It stays as a
    ; function for the reason kind_has_unported_bind_arm does — it is the
    ; INVENTORY that §2's step 3 re-reads against binder.go after a pin bump, and
    ; a new arm upstream is invisible to every measurement this suite has, since a
    ; kind nobody binds simply produces nothing.
    ;
    ; ★ The thirteen it lost were ONE family, the statement-flow chapter, and the
    ; block in bind_children_dispatch that answered them carries the reading that
    ; decided how: ten are ForEachChild's own order, three are a reorder the B
    ; section can see.
    function kind_has_unported_bind_children_arm(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindDoStatement
            return true
        false
    }

    procedure bind_each_child(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        var i 0
        while true
        {
            let c AstNode.child_at(n, i)
            if c = null
                return
            this.bind(c)
            if this.is_unported()
                return
            set i: i + 1
        }
    }

    procedure bind_each(this, list: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?)
    {
        if list = null
            return
        let len list.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            let s: ref[AstNode]? list.at(i)
            this.bind(s)
            if this.is_unported()
                return
            set i: i + 1
        }
    }

    procedure bind_each_statement_functions_first(this, list: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?)
    {
        if list = null
            return
        let len list.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            let s: ref[AstNode]? list.at(i)
            if AstNode.kind_of(s) = KindFunctionDeclaration
                this.bind(s)
            if this.is_unported()
                return
            set i: i + 1
        }
        var j 0
        while j < len
        {
            let s: ref[AstNode]? list.at(j)
            if AstNode.kind_of(s) <> KindFunctionDeclaration
                this.bind(s)
            if this.is_unported()
                return
            set j: j + 1
        }
    }

    ; ── the FLOW ARMS, complete as of slice 92 ──────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT SLICES 33–36 LEFT HERE WAS THE TRAVERSAL, and every one of them
    ; said so at its own line: *"everything the reference's flow half does with
    ; labels, antecedents and FlowNodes is absent here"*. This slice puts it back,
    ; and the traversals do not move — which is the check on the earlier readings
    ; and the reason the four yardsticks stay green through a change this size.
    ;
    ; ★★ The shared vocabulary, once, because every arm below is built from it:
    ; a LABEL is a junction (`createBranchLabel` for a merge, `createLoopLabel`
    ; for one a back edge returns to), an ANTECEDENT is an incoming edge, and
    ; `finishFlowLabel` collapses a junction with one edge into that edge. So the
    ; graph a `while` builds is three labels and five edges, and after finishing
    ; it is usually two nodes.

    ; setContinueTarget. A `continue` inside `outer: inner: for (…)` must reach
    ; the loop and not the labels, so every label directly above this statement
    ; has its continue target pointed at the loop's own.
    procedure set_continue_target(this, n: ref[AstNode], target: ref[FlowNode]) returns ref[FlowNode]
    {
        var active active_label_list
        var node: ref[AstNode]? n
        while active <> null
        {
            let p AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
            if p = null
                break
            if AstNode.kind_of(p as ref[AstNode]) <> KindLabeledStatement
                break
            let l active as ref[ActiveLabel]
            set l.continue_target: target
            set active: l.next
            set node: p
        }
        target
    }

    ; doWithConditionalBranches, for the ONE action it is called with here.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE REFERENCE PASSES A METHOD VALUE and this port cannot: §3.1 refuses
    ; dynamic dispatch in the core, and a delegate would make the callee unknown
    ; at exactly the place the purity and escape questions are asked. There are
    ; two actions upstream — `bind` and `bindOptionalChainRest` — so there are two
    ; procedures here, and they differ in one line each.
    procedure do_with_conditional_branches_bind(this, value: ref[AstNode]?, true_target: ref[FlowNode]?, false_target: ref[FlowNode]?)
    {
        let saved_true current_true_target
        let saved_false current_false_target
        set current_true_target: true_target
        set current_false_target: false_target
        this.bind(value)
        set current_true_target: saved_true
        set current_false_target: saved_false
    }

    procedure do_with_conditional_branches_chain_rest(this, value: ref[AstNode], true_target: ref[FlowNode]?, false_target: ref[FlowNode]?)
    {
        let saved_true current_true_target
        let saved_false current_false_target
        set current_true_target: true_target
        set current_false_target: false_target
        this.bind_optional_chain_rest(value)
        set current_true_target: saved_true
        set current_false_target: saved_false
    }

    ; bindCondition. ★The guard is the interesting half: a logical expression, a
    ; logical ASSIGNMENT and an outermost optional chain have already added their
    ; own antecedents to the two targets from the inside, so adding a condition
    ; node here would double the edge.
    procedure bind_condition(this, node: ref[AstNode]?, true_target: ref[FlowNode], false_target: ref[FlowNode])
    {
        this.do_with_conditional_branches_bind(node, true_target, false_target)
        var adds true
        if node <> null
        {
            let nd node as ref[AstNode]
            if Binder.is_logical_assignment_expression(nd)
                set adds: false
            if Binder.is_logical_expression(nd)
                set adds: false
            if Binder.is_optional_chain(nd)
            {
                if Binder.is_outermost_optional_chain(nd)
                    set adds: false
            }
        }
        if adds
        {
            this.add_antecedent(true_target, this.create_flow_condition(FlowFlagsTrueCondition, current_flow, node))
            this.add_antecedent(false_target, this.create_flow_condition(FlowFlagsFalseCondition, current_flow, node))
        }
    }

    procedure bind_iterative_statement(this, node: ref[AstNode]?, break_target: ref[FlowNode], continue_target: ref[FlowNode])
    {
        let save_break current_break_target
        let save_continue current_continue_target
        set current_break_target: break_target
        set current_continue_target: continue_target
        this.bind(node)
        set current_break_target: save_break
        set current_continue_target: save_continue
    }

    ; bindAssignmentTargetFlow. ★★ Slices 33 and 35 both wrote that this function
    ; "binds NOTHING" and both were right about the TRAVERSAL: what it does is
    ; walk a destructuring target's shape and record one FlowFlagsAssignment
    ; mutation per narrowable leaf. That is the whole reason a `let x` narrowed by
    ; an `if` goes back to its declared type after `[x] = pair`.
    procedure bind_assignment_target_flow(this, node: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        if node = null
            return
        let n node as ref[AstNode]
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindArrayLiteralExpression
        {
            let elements AstNode.elements_of(n)
            let cnt AstNode.list_count(elements)
            var i 0
            while i < cnt
            {
                let e AstNode.child_in_list(elements, i)
                if AstNode.kind_of(e) = KindSpreadElement
                    this.bind_assignment_target_flow(AstNode.expression_of(e))
                else
                    this.bind_destructuring_target_flow(e)
                set i: i + 1
            }
            return
        }
        if k = KindObjectLiteralExpression
        {
            let props AstNode.properties_of(n)
            let cnt AstNode.list_count(props)
            var i 0
            while i < cnt
            {
                let pr AstNode.child_in_list(props, i)
                let pk AstNode.kind_of(pr)
                if pk = KindPropertyAssignment
                    this.bind_destructuring_target_flow(AstNode.initializer_of(pr))
                if pk = KindShorthandPropertyAssignment
                    this.bind_assignment_target_flow(AstNode.name_of(pr))
                if pk = KindSpreadAssignment
                    this.bind_assignment_target_flow(AstNode.expression_of(pr))
                set i: i + 1
            }
            return
        }
        if Binder.is_narrowable_reference(n)
            set current_flow: this.create_flow_mutation(FlowFlagsAssignment, current_flow, n)
    }

    ; bindDestructuringTargetFlow — a defaulted element (`[a = 1] = xs`) is a
    ; binary expression whose LEFT half is the target.
    procedure bind_destructuring_target_flow(this, node: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        if node = null
            return
        let n node as ref[AstNode]
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindBinaryExpression
        {
            if AstNode.binary_operator_kind(n) = KindEqualsToken
            {
                this.bind_assignment_target_flow(AstNode.binary_left_of(n))
                return
            }
        }
        this.bind_assignment_target_flow(n)
    }

    ; ── the iteration statements ────────────────────────────────────────────

    procedure bind_while_statement(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let pre_while_label this.set_continue_target(n, this.create_loop_label())
        let pre_body_label this.create_branch_label()
        let post_while_label this.create_branch_label()
        this.add_antecedent(pre_while_label, current_flow)
        set current_flow: pre_while_label
        this.bind_condition(AstNode.expression_of(n), pre_body_label, post_while_label)
        set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(pre_body_label)
        this.bind_iterative_statement(AstNode.statement_of(n), post_while_label, pre_while_label)
        this.add_antecedent(pre_while_label, current_flow)
        set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(post_while_label)
    }

    procedure bind_do_statement(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let pre_do_label this.create_loop_label()
        let pre_condition_label this.set_continue_target(n, this.create_branch_label())
        let post_do_label this.create_branch_label()
        this.add_antecedent(pre_do_label, current_flow)
        set current_flow: pre_do_label
        this.bind_iterative_statement(AstNode.statement_of(n), post_do_label, pre_condition_label)
        this.add_antecedent(pre_condition_label, current_flow)
        set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(pre_condition_label)
        this.bind_condition(AstNode.expression_of(n), pre_do_label, post_do_label)
        set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(post_do_label)
    }

    ; bindForStatement — initializer, condition, STATEMENT, incrementor, where
    ; ForEachChild visits the incrementor THIRD. The two labels between them
    ; (preBody, preIncrementor) are what the reorder is for, and slice 36 ported
    ; the reorder alone.
    procedure bind_for_statement(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let pre_loop_label this.set_continue_target(n, this.create_loop_label())
        let pre_body_label this.create_branch_label()
        let pre_incrementor_label this.create_branch_label()
        let post_loop_label this.create_branch_label()
        this.bind(AstNode.initializer_of(n))
        this.add_antecedent(pre_loop_label, current_flow)
        set current_flow: pre_loop_label
        this.bind_condition(AstNode.for_condition_of(n), pre_body_label, post_loop_label)
        set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(pre_body_label)
        this.bind_iterative_statement(AstNode.statement_of(n), post_loop_label, pre_incrementor_label)
        this.add_antecedent(pre_incrementor_label, current_flow)
        set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(pre_incrementor_label)
        this.bind(AstNode.for_incrementor_of(n))
        this.add_antecedent(pre_loop_label, current_flow)
        set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(post_loop_label)
    }

    ; The EXPRESSION goes first, where ForEachChild visits it third: `for (x of y)`
    ; evaluates y before it assigns to x, and the reference needs the sequence
    ; expression / loop label / initializer to hang the loop's antecedents on. The
    ; await modifier is bound only on the `of` form — the `in` form is BUILT with a
    ; null there (ForInOrOfStatementData's header), so the test is about the KIND
    ; and not about the slot being empty.
    ;
    ; ★★ SLICE 36's DEFERRAL OF bindAssignmentTargetFlow CAME DUE HERE. Its note
    ; said the call "binds NOTHING … so it adds no traversal and is not ported".
    ; True of the traversal and false of the graph: `for (x of xs)` ASSIGNS to x on
    ; every iteration, and without the mutation a narrowing established before the
    ; loop would survive it.
    procedure bind_for_in_or_of_statement(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let pre_loop_label this.set_continue_target(n, this.create_loop_label())
        let post_loop_label this.create_branch_label()
        this.bind(AstNode.expression_of(n))
        this.add_antecedent(pre_loop_label, current_flow)
        set current_flow: pre_loop_label
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindForOfStatement
            this.bind(AstNode.for_await_modifier_of(n))
        this.add_antecedent(post_loop_label, current_flow)
        let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(n)
        this.bind(initializer)
        if AstNode.kind_of(initializer) <> KindVariableDeclarationList
            this.bind_assignment_target_flow(initializer)
        this.bind_iterative_statement(AstNode.statement_of(n), post_loop_label, pre_loop_label)
        this.add_antecedent(pre_loop_label, current_flow)
        set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(post_loop_label)
    }

    procedure bind_if_statement(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let then_label this.create_branch_label()
        let else_label this.create_branch_label()
        let post_if_label this.create_branch_label()
        this.bind_condition(AstNode.expression_of(n), then_label, else_label)
        set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(then_label)
        this.bind(AstNode.then_statement_of(n))
        this.add_antecedent(post_if_label, current_flow)
        set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(else_label)
        this.bind(AstNode.else_statement_of(n))
        this.add_antecedent(post_if_label, current_flow)
        set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(post_if_label)
    }

    ; ── the three statements that END a flow ────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ ALL THREE SET current_flow TO THE SENTINEL, and that single assignment
    ; is what makes everything after them UNREACHABLE — the mark bindChildren
    ; writes, the TS7027 the checker reports, and the missing HasImplicitReturn on
    ; the function around them. `has_explicit_return` is the return's second
    ; product and its only reader is bindContainer.

    procedure bind_return_statement(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.bind(AstNode.expression_of(n))
        if current_return_target <> null
            this.add_antecedent(current_return_target as ref[FlowNode], current_flow)
        set current_flow: unreachable_flow
        set has_explicit_return: true
        set has_flow_effects: true
    }

    procedure bind_throw_statement(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.bind(AstNode.expression_of(n))
        set current_flow: unreachable_flow
        set has_flow_effects: true
    }

    procedure bind_break_statement(this, n: ref[AstNode])
        this.bind_break_or_continue_statement(AstNode.label_name_of(n), current_break_target, true)

    procedure bind_continue_statement(this, n: ref[AstNode])
        this.bind_break_or_continue_statement(AstNode.label_name_of(n), current_continue_target, false)

    ; bindBreakOrContinueStatement. ★The reference passes a METHOD VALUE to select
    ; which of an active label's two targets to use; a bool selects it here, for
    ; the reason do_with_conditional_branches has two copies.
    ;
    ; ★★ `activeLabel.referenced = true` IS THE WRITE SLICE 36 DEFERRED with its
    ; reason — *"the content is a NODE FLAG, and no dump in this suite carries a
    ; node's flags after a bind"*. This slice is that dump, and the flag's reader
    ; is bindLabeledStatement below.
    procedure bind_break_or_continue_statement(this, label_name: ref[AstNode]?, current_target: ref[FlowNode]?, wants_break: bool)
    {
        this.bind(label_name)
        if label_name = null
        {
            this.bind_break_or_continue_flow(current_target)
            return
        }
        let l label_name as ref[AstNode]
        let active this.find_active_label(AstNode.identifier_text_of(l))
        if active = null
            return
        let a active as ref[ActiveLabel]
        set a.referenced: true
        if wants_break
            this.bind_break_or_continue_flow(a.break_target)
        else
            this.bind_break_or_continue_flow(a.continue_target)
    }

    procedure find_active_label(this, name_data: Slice[char]) returns ref[ActiveLabel]?
    {
        var active active_label_list
        while active <> null
        {
            let l active as ref[ActiveLabel]
            if SymbolTable.names_equal(l.name, name_data)
                return l
            set active: l.next
        }
        null
    }

    ; ★ A `continue` whose active label has no CONTINUE target — `outer: { …
    ; continue outer … }` on a plain block — reaches this with a null and does
    ; nothing at all, leaving the flow reachable. That is upstream's own null test
    ; and not a leniency: the grammar error is the checker's to report.
    procedure bind_break_or_continue_flow(this, flow_label: ref[FlowNode]?)
    {
        if flow_label = null
            return
        this.add_antecedent(flow_label as ref[FlowNode], current_flow)
        set current_flow: unreachable_flow
        set has_flow_effects: true
    }

    ; ── bindTryStatement ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE LARGEST ARM IN THE FILE, AND THE REDUCE LABEL IS WHY. A `finally`
    ; block is reached five ways — normal completion of the try, normal completion
    ; of the catch, a RETURN in either, an exception in the try, an exception in
    ; the catch — but an analysis that walks PAST the finally must consider only
    ; the first two, and one that walks back from a `return` only the third. The
    ; reference solves it by injecting a REDUCE LABEL, a node carrying an
    ; alternate antecedent list that the checker swaps in as it passes.
    ;
    ; ★★ The exception label is REBUILT for the catch clause, because a catch is a
    ; second try: an exception thrown inside it does not go back to its own start.
    procedure bind_try_statement(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let save_return_target current_return_target
        let save_exception_target current_exception_target
        let normal_exit_label this.create_branch_label()
        let return_label this.create_branch_label()
        var exception_label this.create_branch_label()
        let finally_block AstNode.finally_block_of(n)
        if finally_block <> null
            set current_return_target: return_label
        this.add_antecedent(exception_label, current_flow)
        set current_exception_target: exception_label
        this.bind(AstNode.try_block_of(n))
        this.add_antecedent(normal_exit_label, current_flow)
        let catch_clause AstNode.catch_clause_of(n)
        if catch_clause <> null
        {
            set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(exception_label)
            set exception_label: this.create_branch_label()
            this.add_antecedent(exception_label, current_flow)
            set current_exception_target: exception_label
            this.bind(catch_clause)
            this.add_antecedent(normal_exit_label, current_flow)
        }
        set current_return_target: save_return_target
        set current_exception_target: save_exception_target
        if finally_block = null
        {
            set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(normal_exit_label)
            return
        }

        let finally_label this.create_branch_label()
        ; ★ The three lists are combined by DIRECT assignment and not through
        ; add_antecedent, so this is the one label in the file whose antecedents
        ; may contain DUPLICATES — visible in the flow dump as a repeated id, and
        ; it is upstream's shape rather than a defect.
        set finally_label.antecedents: this.combine_flow_lists(normal_exit_label.antecedents, this.combine_flow_lists(exception_label.antecedents, return_label.antecedents))
        set current_flow: finally_label
        this.bind(finally_block)
        if Binder.flow_is_unreachable(current_flow)
        {
            ; The end of the finally is unreachable, so the end of the whole `try`
            ; is unreachable.
            set current_flow: unreachable_flow
            return
        }
        if current_return_target <> null
        {
            if return_label.antecedents <> null
                this.add_antecedent(current_return_target as ref[FlowNode], this.create_reduce_label(finally_label, return_label.antecedents, current_flow))
        }
        if current_exception_target <> null
        {
            if exception_label.antecedents <> null
                this.add_antecedent(current_exception_target as ref[FlowNode], this.create_reduce_label(finally_label, exception_label.antecedents, current_flow))
        }
        ; `try { return 1 } finally { … }` — the finally completes, the try does
        ; not, so what follows the statement is unreachable.
        if normal_exit_label.antecedents = null
        {
            set current_flow: unreachable_flow
            return
        }
        set current_flow: this.create_reduce_label(finally_label, normal_exit_label.antecedents, current_flow)
    }

    ; ── the switch family ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

    procedure bind_switch_statement(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let post_switch_label this.create_branch_label()
        this.bind(AstNode.expression_of(n))
        let save_break_target current_break_target
        let save_pre_switch_case_flow pre_switch_case_flow
        set current_break_target: post_switch_label
        set pre_switch_case_flow: current_flow
        let case_block AstNode.case_block_of(n)
        this.bind(case_block)
        this.add_antecedent(post_switch_label, current_flow)
        ; ★ Without a `default` the switch may fall past every clause, and the
        ; clause range 0..0 is how the reference spells *none of them matched*.
        var has_default false
        let clauses AstNode.case_clauses_of(case_block)
        let cnt AstNode.list_count(clauses)
        var i 0
        while i < cnt
        {
            if AstNode.kind_of(AstNode.child_in_list(clauses, i)) = KindDefaultClause
                set has_default: true
            set i: i + 1
        }
        if has_default = false
            this.add_antecedent(post_switch_label, this.create_flow_switch_clause(pre_switch_case_flow, n, 0, 0))
        set current_break_target: save_break_target
        set pre_switch_case_flow: save_pre_switch_case_flow
        set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(post_switch_label)
    }

    ; bindCaseBlock. ★★ The inner loop is what slice 36 argued about: it advances
    ; over EMPTY clauses so that a fallthrough group `case 1: case 2:` shares one
    ; flow label, and slice 36's reading — every clause is bound exactly once, in
    ; list order — is what makes the traversal ForEachChild's. Both halves are
    ; true, and the flow half is the range `[clauseStart, i+1)` a switch-clause
    ; node carries.
    procedure bind_case_block(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let switch_statement AstNode.parent_node_of(n) as ref[AstNode]
        let clauses AstNode.case_clauses_of(n)
        let cnt AstNode.list_count(clauses)
        let subject AstNode.expression_of(switch_statement)
        var is_narrowing_switch false
        if AstNode.kind_of(subject) = KindTrueKeyword
            set is_narrowing_switch: true
        if Binder.is_narrowing_expression(subject)
            set is_narrowing_switch: true
        var fallthrough_flow: ref[FlowNode]? unreachable_flow
        var i 0
        while i < cnt
        {
            let clause_start i
            while true
            {
                if AstNode.list_count(AstNode.statements_of(AstNode.child_in_list(clauses, i))) <> 0
                    break
                if (i + 1) >= cnt
                    break
                if Binder.flow_is_unreachable(fallthrough_flow)
                    set current_flow: pre_switch_case_flow
                this.bind(AstNode.child_in_list(clauses, i))
                set i: i + 1
            }
            let pre_case_label this.create_branch_label()
            var pre_case_flow pre_switch_case_flow
            if is_narrowing_switch
                set pre_case_flow: this.create_flow_switch_clause(pre_switch_case_flow, switch_statement, clause_start, i + 1)
            this.add_antecedent(pre_case_label, pre_case_flow)
            this.add_antecedent(pre_case_label, fallthrough_flow)
            set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(pre_case_label)
            let clause AstNode.child_in_list(clauses, i) as ref[AstNode]
            this.bind(clause)
            set fallthrough_flow: current_flow
            if Binder.flow_is_unreachable(current_flow) = false
            {
                if i <> (cnt - 1)
                    set clause.fallthrough_flow_node: current_flow
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
    }

    ; bindCaseOrDefaultClause. ★The clause's own EXPRESSION is bound under the
    ; PRE-SWITCH flow rather than under the clause's — `case f():` is evaluated
    ; before any clause has been chosen — and the current flow is put back
    ; afterwards. A default clause has no expression, which is the reference's own
    ; null test rather than a second arm.
    procedure bind_case_or_default_clause(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let expression AstNode.expression_of(n)
        if expression <> null
        {
            let save_current_flow current_flow
            set current_flow: pre_switch_case_flow
            this.bind(expression)
            set current_flow: save_current_flow
        }
        this.bind_each(AstNode.statements_of(n))
    }

    procedure bind_expression_statement(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let expression AstNode.expression_of(n)
        this.bind(expression)
        this.maybe_bind_expression_flow_if_call(expression)
    }

    ; maybeBindExpressionFlowIfCall — a top-level (or comma-operand) call with a
    ; DOTTED callee is potentially an assertion, so it joins the flow graph. ★A
    ; `super()` call is excluded here and admitted by bindCallExpressionFlow's own
    ; arm, which is the same node reached through a different question.
    procedure maybe_bind_expression_flow_if_call(this, node: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        if node = null
            return
        let n node as ref[AstNode]
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) <> KindCallExpression
            return
        let callee AstNode.expression_of(n)
        if AstNode.kind_of(callee) = KindSuperKeyword
            return
        if Binder.is_dotted_name(callee) = false
            return
        set current_flow: this.create_flow_call(current_flow, n)
    }

    ; bindLabeledStatement. ★★ THE UNUSED-LABEL MARK IS THE SECOND DIAGNOSTIC
    ; THIS SLICE BUYS: a label nothing referenced gets NodeFlagsUnreachable on the
    ; LABEL NODE — "the checker will decide whether to report it", upstream's own
    ; comment — and the checker reports TS7028.
    procedure bind_labeled_statement(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let post_statement_label this.create_branch_label()
        let label_name AstNode.label_name_of(n) as ref[AstNode]
        let al &ActiveLabel^host(active_label_list, AstNode.identifier_text_of(label_name), post_statement_label)
        set active_label_list: al
        this.bind(label_name)
        this.bind(AstNode.statement_of(n))
        if al.referenced = false
            set label_name.flags: AstNode.flags_of(label_name) | NodeFlagsUnreachable
        set active_label_list: al.next
        this.add_antecedent(post_statement_label, current_flow)
        set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(post_statement_label)
    }

    ; bindPrefixUnaryExpressionFlow. ★`!x` SWAPS the two conditional targets and
    ; that is its whole content — the negation is not a node in the graph, it is
    ; an exchange of the labels the operand's condition will be added to.
    procedure bind_prefix_unary_expression_flow(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let op AstNode.prefix_unary_operator_kind(n)
        if op = KindExclamationToken
        {
            let save_true current_true_target
            set current_true_target: current_false_target
            set current_false_target: save_true
            this.bind_each_child(n)
            set current_false_target: current_true_target
            set current_true_target: save_true
            return
        }
        this.bind_each_child(n)
        var mutates false
        if op = KindPlusPlusToken
            set mutates: true
        if op = KindMinusMinusToken
            set mutates: true
        if mutates
            this.bind_assignment_target_flow(AstNode.prefix_unary_operand_of(n))
    }

    ; bindPostfixUnaryExpressionFlow. ★The operator test is CONSTANT here for the
    ; reason get_assignment_target's postfix arm gives: the record stores no
    ; operator kind and the grammar admits only `++` and `--`.
    procedure bind_postfix_unary_expression_flow(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.bind_each_child(n)
        this.bind_assignment_target_flow(AstNode.postfix_unary_operand_of(n))
    }

    ; bindDestructuringAssignmentFlow. THE order swap, and it is real: the
    ; operands of `({a} = b)` are bound left-then-right at the top level and
    ; right-then-left one level in — an object literal declares an anonymous
    ; symbol and a symbol per property, so which side is walked first decides the
    ; SEQUENCE the symbols are created in, which the dump compares by index.
    ;
    ; ★ `expr.Type` rides with the LEFT side in both branches, which is upstream's
    ; grouping and not a detail: the slot is the JSDoc reparser's `@type` on an
    ; assignment declaration, i.e. an annotation of the target.
    procedure bind_destructuring_assignment_flow(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if in_assignment_pattern
        {
            set in_assignment_pattern: false
            this.bind(AstNode.binary_operator_token_of(n))
            this.bind(AstNode.binary_right_of(n))
            set in_assignment_pattern: true
            this.bind(AstNode.binary_left_of(n))
            this.bind(AstNode.binary_type_of(n))
        }
        else
        {
            set in_assignment_pattern: true
            this.bind(AstNode.binary_left_of(n))
            this.bind(AstNode.binary_type_of(n))
            set in_assignment_pattern: false
            this.bind(AstNode.binary_operator_token_of(n))
            this.bind(AstNode.binary_right_of(n))
        }
        this.bind_assignment_target_flow(AstNode.binary_left_of(n))
    }

    ; bindBinaryExpressionFlow.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT BINDS FOUR OF THE FIVE CHILD SLOTS: the MODIFIERS are not bound, in
    ; either branch. ForEachChild visits them first (a BinaryExpression carries a
    ; ModifiersBase because the JSDoc reparser hangs `@public`/`@readonly` off a
    ; `this.x = 1` assignment), so a port that reached for bind_each_child here
    ; would bind a child the reference does not.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE LOGICAL BRANCH'S POINT IS `has_flow_effects`: a junction label is
    ; built for `a && b`, and if NOTHING under it diverted control the label is
    ; thrown away and the flow the expression started with is restored. That is
    ; what keeps `if (a && b)` from adding two nodes per operand to every graph.
    procedure bind_binary_expression_flow(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let op AstNode.binary_operator_kind(n)
        var logical false
        if Binder.is_logical_or_coalescing_binary_operator(op)
            set logical: true
        if Binder.is_logical_or_coalescing_assignment_operator(op)
            set logical: true
        if logical
        {
            if Binder.is_top_level_logical_expression(n)
            {
                let post_expression_label this.create_branch_label()
                let save_current_flow current_flow
                let save_has_flow_effects has_flow_effects
                set has_flow_effects: false
                this.bind_logical_like_expression(n, post_expression_label, post_expression_label)
                if has_flow_effects
                    set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(post_expression_label)
                else
                    set current_flow: save_current_flow
                if save_has_flow_effects
                    set has_flow_effects: true
                return
            }
            this.bind_logical_like_expression(n, current_true_target as ref[FlowNode], current_false_target as ref[FlowNode])
            return
        }

        this.bind(AstNode.binary_left_of(n))
        this.bind(AstNode.binary_type_of(n))
        if op = KindCommaToken
            this.maybe_bind_expression_flow_if_call(AstNode.binary_left_of(n))
        this.bind(AstNode.binary_operator_token_of(n))
        this.bind(AstNode.binary_right_of(n))
        if op = KindCommaToken
            this.maybe_bind_expression_flow_if_call(AstNode.binary_right_of(n))
        if Parser.is_assignment_operator(op) = false
            return
        if Binder.is_assignment_target(n)
            return
        this.bind_assignment_target_flow(AstNode.binary_left_of(n))
        ; ★ `a[i] = x` on a narrowable `a` is an ARRAY MUTATION as well as an
        ; assignment: the element's identity is not stable, so what is recorded is
        ; that the array changed.
        if op <> KindEqualsToken
            return
        let left AstNode.binary_left_of(n)
        if AstNode.kind_of(left) <> KindElementAccessExpression
            return
        if Binder.is_narrowable_operand(AstNode.expression_of(left))
            set current_flow: this.create_flow_mutation(FlowFlagsArrayMutation, current_flow, n)
    }

    ; bindLogicalLikeExpression, i.e. `a && b`, `a || b`, `a ?? b` and their three
    ; compound assignments.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT SKIPS `expr.Type` AS WELL AS THE MODIFIERS, which the non-logical
    ; branch above does not — the only place in this port where two branches of
    ; one reference function visit different CHILD SLOTS.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ASYMMETRY OF `&&` AND `||` IS THE WHOLE GRAPH: for `a && b` the
    ; LEFT's true branch goes to the right operand and its false branch straight
    ; to the caller's false target; for `a || b` it is the other way round. Three
    ; lines, and every narrowing of a guarded expression rests on them.
    procedure bind_logical_like_expression(this, n: ref[AstNode], true_target: ref[FlowNode], false_target: ref[FlowNode])
    {
        let pre_right_label this.create_branch_label()
        let op AstNode.binary_operator_kind(n)
        var is_and false
        if op = KindAmpersandAmpersandToken
            set is_and: true
        if op = KindAmpersandAmpersandEqualsToken
            set is_and: true
        if is_and
            this.bind_condition(AstNode.binary_left_of(n), pre_right_label, false_target)
        else
            this.bind_condition(AstNode.binary_left_of(n), true_target, pre_right_label)
        set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(pre_right_label)
        this.bind(AstNode.binary_operator_token_of(n))
        if Binder.is_logical_or_coalescing_assignment_operator(op)
        {
            this.do_with_conditional_branches_bind(AstNode.binary_right_of(n), true_target, false_target)
            this.bind_assignment_target_flow(AstNode.binary_left_of(n))
            this.add_antecedent(true_target, this.create_flow_condition(FlowFlagsTrueCondition, current_flow, n))
            this.add_antecedent(false_target, this.create_flow_condition(FlowFlagsFalseCondition, current_flow, n))
            return
        }
        this.bind_condition(AstNode.binary_right_of(n), true_target, false_target)
    }

    ; bindDeleteExpressionFlow — `delete a.b` narrows `a.b` back to its declared
    ; type, which is why it records an assignment.
    procedure bind_delete_expression_flow(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.bind_each_child(n)
        let expression AstNode.expression_of(n)
        if AstNode.kind_of(expression) = KindPropertyAccessExpression
            this.bind_assignment_target_flow(expression)
    }

    ; bindConditionalExpressionFlow — the same has_flow_effects bargain the
    ; logical branch makes, over three labels instead of one.
    procedure bind_conditional_expression_flow(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let true_label this.create_branch_label()
        let false_label this.create_branch_label()
        let post_expression_label this.create_branch_label()
        let save_current_flow current_flow
        let save_has_flow_effects has_flow_effects
        set has_flow_effects: false
        this.bind_condition(AstNode.conditional_condition_of(n), true_label, false_label)
        set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(true_label)
        this.bind(AstNode.question_token_of(n))
        this.bind(AstNode.conditional_when_true_of(n))
        this.add_antecedent(post_expression_label, current_flow)
        set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(false_label)
        this.bind(AstNode.colon_token_of(n))
        this.bind(AstNode.conditional_when_false_of(n))
        this.add_antecedent(post_expression_label, current_flow)
        if has_flow_effects
            set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(post_expression_label)
        else
            set current_flow: save_current_flow
        if save_has_flow_effects
            set has_flow_effects: true
    }

    ; bindVariableDeclarationFlow. ★The `for (x of xs)` test is why a declaration
    ; with NO initializer can still be an assignment: the loop assigns to it.
    procedure bind_variable_declaration_flow(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.bind_each_child(n)
        var assigns false
        if AstNode.initializer_of(n) <> null
            set assigns: true
        let grandparent AstNode.parent_node_of(AstNode.parent_node_of(n))
        if grandparent <> null
        {
            let gk AstNode.kind_of(grandparent as ref[AstNode])
            if gk = KindForInStatement
                set assigns: true
            if gk = KindForOfStatement
                set assigns: true
        }
        if assigns
            this.bind_initialized_variable_flow(n)
    }

    ; bindInitializedVariableFlow — a binding PATTERN records one mutation per
    ; leaf, not one for the pattern.
    procedure bind_initialized_variable_flow(this, node: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        if node = null
            return
        let n node as ref[AstNode]
        var name: ref[AstNode]? null
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindVariableDeclaration
            set name: AstNode.name_of(n)
        if k = KindBindingElement
            set name: AstNode.name_of(n)
        if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(name)
        {
            let elements AstNode.elements_of(name)
            let cnt AstNode.list_count(elements)
            var i 0
            while i < cnt
            {
                this.bind_initialized_variable_flow(AstNode.child_in_list(elements, i))
                set i: i + 1
            }
            return
        }
        set current_flow: this.create_flow_mutation(FlowFlagsAssignment, current_flow, n)
    }

    ; bindCallExpressionFlow. Three paths.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE IIFE REORDER IS SLICE 33's SECOND REAL FINDING AND IT STANDS: when
    ; the callee is a function expression or an arrow — `(function f() {})()`,
    ; `(() => {})()` — the reference binds the TYPE ARGUMENTS and the ARGUMENTS
    ; FIRST and the callee LAST, so that the callee's flow node includes the
    ; evaluation of its own arguments. ForEachChild's order is the exact opposite
    ; at the slot that matters, and a function expression declares a symbol, so the
    ; two orders produce different symbol indices whenever an argument declares one.
    ;
    ; ★ SkipParentheses is upstream's, and it is load-bearing: `(function(){})()`
    ; parses with the callee wrapped in a ParenthesizedExpression, which is the
    ; ONLY spelling of an IIFE that does not need a preceding operator.
    procedure bind_call_expression_flow(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if Binder.is_optional_chain(n)
            this.bind_optional_chain_flow(n)
        else
        {
            let callee Parser.skip_parentheses(AstNode.expression_of(n))
            var is_iife false
            if callee <> null
            {
                if AstNode.kind_of(callee) = KindFunctionExpression
                    set is_iife: true
                if AstNode.kind_of(callee) = KindArrowFunction
                    set is_iife: true
            }
            if is_iife
            {
                this.bind_each(AstNode.type_arguments_of(n))
                this.bind_each(AstNode.call_arguments_of(n))
                this.bind(AstNode.expression_of(n))
            }
            else
            {
                this.bind_each_child(n)
                if AstNode.kind_of(AstNode.expression_of(n)) = KindSuperKeyword
                    set current_flow: this.create_flow_call(current_flow, n)
            }
        }

        ; The tail both paths share: `a.push(x)` is an ARRAY MUTATION for the
        ; narrowing engine, which is the whole reason IsPushOrUnshiftIdentifier
        ; exists.
        let callee AstNode.expression_of(n)
        if AstNode.kind_of(callee) <> KindPropertyAccessExpression
            return
        let name AstNode.property_access_name_of(callee as ref[AstNode])
        if AstNode.kind_of(name) <> KindIdentifier
            return
        if Binder.is_narrowable_operand(AstNode.expression_of(callee)) = false
            return
        if Binder.is_push_or_unshift_identifier(name) = false
            return
        set current_flow: this.create_flow_mutation(FlowFlagsArrayMutation, current_flow, n)
    }

    ; bindAccessExpressionFlow / bindNonNullExpressionFlow: one line each
    ; upstream, and the same line.
    procedure bind_access_expression_flow(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if Binder.is_optional_chain(n)
        {
            this.bind_optional_chain_flow(n)
            return
        }
        this.bind_each_child(n)
    }

    procedure bind_non_null_expression_flow(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if Binder.is_optional_chain(n)
        {
            this.bind_optional_chain_flow(n)
            return
        }
        this.bind_each_child(n)
    }

    ; ── the optional chain, as the reference explains it ────────────────────
    ;
    ; `a?.b` is `a && a.b`, so a chain builds the graph a logical expression
    ; builds: descend to the ROOT of the chain, bind that as the first condition,
    ; and bind everything above it as the TRUE branch. Slice 33 ported the
    ; traversal of this and argued it was ForEachChild's order; that is still true,
    ; and what arrives now is the labels it hangs off.
    procedure bind_optional_chain_flow(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if Binder.is_top_level_logical_expression(n)
        {
            let post_expression_label this.create_branch_label()
            let save_current_flow current_flow
            let save_has_flow_effects has_flow_effects
            this.bind_optional_chain(n, post_expression_label, post_expression_label)
            if has_flow_effects
                set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(post_expression_label)
            else
                set current_flow: save_current_flow
            if save_has_flow_effects
                set has_flow_effects: true
            return
        }
        this.bind_optional_chain(n, current_true_target, current_false_target)
    }

    ; ★ The reference does NOT clear has_flow_effects before the chain the way it
    ; does before a logical expression and a conditional — so a chain inherits the
    ; flag from its surroundings and can keep a label it did not earn. Transcribed
    ; as written; it is a difference the flow dump would show if it were an error.
    procedure bind_optional_chain(this, n: ref[AstNode], true_target: ref[FlowNode]?, false_target: ref[FlowNode]?)
    {
        var pre_chain_label: ref[FlowNode]? null
        if Binder.is_optional_chain_root(n)
            set pre_chain_label: this.create_branch_label()
        var expression_true_target true_target
        if pre_chain_label <> null
            set expression_true_target: pre_chain_label
        this.bind_optional_expression(AstNode.expression_of(n), expression_true_target, false_target)
        if pre_chain_label <> null
            set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(pre_chain_label as ref[FlowNode])
        this.do_with_conditional_branches_chain_rest(n, true_target, false_target)
        if Binder.is_outermost_optional_chain(n)
        {
            this.add_antecedent(true_target as ref[FlowNode], this.create_flow_condition(FlowFlagsTrueCondition, current_flow, n))
            this.add_antecedent(false_target as ref[FlowNode], this.create_flow_condition(FlowFlagsFalseCondition, current_flow, n))
        }
    }

    procedure bind_optional_expression(this, node: ref[AstNode]?, true_target: ref[FlowNode]?, false_target: ref[FlowNode]?)
    {
        this.do_with_conditional_branches_bind(node, true_target, false_target)
        var adds true
        if node <> null
        {
            let n node as ref[AstNode]
            if Binder.is_optional_chain(n)
            {
                if Binder.is_outermost_optional_chain(n) = false
                    set adds: false
            }
        }
        if adds
        {
            this.add_antecedent(true_target as ref[FlowNode], this.create_flow_condition(FlowFlagsTrueCondition, current_flow, node))
            this.add_antecedent(false_target as ref[FlowNode], this.create_flow_condition(FlowFlagsFalseCondition, current_flow, node))
        }
    }

    ; bindOptionalChainRest. A NonNullExpression has no arm — its only child IS
    ; the expression bound above — and the absence is upstream's.
    procedure bind_optional_chain_rest(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindPropertyAccessExpression
        {
            this.bind(AstNode.question_dot_token_of(n))
            this.bind(AstNode.property_access_name_of(n))
            return
        }
        if k = KindElementAccessExpression
        {
            this.bind(AstNode.question_dot_token_of(n))
            this.bind(AstNode.element_access_argument_of(n))
            return
        }
        if k = KindCallExpression
        {
            this.bind(AstNode.question_dot_token_of(n))
            this.bind_each(AstNode.type_arguments_of(n))
            this.bind_each(AstNode.call_arguments_of(n))
        }
    }

    ; bindBindingElementFlow / bindParameterFlow — the two REORDERS slices 35 and
    ; 36 already carry, now routed through bindInitializer for the initializer
    ; slot.
    procedure bind_binding_element_flow(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.bind(AstNode.dot_dot_dot_token_of(n))
        this.bind(AstNode.property_name_of(n))
        this.bind_initializer(AstNode.initializer_of(n))
        this.bind(AstNode.name_of(n))
    }

    procedure bind_parameter_flow(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.bind_each(AstNode.modifiers_of(n))
        this.bind(AstNode.dot_dot_dot_token_of(n))
        this.bind(AstNode.parameter_question_token_of(n))
        this.bind(AstNode.type_of(n))
        this.bind_initializer(AstNode.initializer_of(n))
        this.bind(AstNode.name_of(n))
    }

    ; bindInitializer — "a BindingElement/Parameter does not have side effects if
    ; initializers are not evaluated and used" (GH#49759). ★The join is what says
    ; that: the flow AFTER a default initializer is the merge of *the initializer
    ; ran* and *it did not*, so a mutation inside it does not narrow the parameter
    ; for the body.
    procedure bind_initializer(this, node: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        if node = null
            return
        let entry_flow current_flow
        this.bind(node)
        if Binder.flow_is_unreachable(entry_flow)
            return
        if entry_flow = current_flow
            return
        let exit_flow this.create_branch_label()
        this.add_antecedent(exit_flow, entry_flow)
        this.add_antecedent(exit_flow, current_flow)
        set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(exit_flow)
    }

    ; checkStrictModeBinaryExpression. The one DIAGNOSTIC of the binary arm:
    ; `eval = 1` and `arguments = 1` are errors, because Annex C forbids either
    ; name as the target of an assignment.
    ;
    ; ★ IsLeftHandSideExpression is `isLeftHandSideExpressionKind(
    ; SkipPartiallyEmittedExpressions(node).Kind)`, and the skip is not ported:
    ; a PartiallyEmittedExpression is a TRANSFORM node, created by the emitter's
    ; factory and never by a parse, so the walk it heads cannot reach a bind.
    procedure check_strict_mode_binary_expression(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let left AstNode.binary_left_of(n)
        if left = null
            return
        if Parser.is_left_hand_side_expression_kind(AstNode.kind_of(left)) = false
            return
        if Parser.is_assignment_operator(AstNode.binary_operator_kind(n)) = false
            return
        this.check_strict_mode_eval_or_arguments(n, left)
    }

    ; ── the ast predicates the arms above need ──────────────────────────────

    ; ast.IsDestructuringAssignment: an assignment with `=` exactly — a compound
    ; assignment is excluded — whose target is an object or array LITERAL. The
    ; literal is what makes it a pattern; `a = b` is not one.
    function is_destructuring_assignment(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) <> KindBinaryExpression
            return false
        if AstNode.binary_operator_kind(n) <> KindEqualsToken
            return false
        let left AstNode.binary_left_of(n)
        if left = null
            return false
        if Parser.is_left_hand_side_expression_kind(AstNode.kind_of(left)) = false
            return false
        let lk AstNode.kind_of(left)
        if lk = KindObjectLiteralExpression
            return true
        lk = KindArrayLiteralExpression
    }

    ; ast.IsLogicalOrCoalescingBinaryOperator and
    ; ast.IsLogicalOrCoalescingAssignmentOperator. Two functions rather than one
    ; five-armed test because the reference has two and their other callers do not
    ; overlap.
    ; ast.IsLogicalBinaryOperator — slice 88 splits it back out of the function
    ; below, because the reference has THREE here and not two, and the third one's
    ; caller arrived with the binary expression: checkBinaryLikeExpression's
    ; pre-pass asks the COALESCING question to decide whether to walk up the
    ; parents at all and the LOGICAL one to decide whether to check truthiness, and
    ; `??` answers the two differently.
    function is_logical_binary_operator(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindBarBarToken
            return true
        k = KindAmpersandAmpersandToken
    }

    function is_logical_or_coalescing_binary_operator(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if is_logical_binary_operator(k)
            return true
        k = KindQuestionQuestionToken
    }

    function is_logical_or_coalescing_assignment_operator(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindAmpersandAmpersandEqualsToken
            return true
        if k = KindBarBarEqualsToken
            return true
        k = KindQuestionQuestionEqualsToken
    }

    ; ast.IsOptionalChain: the FLAG plus the four kinds that may carry it. The
    ; kind test is not redundant — NodeFlagsOptionalChain shares its bit with
    ; ContainsThis and NestedNamespace (NodeFlags.scaly says so at the bit), so on
    ; any other kind the same bit means something else entirely.
    function is_optional_chain(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        if (AstNode.flags_of(n) & NodeFlagsOptionalChain) = 0
            return false
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindPropertyAccessExpression
            return true
        if k = KindElementAccessExpression
            return true
        if k = KindCallExpression
            return true
        k = KindNonNullExpression
    }

    ; ── the NARROWING family (slice 92) ──────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ SEVEN MUTUALLY RECURSIVE PREDICATES, AND THEIR JOB IS TO KEEP THE GRAPH
    ; SMALL. createFlowCondition builds nothing for a condition that narrows
    ; nothing, and bindAssignmentTargetFlow records no mutation for a target
    ; nothing can refer to — so between them these decide the SIZE of every flow
    ; graph in the corpus, and getting one of them too permissive costs nodes
    ; rather than answers. Read off binder.go's own block, function for function.

    ; isNarrowingExpression.
    function is_narrowing_expression(e: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if e = null
            return false
        let n e as ref[AstNode]
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindIdentifier
            return true
        if k = KindThisKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindPropertyAccessExpression
            return Binder.contains_narrowable_reference(n)
        if k = KindElementAccessExpression
            return Binder.contains_narrowable_reference(n)
        if k = KindCallExpression
            return Binder.has_narrowable_argument(n)
        if k = KindParenthesizedExpression
            return Binder.is_narrowing_expression(AstNode.expression_of(n))
        if k = KindNonNullExpression
            return Binder.is_narrowing_expression(AstNode.expression_of(n))
        if k = KindTypeOfExpression
            return Binder.is_narrowing_expression(AstNode.expression_of(n))
        if k = KindBinaryExpression
            return Binder.is_narrowing_binary_expression(n)
        if k = KindPrefixUnaryExpression
        {
            if AstNode.prefix_unary_operator_kind(n) <> KindExclamationToken
                return false
            return Binder.is_narrowing_expression(AstNode.prefix_unary_operand_of(n))
        }
        false
    }

    ; containsNarrowableReference — the reference itself, or the reference at the
    ; ROOT of an optional chain. ★The chain arm is gated on the FLAG rather than
    ; on is_optional_chain, which is upstream's own spelling here and differs from
    ; it: the four kinds are re-tested by the switch that follows.
    function contains_narrowable_reference(e: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if e = null
            return false
        let n e as ref[AstNode]
        if Binder.is_narrowable_reference(n)
            return true
        if (AstNode.flags_of(n) & NodeFlagsOptionalChain) = 0
            return false
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        var chained false
        if k = KindPropertyAccessExpression
            set chained: true
        if k = KindElementAccessExpression
            set chained: true
        if k = KindCallExpression
            set chained: true
        if k = KindNonNullExpression
            set chained: true
        if chained
            return Binder.contains_narrowable_reference(AstNode.expression_of(n))
        false
    }

    ; isNarrowableReference.
    function is_narrowable_reference(node: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if node = null
            return false
        let n node as ref[AstNode]
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindIdentifier
            return true
        if k = KindThisKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindSuperKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindMetaProperty
            return true
        if k = KindPropertyAccessExpression
            return Binder.is_narrowable_reference(AstNode.expression_of(n))
        if k = KindParenthesizedExpression
            return Binder.is_narrowable_reference(AstNode.expression_of(n))
        if k = KindNonNullExpression
            return Binder.is_narrowable_reference(AstNode.expression_of(n))
        if k = KindElementAccessExpression
        {
            let arg AstNode.element_access_argument_of(n)
            if Parser.is_string_or_numeric_literal_like(arg)
                return true
            if Binder.is_entity_name_expression(arg)
                return Binder.is_narrowable_reference(AstNode.expression_of(n))
            return false
        }
        if k = KindBinaryExpression
        {
            let op AstNode.binary_operator_kind(n)
            if op = KindCommaToken
                return Binder.is_narrowable_reference(AstNode.binary_right_of(n))
            if Parser.is_assignment_operator(op)
                return Binder.is_left_hand_side_expression(AstNode.binary_left_of(n))
            return false
        }
        false
    }

    ; hasNarrowableArgument.
    function has_narrowable_argument(e: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let args AstNode.call_arguments_of(e)
        let n AstNode.list_count(args)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            if Binder.contains_narrowable_reference(AstNode.child_in_list(args, i))
                return true
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let callee AstNode.expression_of(e)
        if callee = null
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(callee as ref[AstNode]) <> KindPropertyAccessExpression
            return false
        Binder.contains_narrowable_reference(AstNode.expression_of(callee))
    }

    ; isNarrowingBinaryExpression. ★The equality arm is FIVE disjuncts and the
    ; last of them is a pair — a boolean literal on either side with a narrowing
    ; expression on the other — which is what makes `x === true` narrow `x`.
    function is_narrowing_binary_expression(e: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let op AstNode.binary_operator_kind(e)
        var is_assign false
        if op = KindEqualsToken
            set is_assign: true
        if op = KindBarBarEqualsToken
            set is_assign: true
        if op = KindAmpersandAmpersandEqualsToken
            set is_assign: true
        if op = KindQuestionQuestionEqualsToken
            set is_assign: true
        if is_assign
            return Binder.contains_narrowable_reference(AstNode.binary_left_of(e))
        var is_equality false
        if op = KindEqualsEqualsToken
            set is_equality: true
        if op = KindExclamationEqualsToken
            set is_equality: true
        if op = KindEqualsEqualsEqualsToken
            set is_equality: true
        if op = KindExclamationEqualsEqualsToken
            set is_equality: true
        if is_equality
        {
            let left Parser.skip_parentheses(AstNode.binary_left_of(e))
            let right Parser.skip_parentheses(AstNode.binary_right_of(e))
            if Binder.is_narrowable_operand(left)
                return true
            if Binder.is_narrowable_operand(right)
                return true
            if Binder.is_narrowing_type_of_operands(right, left)
                return true
            if Binder.is_narrowing_type_of_operands(left, right)
                return true
            if Binder.is_boolean_literal(right)
            {
                if Binder.is_narrowing_expression(left)
                    return true
            }
            if Binder.is_boolean_literal(left)
            {
                if Binder.is_narrowing_expression(right)
                    return true
            }
            return false
        }
        if op = KindInstanceOfKeyword
            return Binder.is_narrowable_operand(AstNode.binary_left_of(e))
        if op = KindInKeyword
            return Binder.is_narrowing_expression(AstNode.binary_right_of(e))
        if op = KindCommaToken
            return Binder.is_narrowing_expression(AstNode.binary_right_of(e))
        false
    }

    ; isNarrowableOperand. ★Its two special arms FALL THROUGH rather than answer:
    ; a binary expression whose operator is neither `=` nor `,` still reaches
    ; containsNarrowableReference at the bottom, which is upstream's own control
    ; flow (the inner switch has no default) and not a transcription slip.
    function is_narrowable_operand(e: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if e = null
            return false
        let n e as ref[AstNode]
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindParenthesizedExpression
            return Binder.is_narrowable_operand(AstNode.expression_of(n))
        if k = KindBinaryExpression
        {
            let op AstNode.binary_operator_kind(n)
            if op = KindEqualsToken
                return Binder.is_narrowable_operand(AstNode.binary_left_of(n))
            if op = KindCommaToken
                return Binder.is_narrowable_operand(AstNode.binary_right_of(n))
        }
        Binder.contains_narrowable_reference(n)
    }

    ; isNarrowingTypeOfOperands — `typeof x === "string"`, in that order.
    function is_narrowing_type_of_operands(e1: ref[AstNode]?, e2: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if e1 = null
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(e1 as ref[AstNode]) <> KindTypeOfExpression
            return false
        if Binder.is_narrowable_operand(AstNode.expression_of(e1)) = false
            return false
        Parser.is_string_literal_like(e2)
    }

    ; ── the small ast predicates the flow half needs ─────────────────────────

    function is_boolean_literal(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n as ref[AstNode])
        if k = KindTrueKeyword
            return true
        k = KindFalseKeyword
    }

    ; ast.IsLeftHandSideExpression. ★The reference runs
    ; SkipPartiallyEmittedExpressions first and this port does not, because a
    ; PartiallyEmittedExpression is made by the TRANSFORMERS and never by the
    ; parser: over both corpora there is no node of that kind for the skip to
    ; step over. Stated rather than dropped, so a pin bump that starts producing
    ; one has a line to fail against.
    function is_left_hand_side_expression(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        Parser.is_left_hand_side_expression_kind(AstNode.kind_of(n as ref[AstNode]))
    }

    ; ast.IsOptionalChainRoot — the node that CARRIES the `?.`, which is every
    ; optional chain except a non-null assertion (`a?.b!` is in the chain but is
    ; not its root) and except a link that inherited the flag from its parent.
    function is_optional_chain_root(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if Binder.is_optional_chain(n) = false
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(n as ref[AstNode]) = KindNonNullExpression
            return false
        AstNode.question_dot_token_of(n) <> null
    }

    ; ast.IsOutermostOptionalChain — the three cases the reference enumerates in
    ; its own comment, as three disjuncts.
    function is_outermost_optional_chain(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
        if Binder.is_optional_chain(parent) = false
            return true
        if Binder.is_optional_chain_root(parent)
            return true
        AstNode.expression_of(parent) <> n
    }

    ; ast.IsExpressionOfOptionalChainRoot — `a` in `a?.b`.
    function is_expression_of_optional_chain_root(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
        if Binder.is_optional_chain_root(parent) = false
            return false
        AstNode.expression_of(parent) = n
    }

    function is_nullish_coalesce(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        let node n as ref[AstNode]
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) <> KindBinaryExpression
            return false
        AstNode.binary_operator_kind(node) = KindQuestionQuestionToken
    }

    ; ast.IsLogicalExpression — a `&&`/`||`/`??` under any number of parentheses
    ; and `!`s. ★It is a LOOP and not a recursion upstream, and the loop is the
    ; point: the answer is about the expression the parentheses wrap, so peeling
    ; is not the same as asking the operand.
    function is_logical_expression(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        var cur: ref[AstNode]? n
        while cur <> null
        {
            let node cur as ref[AstNode]
            let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
            if k = KindParenthesizedExpression
            {
                set cur: AstNode.expression_of(node)
                continue
            }
            if k = KindPrefixUnaryExpression
            {
                if AstNode.prefix_unary_operator_kind(node) = KindExclamationToken
                {
                    set cur: AstNode.prefix_unary_operand_of(node)
                    continue
                }
            }
            if k <> KindBinaryExpression
                return false
            return Binder.is_logical_or_coalescing_binary_operator(AstNode.binary_operator_kind(node))
        }
        false
    }

    ; binder.isLogicalAssignmentExpression — `&&=`, `||=`, `??=` under parentheses.
    function is_logical_assignment_expression(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        let e Parser.skip_parentheses(n)
        if e = null
            return false
        let node e as ref[AstNode]
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) <> KindBinaryExpression
            return false
        Binder.is_logical_or_coalescing_assignment_operator(AstNode.binary_operator_kind(node))
    }

    ; binder.isStatementCondition — is this node the CONDITION slot of the
    ; statement (or ternary) above it. Four parents, and each asks a different
    ; slot, which is why it cannot be a kind test.
    function is_statement_condition(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
        if parent = null
            return false
        let p parent as ref[AstNode]
        let pk AstNode.kind_of(p)
        var expression_slot false
        if pk = KindIfStatement
            set expression_slot: true
        if pk = KindWhileStatement
            set expression_slot: true
        if pk = KindDoStatement
            set expression_slot: true
        if expression_slot
            return AstNode.expression_of(p) = n
        if pk = KindForStatement
            return AstNode.for_condition_of(p) = n
        if pk = KindConditionalExpression
            return AstNode.conditional_condition_of(p) = n
        false
    }

    ; binder.isTopLevelLogicalExpression — climb out of parentheses and `!`s, then
    ; ask whether what is left is being used AS a condition. A top-level logical
    ; expression is the one that has to build its own junction label; everything
    ; else inherits its caller's true/false targets.
    function is_top_level_logical_expression(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        var node n
        while true
        {
            let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
            if parent = null
                break
            let p parent as ref[AstNode]
            let pk AstNode.kind_of(p)
            var climbs false
            if pk = KindParenthesizedExpression
                set climbs: true
            if pk = KindPrefixUnaryExpression
            {
                if AstNode.prefix_unary_operator_kind(p) = KindExclamationToken
                    set climbs: true
            }
            if climbs = false
                break
            set node: p
        }
        if Binder.is_statement_condition(node)
            return false
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        if Binder.is_logical_expression(parent)
            return false
        if Binder.is_optional_chain(parent)
        {
            if AstNode.expression_of(parent) = node
                return false
        }
        true
    }

    ; ast.IsDottedName — `a`, `a.b`, `this.a`, `(a).b`. Its reader is
    ; maybeBindExpressionFlowIfCall, which admits a call into the flow graph only
    ; when the callee is one of these: a dotted name is what an assertion
    ; signature can be looked up by.
    function is_dotted_name(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        let node n as ref[AstNode]
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        if k = KindIdentifier
            return true
        if k = KindThisKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindSuperKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindMetaProperty
            return true
        if k = KindPropertyAccessExpression
            return Binder.is_dotted_name(AstNode.expression_of(node))
        if k = KindParenthesizedExpression
            return Binder.is_dotted_name(AstNode.expression_of(node))
        false
    }

    ; ast.IsPushOrUnshiftIdentifier — the two Array methods that MUTATE, which is
    ; the whole content of the FlowFlagsArrayMutation arm.
    function is_push_or_unshift_identifier(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.text_is(n, "push")
            return true
        AstNode.text_is(n, "unshift")
    }

    ; ast.IsAssignmentTarget.
    function is_assignment_target(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
        Binder.get_assignment_target(n) <> null

    ; ast.IsPartOfTypeQuery — climb through the dotted name and ask whether what
    ; is above it is a `typeof X`. ★It is the QualifiedName slot's whole guard:
    ; `A.B` in a type query is a value reference the checker narrows, and `A.B`
    ; anywhere else in a type is not.
    function is_part_of_type_query(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        var cur: ref[AstNode]? n
        while cur <> null
        {
            let k AstNode.kind_of(cur as ref[AstNode])
            if k = KindQualifiedName
            {
                set cur: AstNode.parent_node_of(cur)
                continue
            }
            if k = KindIdentifier
            {
                set cur: AstNode.parent_node_of(cur)
                continue
            }
            return k = KindTypeQuery
        }
        false
    }

    ; binder.isGeneratorFunctionExpression — a `function*` expression, which is
    ; NOT treated as an IIFE even when it is immediately invoked.
    function is_generator_function_expression(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) <> KindFunctionExpression
            return false
        AstNode.asterisk_token_of(n) <> null
    }

    ; ast.NodeIsPresent — the reference's own double negative, kept as one so that
    ; the reader who greps for either name finds it.
    function node_is_present(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
        Binder.node_is_missing(n) = false

    ; ast.IsPotentiallyExecutableNode — what counts as executable for the
    ; UNREACHABLE marker. ★The variable-statement arm is the interesting one: a
    ; `var` list with no initializers is NOT executable (hoisting makes it
    ; harmless past a `return`), while a block-scoped one always is.
    function is_potentially_executable_node(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k >= KindFirstStatement
        {
            if k <= KindLastStatement
            {
                if k <> KindVariableStatement
                    return true
                let list AstNode.variable_statement_list_of(n)
                if (Binder.combined_node_flags(list as ref[AstNode]) & NodeFlagsBlockScoped) <> 0
                    return true
                let decls AstNode.variable_declarations_of(list)
                let cnt AstNode.list_count(decls)
                var i 0
                while i < cnt
                {
                    if AstNode.initializer_of(AstNode.child_in_list(decls, i)) <> null
                        return true
                    set i: i + 1
                }
                return false
            }
        }
        if k = KindClassDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindEnumDeclaration
            return true
        k = KindModuleDeclaration
    }

    ; ── containers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; GetContainerFlags, whole. It is ported in full although this slice binds only
    ; a few kinds, because the flags decide the TABLE a declaration lands in and a
    ; narrowed version would answer None for a container this slice does bind
    ; through — the §3.5br class, where an accessor narrowed to its callers costs
    ; more the second time.
    ;
    ; ★ The Block arm reads node.PARENT, which is why Parent had to be ported with
    ; this slice: a block parented by a function-like node is not a scope of its
    ; own (the function already is one), and any other block is.
    function get_container_flags(n: ref[AstNode]) returns int
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindClassExpression
            return ContainerFlagsIsContainer
        if k = KindClassDeclaration
            return ContainerFlagsIsContainer
        if k = KindEnumDeclaration
            return ContainerFlagsIsContainer
        if k = KindObjectLiteralExpression
            return ContainerFlagsIsContainer
        if k = KindTypeLiteral
            return ContainerFlagsIsContainer
        if k = KindJsxAttributes
            return ContainerFlagsIsContainer
        if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            return ContainerFlagsIsContainer | ContainerFlagsIsInterface
        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
            return ContainerFlagsIsContainer | ContainerFlagsHasLocals
        if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
            return ContainerFlagsIsContainer | ContainerFlagsHasLocals
        if k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
            return ContainerFlagsIsContainer | ContainerFlagsHasLocals
        if k = KindMappedType
            return ContainerFlagsIsContainer | ContainerFlagsHasLocals
        if k = KindIndexSignature
            return ContainerFlagsIsContainer | ContainerFlagsHasLocals
        if k = KindSourceFile
            return ContainerFlagsIsContainer | ContainerFlagsIsControlFlowContainer | ContainerFlagsHasLocals

        ; The three method-ish kinds, whose flags depend on where the method sits:
        ; a method of an object literal or of a class EXPRESSION carries two extra
        ; bits, and the reference reaches the shared arm by falling through.
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            return Binder.method_container_flags(n)
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            return Binder.method_container_flags(n)
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            return Binder.method_container_flags(n)

        if k = KindConstructor
            return Binder.function_like_container_flags()
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return Binder.function_like_container_flags()
        if k = KindClassStaticBlockDeclaration
            return Binder.function_like_container_flags()

        if k = KindMethodSignature
            return Binder.signature_container_flags()
        if k = KindCallSignature
            return Binder.signature_container_flags()
        if k = KindFunctionType
            return Binder.signature_container_flags()
        if k = KindConstructSignature
            return Binder.signature_container_flags()
        if k = KindConstructorType
            return Binder.signature_container_flags()

        if k = KindFunctionExpression
            return ContainerFlagsIsContainer | ContainerFlagsIsControlFlowContainer | ContainerFlagsHasLocals | ContainerFlagsIsFunctionLike | ContainerFlagsIsFunctionExpression | ContainerFlagsIsThisContainer
        if k = KindArrowFunction
            return ContainerFlagsIsContainer | ContainerFlagsIsControlFlowContainer | ContainerFlagsHasLocals | ContainerFlagsIsFunctionLike | ContainerFlagsIsFunctionExpression | ContainerFlagsPropagatesThisKeyword
        if k = KindModuleBlock
            return ContainerFlagsIsControlFlowContainer

        ; A property declaration is a control-flow container only when it has an
        ; initializer — there is nothing to flow through otherwise.
        if k = KindPropertyDeclaration
        {
            if AstNode.initializer_of(n) <> null
                return ContainerFlagsIsControlFlowContainer | ContainerFlagsIsThisContainer
            return ContainerFlagsNone
        }

        if k = KindCatchClause
            return ContainerFlagsIsBlockScopedContainer | ContainerFlagsHasLocals
        if k = KindForStatement
            return ContainerFlagsIsBlockScopedContainer | ContainerFlagsHasLocals
        if k = KindForInStatement
            return ContainerFlagsIsBlockScopedContainer | ContainerFlagsHasLocals
        if k = KindForOfStatement
            return ContainerFlagsIsBlockScopedContainer | ContainerFlagsHasLocals
        if k = KindCaseBlock
            return ContainerFlagsIsBlockScopedContainer | ContainerFlagsHasLocals

        ; ★ THE PARENT IS THE QUESTION HERE. A block whose parent is function-like
        ; (or a class static block) is not a scope: its function already is one, and
        ; giving the block locals of its own would put a `var` in the wrong table.
        if k = KindBlock
        {
            let p AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
            if p <> null
            {
                if AstNode.is_function_like(p)
                    return ContainerFlagsNone
                if AstNode.kind_of(p) = KindClassStaticBlockDeclaration
                    return ContainerFlagsNone
            }
            return ContainerFlagsIsBlockScopedContainer | ContainerFlagsHasLocals
        }

        ContainerFlagsNone
    }

    function function_like_container_flags() returns int
        ContainerFlagsIsContainer | ContainerFlagsIsControlFlowContainer | ContainerFlagsHasLocals | ContainerFlagsIsFunctionLike | ContainerFlagsIsThisContainer

    function signature_container_flags() returns int
        ContainerFlagsIsContainer | ContainerFlagsIsControlFlowContainer | ContainerFlagsHasLocals | ContainerFlagsIsFunctionLike | ContainerFlagsPropagatesThisKeyword

    function method_container_flags(n: ref[AstNode]) returns int
    {
        if Binder.is_object_literal_or_class_expression_method_or_accessor(n)
            return ContainerFlagsIsContainer | ContainerFlagsIsControlFlowContainer | ContainerFlagsHasLocals | ContainerFlagsIsFunctionLike | ContainerFlagsIsObjectLiteralOrClassExpressionMethodOrAccessor | ContainerFlagsIsThisContainer
        Binder.function_like_container_flags()
    }

    ; ast.IsObjectLiteralOrClassExpressionMethodOrAccessor: a method or accessor
    ; whose PARENT is an object literal or a class expression.
    function is_object_literal_or_class_expression_method_or_accessor(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        var is_member false
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            set is_member: true
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            set is_member: true
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            set is_member: true
        if is_member = false
            return false
        let p AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
        if p = null
            return false
        let pk AstNode.kind_of(p)
        if pk = KindObjectLiteralExpression
            return true
        if pk = KindClassExpression
            return true
        false
    }

    ; bindContainer, minus the flow half. What remains is the three registers, the
    ; container chain and the recursion — and the SAVE/RESTORE around it, which is
    ; the whole reason a container is a container.
    procedure bind_container(this, n: ref[AstNode], container_flags: int)
    {
        let save_container container
        let save_this_container this_container
        let saved_block_scope_container block_scope_container

        if (container_flags & ContainerFlagsIsContainer) <> 0
        {
            set container: n
            set block_scope_container: n
            if (container_flags & ContainerFlagsHasLocals) <> 0
                this.add_to_container_chain(n)
        }
        else
        {
            if (container_flags & ContainerFlagsIsBlockScopedContainer) <> 0
            {
                set block_scope_container: n
                this.add_to_container_chain(n)
            }
        }
        if (container_flags & ContainerFlagsIsThisContainer) <> 0
            set this_container: n

        ; ★ THE LOCALS TABLE IS NOT CREATED HERE, and that is the reference's own
        ; arrangement rather than an omission: upstream's eager creation is
        ; commented out and every reader goes through ast.GetLocals, which makes the
        ; table on first use. It is observable — 754 of the 12 444 stage-2 files
        ; have no locals table at all — so it is ported as laziness.
        ; ── bindContainer's THREE-WAY SPLIT, complete as of slice 92 ─────────
        ;
        ; ★★★ THE CONTROL-FLOW ARM IS THE ONE THIS FILE'S HEADER DEFERRED, and it
        ; is where a function gets a flow graph of its own. Everything in it is
        ; save / reset / walk / read / restore, and the READ in the middle is the
        ; slice's diagnostic product: a function-like whose end is REACHABLE and
        ; whose body is present carries NodeFlagsHasImplicitReturn, plus
        ; NodeFlagsHasExplicitReturn when a `return` was seen anywhere under it.
        ; The checker reads that pair for TS2378 and TS2366 without ever touching
        ; the flow analyzer.
        if (container_flags & ContainerFlagsIsControlFlowContainer) <> 0
        {
            let save_current_flow current_flow
            let save_break_target current_break_target
            let save_continue_target current_continue_target
            let save_return_target current_return_target
            let save_exception_target current_exception_target
            let save_active_label_list active_label_list
            let save_has_explicit_return has_explicit_return
            let save_seen_this seen_this_keyword

            ; ★★ AN IIFE IS PART OF THE CONTAINING CONTROL FLOW, which is why it
            ; gets no Start node: `(function () { … })()` runs where it is
            ; written, so a `return` in it behaves like a break to a label just
            ; past the body. A GENERATOR or an ASYNC function expression is
            ; excluded — neither runs to completion at the call — and a class
            ; STATIC BLOCK is included for the same reason a plain IIFE is.
            var is_immediately_invoked false
            if (container_flags & ContainerFlagsIsFunctionExpression) <> 0
            {
                if this.has_syntactic_modifier(n, ModifierFlagsAsync) = false
                {
                    if Binder.is_generator_function_expression(n) = false
                    {
                        if AstNode.get_immediately_invoked_function_expression(n) <> null
                            set is_immediately_invoked: true
                    }
                }
            }
            if AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindClassStaticBlockDeclaration
                set is_immediately_invoked: true

            if is_immediately_invoked = false
            {
                let flow_start this.new_flow_node(FlowFlagsStart)
                set current_flow: flow_start
                ; ★ The Start node carries its NODE only for the two container
                ; shapes whose flow the checker has to look BACK into — a function
                ; expression and an object-literal/class-expression method — and
                ; for nothing else. That asymmetry is visible in the flow dump's
                ; `N` section as a Start with a node against a Start without one.
                var carries_node false
                if (container_flags & ContainerFlagsIsFunctionExpression) <> 0
                    set carries_node: true
                if (container_flags & ContainerFlagsIsObjectLiteralOrClassExpressionMethodOrAccessor) <> 0
                    set carries_node: true
                if carries_node
                    set flow_start.node: n
            }

            ; A return control flow graph is built for an IIFE and for a
            ; CONSTRUCTOR — the constructor's is what strict property
            ; initialization reads.
            var wants_return_target is_immediately_invoked
            if AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindConstructor
                set wants_return_target: true
            if wants_return_target
                set current_return_target: this.new_flow_node(FlowFlagsBranchLabel)
            else
                set current_return_target: null
            set current_exception_target: null
            set current_break_target: null
            set current_continue_target: null
            set active_label_list: null
            set has_explicit_return: false
            set seen_this_keyword: false

            this.bind_children(n)

            ; "Reset flags (for incremental scenarios)" — upstream's own comment.
            ; ★ NodeFlagsHasAsyncFunctions is in the mask the reference clears and
            ; this port never SETS it: `b.emitFlags` has three writers upstream and
            ; one reader, the source file's own flags word, which no dump here can
            ; see (this file's header says so of the same bit from the other end).
            ; The mask is written whole anyway, because clearing a bit nobody sets
            ; is free and a narrowed mask would have to be re-decided the day the
            ; bit arrives.
            ; ★ Scaly has no bitwise NOT — the eleven operator characters do not
            ; include one and `xor` is the word for XOR — so a complement is
            ; `-1 - mask`, which is what `^mask` means in two's complement. The
            ; interface arm two branches down clears ONE bit and can use the
            ; guard-plus-xor idiom; a four-bit mask cannot.
            let clear_mask NodeFlagsReachabilityAndEmitFlags | NodeFlagsContainsThis
            set n.flags: AstNode.flags_of(n) & (0 - 1 - clear_mask)

            if Binder.flow_is_unreachable(current_flow) = false
            {
                if (container_flags & ContainerFlagsIsFunctionLike) <> 0
                {
                    let body AstNode.body_of(n)
                    if Binder.node_is_present(body)
                    {
                        set n.flags: AstNode.flags_of(n) | NodeFlagsHasImplicitReturn
                        if has_explicit_return
                            set n.flags: AstNode.flags_of(n) | NodeFlagsHasExplicitReturn
                        set n.end_flow_node: current_flow
                    }
                }
            }
            if seen_this_keyword
                set n.flags: AstNode.flags_of(n) | NodeFlagsContainsThis
            if AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindSourceFile
                set n.end_flow_node: current_flow

            if current_return_target <> null
            {
                let rt current_return_target as ref[FlowNode]
                this.add_antecedent(rt, current_flow)
                set current_flow: this.finish_flow_label(rt)
                ; setReturnFlowNode's switch has four arms and bindContainer
                ; reaches exactly TWO of them; the other two (a function
                ; declaration, a function expression) have no caller anywhere in
                ; the reference at this pin, so the slot they would fill is
                ; printed as -1 on every unit of both corpora.
                var wants_return_slot false
                if AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindConstructor
                    set wants_return_slot: true
                if AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindClassStaticBlockDeclaration
                    set wants_return_slot: true
                if wants_return_slot
                    set n.return_flow_node: current_flow
            }

            if is_immediately_invoked = false
                set current_flow: save_current_flow
            set current_break_target: save_break_target
            set current_continue_target: save_continue_target
            set current_return_target: save_return_target
            set current_exception_target: save_exception_target
            set active_label_list: save_active_label_list
            set has_explicit_return: save_has_explicit_return
            ; ★ PropagatesThisKeyword is an OR and not a restore: an arrow
            ; function's `this` belongs to the container above it, so the flag
            ; travels outward through exactly the containers that do not bind
            ; their own `this`.
            if (container_flags & ContainerFlagsPropagatesThisKeyword) <> 0
            {
                if save_seen_this
                    set seen_this_keyword: true
            }
            else
                set seen_this_keyword: save_seen_this
        }
        else
            if (container_flags & ContainerFlagsIsInterface) <> 0
            {
                let save_seen_this seen_this_keyword
                set seen_this_keyword: false
                this.bind_children(n)
                ; The reference CLEARS the flag when nothing was seen rather than
                ; leaving it — it shares its bit with IdentifierHasExtendedUnicodeEscape
                ; (NodeFlags.scaly says so), and an interface is not an identifier, so
                ; the clear is about re-binding rather than about overlap. Both
                ; directions are written.
                if seen_this_keyword
                    set n.flags: AstNode.flags_of(n) | NodeFlagsContainsThis
                else
                {
                    if (AstNode.flags_of(n) & NodeFlagsContainsThis) <> 0
                        set n.flags: AstNode.flags_of(n) xor NodeFlagsContainsThis
                }
                set seen_this_keyword: save_seen_this
            }
        else
            this.bind_children(n)

        ; ★★ THE TAIL, and slice 30 owns it because slice 30 is what makes it
        ; reachable — which is the §3.5p note the paragraph this replaces ended on,
        ; coming due. All THREE of its parts are ported as of slice 37:
        ;
        ; (a) A JavaScript file's top-level `@typedef` aliases, DEFERRED to here so
        ;     that the CommonJS module indicators are known first. That deferral is
        ;     why bind()'s JSTypeAliasDeclaration arm binds only the ones that are
        ;     NOT top level: either half alone is a typedef that declares nothing.
        ; (b) `declareCommonJSVariable("module")` / `("exports")`, which is what
        ;     the deferral in (a) is FOR — the indicator is set by an arm somewhere
        ;     in the walk, and both lines read it after the walk is over.
        ; (c) bindCommonJSTypeExports for an external or ambient module.
        ;
        ; ★★★ (b) was the paragraph this replaces, and it named its own condition:
        ; "the slice that ports an expando arm owns both lines". Slice 37 ports
        ; three of the five assignment kinds plus the `require` call, so
        ; file.CommonJSModuleIndicator is written now and the two variables are
        ; declared — and `IsExternalOrCommonJSModule` in (c) stops being
        ; `is_external_module` at the same moment, which is why the two changes are
        ; one edit.
        ;
        ; ★ Neither part returns early on `unported`: the three registers are
        ; restored below, and a return would leave the binder's container pointing
        ; at a child. The flag is sticky, so guarding the WORK is enough.
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindSourceFile
        {
            if (AstNode.flags_of(n) & NodeFlagsJavaScriptFile) <> 0
            {
                let sts AstNode.statements_of(n)
                if sts <> null
                {
                    let len sts.get_length() as int
                    var i 0
                    while i < len
                    {
                        if this.is_unported() = false
                        {
                            let st sts[i]
                            if AstNode.kind_of(st) = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
                                this.bind_block_scoped_declaration(st, SymbolFlagsTypeAlias, SymbolFlagsTypeAliasExcludes)
                        }
                        set i: i + 1
                    }
                }
                ; The two CommonJS variables. `module` and `exports` are declared
                ; into the FILE's locals as function-scoped variables, once each,
                ; and only when something in the walk made the file a CommonJS
                ; module.
                if common_js_module_indicator <> null
                {
                    if this.is_unported() = false
                    {
                        this.declare_common_js_variable("module")
                        this.declare_common_js_variable("exports")
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        var folds_type_exports false
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindSourceFile
        {
            if this.is_external_or_common_js_module()
                set folds_type_exports: true
        }
        if Binder.is_ambient_module(n)
            set folds_type_exports: true
        if folds_type_exports
        {
            if this.is_unported() = false
                this.bind_common_js_type_exports(AstNode.symbol_of(n))
        }

        set container: save_container
        set this_container: save_this_container
        set block_scope_container: saved_block_scope_container
    }

    procedure add_to_container_chain(this, next: ref[AstNode])
    {
        set last_container: next
    }

    ; ast.GetLocals: the container's own table, made on first use.
    function get_locals(host: ref[Page], n: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[SymbolTable]
    {
        if n.locals = null
            set n.locals: SymbolTable.create(host)
        n.locals as ref[SymbolTable]
    }

    ; ── the symbol machinery ────────────────────────────────────────────────

    ; newSymbol. The counter is the reference's and is compared by the dump.
    procedure new_symbol(this, flags: int, name_data: Slice[char]) returns ref[Symbol]
    {
        set symbol_count: symbol_count + 1
        Symbol.create(host, flags, name_data)
    }

    ; ── ast.GetSymbolId, slice 41 ────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ LAZY, and that is the whole content of the function. Upstream:
    ;
    ;     id := symbol.id.Load()
    ;     if id == 0 { id = nextSymbolId.Add(1); CAS … }
    ;
    ; so a symbol draws a number the FIRST time somebody asks for one and keeps
    ; it, and a symbol nobody asks about never draws one at all. Both halves are
    ; observable here, because the only asker in the reference binder is the
    ; private-name minter and the number it draws lands in a symbol NAME: assign at
    ; symbol CREATION instead and every class in the file shifts the numbers of the
    ; classes after it; assign twice and the same class gets two names.
    ;
    ; The CAS is the parallelism upstream's shared counter needs and this port does
    ; not have — one binder, one file, one thread — so what is ported is the
    ; contract (draw once, remember) and not the instruction. §3.10 is the standing
    ; argument for that direction; §3.5cl is what makes it invisible to the
    ; yardstick.
    procedure get_symbol_id(this, s: ref[Symbol]) returns int
    {
        if s.id = 0
        {
            set next_symbol_id: next_symbol_id + 1
            set s.id: next_symbol_id
        }
        s.id
    }

    ; GetSymbolNameForPrivateIdentifier:
    ;
    ;     InternalSymbolNamePrefix + "#" + strconv.Itoa(GetSymbolId(sym)) + "@" + description
    ;
    ; ★ The PREFIX is the 0xFE byte, so the name is unspellable — the reason every
    ; other internal name carries it, and here it carries a second weight: `#x` in
    ; two different classes must be two different symbols, and the class's id is
    ; what tells them apart.
    ;
    ; ★ The `@` is TRANSCRIBED, not reasoned about, and the obvious reason for it is
    ; wrong: a delimiter between a variable-length number and a description is
    ; needed in general, but the description here is a PrivateIdentifier's whole
    ; token and so always begins with its own `#` — the number cannot run into it.
    ; So the `@` buys no uniqueness and is written because the reference writes it;
    ; control c8 is what makes that a measurement rather than a preference.
    ;
    ; Shaped after anonymous_parameter_name, for its reason: the digits are written
    ; most-significant-LAST, so their count has to be known before the buffer is
    ; taken.
    function private_identifier_name(host: ref[Page], id: int, desc_data: Slice[char]) returns Slice[char]
    {
        var digits 1
        var rest id / 10
        while rest > 0
        {
            set digits: digits + 1
            set rest: rest / 10
        }
        let total digits + desc_data.length + 3
        let buf host.allocate(total as size_t, 1) as pointer[char]
        set *buf: 0xFE as char
        set *(buf + 1): "#" as char
        var value id
        var i digits
        while i > 0
        {
            set *(buf + 1 + i): ((("0" as char) as int) + (value % 10)) as char
            set value: value / 10
            set i: i - 1
        }
        set *(buf + 2 + digits): "@" as char
        var j 0
        while j < desc_data.length
        {
            set *(buf + 3 + digits + j): desc_data[j]
            set j: j + 1
        }
        Slice[char](total, buf)
    }

    ; addDeclarationToSymbol. `AppendIfUnique` is the reference's own append: a
    ; node may reach here twice — a parameter property declares into two tables —
    ; and the declaration list must not carry it twice.
    procedure add_declaration_to_symbol(this, s: ref[Symbol], n: ref[AstNode], symbol_flags: int)
    {
        set s.flags: s.flags | symbol_flags
        set n.symbol: s
        if s.declarations = null
        {
            let a &Array[ref[AstNode]?]^host()
            a.add(n)
            set s.declarations: a
        }
        else
        {
            var found false
            let len s.declarations.get_length() as int
            var i 0
            while i < len
            {
                if s.declarations[i] = n
                    set found: true
                set i: i + 1
            }
            if found = false
                s.declarations.add(n)
        }

        ; ★★ The const-enum-only reset, and slice 31 is the slice its stand-in
        ; named: it can only fire on a symbol that already carries
        ; ConstEnumOnlyModule, which only bindModuleDeclaration sets. What the
        ; reference's comment states is the DIRECTION — "on merge of const enum
        ; module with class or function, reset const enum only flag (namespaces
        ; will already recalculate)" — so this is the half that a NON-module
        ; declaration does, and bind_module_declaration is the half a module does.
        ; The side set is written here too, or a later module declaration of the
        ; same name would set the bit back.
        if (s.flags & SymbolFlagsConstEnumOnlyModule) <> 0
        {
            if (s.flags & (SymbolFlagsFunction | SymbolFlagsClass | SymbolFlagsRegularEnum)) <> 0
            {
                Binder.clear_const_enum_only_module(s)
                this.add_not_const_enum_only(s)
            }
        }

        if (symbol_flags & SymbolFlagsValue) <> 0
            Binder.set_value_declaration(s, n)
    }

    ; SetValueDeclaration. Three conditions, and slice 31 makes the THIRD one
    ; reachable: a non-namespace declaration takes precedence over a namespace
    ; one, so a `function f` merging into a `namespace f` moves the symbol's value
    ; declaration to the function. The dump carries the value declaration's span,
    ; so this is compared rather than inferred.
    ;
    ; ★ isEffectiveModuleDeclaration is `IsModuleDeclaration(node) ||
    ; IsIdentifier(node)`, and the second disjunct stays with the first: an
    ; IDENTIFIER as a value declaration is a JS expando assignment, whose arms are
    ; unported — transcribed here rather than left out, because the two are one
    ; predicate in the reference and splitting it would be a claim about which
    ; half is live.
    ;
    ; ★★ The remaining condition — an ASSIGNMENT declaration losing to a
    ; non-assignment one — was named at this line rather than transcribed while
    ; no arm could produce one, and slice 37 is the slice that produces them:
    ; `exports.a = 1` followed by `function a() {}` in a CommonJS file declares
    ; the same symbol twice, and the FUNCTION is its value declaration.
    ;
    ; ★ The equal-KIND early return below is not the reference's shape and is
    ; equivalent to it, which is worth an argument rather than a glance: with the
    ; kinds equal, isAssignmentDeclaration answers the same for both nodes, so
    ; `A && !A` is false — and the third disjunct's own `Kind != Kind` is false by
    ; assumption. So neither disjunct can be true there, in the ported set and in
    ; any future one, because the predicate reads nothing but the KIND.
    function set_value_declaration(s: ref[Symbol], n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if s.value_declaration = null
        {
            set s.value_declaration: n
            return
        }
        if AstNode.kind_of(s.value_declaration) = AstNode.kind_of(n)
            return
        if Binder.is_assignment_declaration(s.value_declaration)
        {
            if Binder.is_assignment_declaration(n) = false
            {
                set s.value_declaration: n
                return
            }
        }
        if Binder.is_effective_module_declaration(s.value_declaration)
            set s.value_declaration: n
    }

    ; binder.isAssignmentDeclaration — the four node kinds a JavaScript
    ; assignment declaration can have. Transcribed whole: the access-expression
    ; and identifier readings belong to the expando pass, which is not this
    ; slice, and splitting the predicate would be a claim about which half is
    ; live.
    function is_assignment_declaration(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindBinaryExpression
            return true
        if k = KindPropertyAccessExpression
            return true
        if k = KindElementAccessExpression
            return true
        if k = KindIdentifier
            return true
        k = KindCallExpression
    }

    ; isEffectiveModuleDeclaration.
    function is_effective_module_declaration(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
            return true
        k = KindIdentifier
    }

    ; declareSymbol / declareSymbolEx. The heart of the binder: find or make the
    ; symbol of this name in this table, report where the two declarations may not
    ; merge, and hand the node to it.
    ;
    ; ★★ The pair is the reference's own — `declareSymbol` IS `declareSymbolEx`
    ; with both flags false — and slice 42 is what made the split earn its keep:
    ; `this.a = x` is the one caller in the whole binder that passes either flag.
    ; Every other call site keeps the five-argument spelling and is untouched.
    procedure declare_symbol(this, table: ref[SymbolTable], parent: ref[Symbol]?, n: ref[AstNode]?, includes: int, excludes: int) returns ref[Symbol]?
        this.declare_symbol_ex(table, parent, n, includes, excludes, false, false)

    ; ★ `isComputedName` is not "the name is computed" — it is the CALLER telling
    ; declareSymbolEx that it already knows the name is dynamic and wants the
    ; anonymous `computed` one. Upstream asserts exactly that
    ; (`Debug.assert(isComputedName || !HasDynamicName(node))`), which is why the
    ; flag is a parameter rather than a question this function could ask for
    ; itself.
    procedure declare_symbol_ex(this, table: ref[SymbolTable], parent: ref[Symbol]?, n: ref[AstNode]?, includes: int, excludes: int, is_replaceable_by_method: bool, is_computed_name: bool) returns ref[Symbol]?
    {
        var name_data Slice[char]()
        ; isDefaultExport is `HasSyntacticModifier(node, Default) ||
        ; IsExportSpecifier(node) && ModuleExportNameIsDefault(node.Name())`, and
        ; slice 29 owns the second disjunct because it is the slice that binds an
        ; export specifier. `ModuleExportNameIsDefault` is `name.Text() ==
        ; "default"` over a ModuleExportName, i.e. an Identifier or a STRING
        ; LITERAL (`export { x as "default" }`), and BOTH are answered since slice
        ; 31 stored a literal's text. Answering `false` for the literal spelling
        ; would have been the quiet wrong answer §3.9e rejects: it decides the
        ; symbol's NAME.
        var is_default_export: bool this.has_syntactic_modifier(n, ModifierFlagsDefault)
        if is_default_export = false
        {
            if AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindExportSpecifier
            {
                let en AstNode.name_of(n)
                if en <> null
                {
                    if AstNode.kind_of(en) = KindIdentifier
                    {
                        var dd Slice[char]()
                        set dd: Binder.internal_name_unprefixed(host, "default")
                        if SymbolTable.names_equal(AstNode.identifier_text_of(en), dd)
                            set is_default_export: true
                    }
                    ; ★ Slice 31: a STRING-named export specifier is answerable
                    ; now — `export { x as "default" }` is a default export and its
                    ; duplicate is TS2528 rather than TS2300, which is the one
                    ; place slice 29 measured this disjunct to be visible. The
                    ; stand-in it replaces was not about the specifier at all: it
                    ; was about a literal's text.
                    else
                    {
                        if AstNode.kind_of(en) = KindStringLiteral
                        {
                            var dd Slice[char]()
                            set dd: Binder.internal_name_unprefixed(host, "default")
                            if SymbolTable.names_equal(AstNode.literal_text_of(en), dd)
                                set is_default_export: true
                        }
                        else
                            this.record_unported("export-specifier-name-literal", AstNode.kind_of(en))
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        if this.is_unported()
            return null

        ; ★★ The reference's name switch is ORDERED and the computed arm is FIRST,
        ; ahead of the default-export one. It cannot matter today — a dynamically
        ; named declaration carries no `default` modifier — but the order is the
        ; reference's and a later arm reaching this function must not have to
        ; rediscover it.
        if is_computed_name
            set name_data: Binder.internal_name(host, "computed")
        else if is_default_export
        {
            if parent <> null
                set name_data: Binder.internal_name_unprefixed(host, "default")
            else
                this.get_declaration_name(n, &name_data)
        }
        else
            this.get_declaration_name(n, &name_data)

        if this.is_unported()
            return null

        var s: ref[Symbol]? null
        var missing_data Slice[char]()
        set missing_data: Binder.internal_name(host, "missing")
        if SymbolTable.names_equal(name_data, missing_data)
            set s: this.new_symbol(SymbolFlagsNone, missing_data)
        else
        {
            set s: SymbolTable.get(table, name_data)
            if (includes & SymbolFlagsClassifiable) <> 0
                SymbolTable.set_entry(host, classifiable_names, name_data, null)
            if s = null
            {
                set s: this.new_symbol(SymbolFlagsNone, name_data)
                SymbolTable.set_entry(host, table, name_data, s)
                ; ★★ Slice 42. The flag is written on the symbol, not kept in the
                ; call — a `this.a = x` in a JavaScript constructor marks its
                ; symbol REPLACEABLE, and the two arms below read that mark on a
                ; LATER declaration of the same name, which may come from an
                ; ordinary prototype method that passes the flag as false.
                if is_replaceable_by_method
                    set s.flags: s.flags | SymbolFlagsReplaceableByMethod
            }
            else
            {
                ; ★★★ Slice 42 retires the marker that stood here. isReplaceableByMethod
                ; is the JavaScript constructor-property rule — `this.method = …`
                ; must LOSE to a prototype method of the same name, so that
                ; `this.method = this.method.bind(this)` is the pattern it looks
                ; like and not a duplicate — and it has TWO arms, which is easy to
                ; read as one:
                ;
                ;   the CONSTRUCTOR property arrives second (this call passes the
                ;   flag, the standing symbol does not carry it): the property
                ;   loses immediately — return the existing symbol WITHOUT adding
                ;   a declaration, before the excludes are even consulted.
                ;
                ;   the constructor property arrived FIRST (the standing symbol
                ;   carries the mark) and now conflicts: the property loses again,
                ;   but the other way round — a FRESH symbol replaces it in the
                ;   table and the marked one is dropped, with no diagnostic.
                ;
                ; ★ The first arm is ahead of the excludes test in the reference,
                ; and the order is load-bearing: without it a conflicting pair
                ; would report a duplicate before either arm could discard.
                var replaceable_loses false
                if is_replaceable_by_method
                {
                    if (s.flags & SymbolFlagsReplaceableByMethod) = 0
                        set replaceable_loses: true
                }
                if replaceable_loses
                    return s

                if (s.flags & excludes) <> 0
                {
                    if (s.flags & SymbolFlagsReplaceableByMethod) <> 0
                    {
                        set s: this.new_symbol(SymbolFlagsNone, name_data)
                        SymbolTable.set_entry(host, table, name_data, s)
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        ; The assignment/variable merge — `SymbolFlagsAssignment`
                        ; is set only by the JS expando arms, which are unported,
                        ; so neither side of the reference's disjunction can be
                        ; true here.
                        var merges_as_assignment false
                        if (includes & SymbolFlagsVariable) <> 0
                        {
                            if (s.flags & SymbolFlagsAssignment) <> 0
                                set merges_as_assignment: true
                        }
                        if (includes & SymbolFlagsAssignment) <> 0
                        {
                            if (s.flags & SymbolFlagsVariable) <> 0
                                set merges_as_assignment: true
                        }
                        if merges_as_assignment = false
                        {
                            this.report_duplicate(s, n, includes, is_default_export)
                            if this.is_unported()
                                return null
                            ; When an accessor conflicts with a non-accessor the
                            ; reference marks the symbol a FULL accessor, so every
                            ; later declaration of that name conflicts too.
                            if (s.flags & SymbolFlagsAccessor) <> 0
                            {
                                if (s.flags & SymbolFlagsAccessor) <> (includes & SymbolFlagsAccessor)
                                    set s.flags: s.flags | SymbolFlagsAccessor
                            }
                            ; ★★★ THE FRESH SYMBOL IS NOT PUT BACK IN THE TABLE,
                            ; and the line that did it was here for four slices as
                            ; a NO-OP: `SymbolTable.set_entry` could not replace an
                            ; existing entry (§3.5bx-adjacent — the explicit-deref
                            ; store, see ast.scaly's set_entry), so writing the
                            ; second symbol over the first did nothing and the dump
                            ; agreed with the reference by accident. Fixing the
                            ; store turned ten units red at once, which is what the
                            ; line really claimed. Upstream keeps the FIRST symbol
                            ; in the table — a duplicate declaration gets a symbol
                            ; of its own that nothing can look up — and re-inserts
                            ; only in the isReplaceableByMethod branch, which is a
                            ; JavaScript constructor property and unported.
                            set s: this.new_symbol(SymbolFlagsNone, name_data)
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        if this.is_unported()
            return null

        this.add_declaration_to_symbol(s as ref[Symbol], n as ref[AstNode], includes)
        if s.parent = null
            set s.parent: parent
        else
        {
            ; The reference PANICS here ("Existing symbol parent should match new
            ; one"). A panic is not something to reproduce, so the marker stands
            ; where it is — the parser's five markers follow the same convention.
            if s.parent <> parent
                this.record_unported("symbol-parent-mismatch", 0)
        }
        s
    }

    ; ast.GetNameOfDeclaration, and it is NOT `Node.Name()` — which is what this
    ; port used at both of report_duplicate's sites until slice 29's stage-2 run
    ; found the difference on two units of one case
    ; (conformance/externalModules/duplicateExportAssignments, `export = x;` twice).
    ; An ExportAssignment has no name SLOT; the reference answers its EXPRESSION
    ; when that is an identifier, so a duplicate `export = x` is reported at the
    ; `x` and not across the whole statement. Our span was eleven characters wide
    ; where the reference's is one.
    ;
    ; ★★★ The lesson is the one §3.5br keeps paying for: a NARROWED accessor is
    ; not the function it stands in for. `name_of` is `Node.Name()`, a slot read;
    ; `GetNameOfDeclaration` is a function with three special cases on top of it,
    ; and the two agree on every kind that HAS a name slot — which is why the
    ; substitution survived twenty-eight slices and broke on the first kind that
    ; has none. There is no yardstick for "these two functions are the same"; only
    ; a corpus wide enough to contain the difference.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE JS ASSIGNMENT ARMS ARE ANSWERED SINCE SLICE 37, and all FIVE kinds
    ; are, not the three that slice binds: GetNonAssignedNameOfDeclaration is a
    ; pure reading of the tree — no symbol, no table, no indicator — so narrowing
    ; it to today's callers would be §3.5ar's mistake, and the two kinds whose
    ; bind arm still reports simply never reach it.
    ;
    ; ★ The remaining special case, GetAssignedName over the three EXPRESSION
    ; kinds, is still REPORTED rather than answered: it reads the PARENT, a
    ; different question from anything this function otherwise asks.
    procedure get_name_of_declaration(this, n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindExportAssignment
        {
            let e AstNode.expression_of(n)
            if e = null
                return null
            if AstNode.kind_of(e) = KindIdentifier
                return e
            return null
        }
        ; ★ The two kinds share ONE arm upstream and the switch inside it is on
        ; the assignment-declaration kind rather than on the node kind, so a
        ; `Object.defineProperty` CALL and an `exports.a =` BINARY meet here.
        ; Three of the five answer the assigned-to NAME — the property access's
        ; own name where it has a usable one, and the whole left operand where it
        ; does not, which get_declaration_name then reads as `missing` — and the
        ; two defineProperty kinds answer the call's SECOND argument.
        if k = KindBinaryExpression
            return Binder.assignment_declaration_name(n)
        if k = KindCallExpression
            return Binder.assignment_declaration_name(n)

        let name AstNode.name_of(n)
        if name <> null
            return name

        ; ── slice 124: GetAssignedName ────────────────────────────────────────
        ;
        ; An anonymous function, arrow or class expression is named by the thing it
        ; is assigned to. ★★★IT WAS A STOP, AND THE STOP WAS HIDING A WRONG ANSWER
        ; rather than an absence: `write_symbol_name`'s caller reads a nil here and
        ; falls through to its own `(Anonymous class)` arm, so `x = class {}`
        ; printed `typeof (Anonymous class)` where the reference prints `typeof x`
        ; — with the stop duly recorded and the dump duly wrong. It surfaced the
        ; moment slice 124's checkClassExpression gave that node a type at all.
        var assigned false
        if k = KindFunctionExpression
            set assigned: true
        if k = KindArrowFunction
            set assigned: true
        if k = KindClassExpression
            set assigned: true
        if assigned
            return Binder.get_assigned_name(n)
        null
    }

    ; ast.GetAssignedName, whole. ★The ELEMENT-ACCESS arm answers only for a
    ; literal argument (`o["a"] = class {}`), and a non-literal one falls out of
    ; the switch to nil — which is the reference's own shape and not a gap.
    function get_assigned_name(node: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        if parent = null
            return null
        let p parent as ref[AstNode]
        let pk AstNode.kind_of(p)
        if pk = KindPropertyAssignment
            return AstNode.name_of(p)
        if pk = KindBindingElement
            return AstNode.name_of(p)
        if pk = KindBinaryExpression
        {
            if AstNode.binary_right_of(p) <> node
                return null
            let left AstNode.binary_left_of(p)
            if left = null
                return null
            let l left as ref[AstNode]
            let lk AstNode.kind_of(l)
            if lk = KindIdentifier
                return l
            if lk = KindPropertyAccessExpression
                return AstNode.property_access_name_of(l)
            if lk = KindElementAccessExpression
            {
                let arg AstNode.element_access_argument_of(l)
                if arg = null
                    return null
                let a Parser.skip_parentheses(arg as ref[AstNode])
                if Binder.is_string_or_numeric_literal_like(a)
                    return a
            }
            return null
        }
        if pk = KindVariableDeclaration
        {
            let name AstNode.name_of(p)
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(name) = KindIdentifier
                return name
        }
        null
    }

    ; The JS half of ast.GetNonAssignedNameOfDeclaration, lifted out because both
    ; of its call sites ask it with a different node kind and the switch inside it
    ; is on neither of them.
    function assignment_declaration_name(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        let adk Parser.get_assignment_declaration_kind(n)
        var wants_left false
        if adk = JSDeclarationKindProperty
            set wants_left: true
        if adk = JSDeclarationKindThisProperty
            set wants_left: true
        if adk = JSDeclarationKindExportsProperty
            set wants_left: true
        if wants_left
        {
            let left AstNode.binary_left_of(n)
            let name Parser.get_element_or_property_access_name(left)
            if name <> null
                return name
            return left
        }
        var wants_arg false
        if adk = JSDeclarationKindObjectDefinePropertyValue
            set wants_arg: true
        if adk = JSDeclarationKindObjectDefinePropertyExports
            set wants_arg: true
        if wants_arg
        {
            ; `declaration.Arguments()[1]`, and the index is safe by
            ; construction: only IsBindableObjectDefinePropertyCall answers either
            ; of those two kinds, and it requires exactly three arguments.
            let args AstNode.call_arguments_of(n)
            if args = null
                return null
            if args.get_length() < 2
                return null
            return args[1]
        }
        null
    }

    ; The diagnostic half of declareSymbolEx: one report at the new declaration's
    ; name and one at every declaration the symbol already had.
    ;
    ; ★ The RELATED INFO the reference attaches (Another_export_default_is_here,
    ; The_first_export_default_is_here, Did_you_mean_0) is deliberately absent: it
    ; hangs off a diagnostic rather than being appended to the list, so the dump
    ; cannot see it — the same reason the parser's related info is invisible in the
    ; D section.
    procedure report_duplicate(this, s: ref[Symbol], n: ref[AstNode]?, includes: int, is_default_export: bool)
    {
        var code: int DiagDuplicate_identifier_0
        if (s.flags & SymbolFlagsBlockScopedVariable) <> 0
            set code: DiagCannot_redeclare_block_scoped_variable_0
        var enum_merge false
        if (s.flags & SymbolFlagsEnum) <> 0
            set enum_merge: true
        if (includes & SymbolFlagsEnum) <> 0
            set enum_merge: true
        if enum_merge
            set code: DiagEnum_declarations_can_only_merge_with_namespace_or_other_enum_declarations

        var multiple_defaults false
        if Symbol.declaration_count(s) <> 0
        {
            if is_default_export
            {
                set code: DiagA_module_cannot_have_multiple_default_exports
                set multiple_defaults: true
            }
            else
            {
                ; The second half of the reference's test, live since slice 29:
                ; `export default 1` twice carries no Default MODIFIER — an export
                ; assignment's `default` is not a modifier — so the multiple-default
                ; report has to be reached through the kind instead.
                if AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindExportAssignment
                {
                    if AstNode.is_export_equals_of(n) = false
                    {
                        set code: DiagA_module_cannot_have_multiple_default_exports
                        set multiple_defaults: true
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        if this.is_unported()
            return

        var declaration_name this.get_name_of_declaration(n)
        if this.is_unported()
            return
        if declaration_name = null
            set declaration_name: n

        ; ★ THE ORDER IS THE REFERENCE'S: every EXISTING declaration first, then
        ; the new one. The dump compares the list as it was appended, so a port
        ; that reported the new declaration first would differ on every conflict.
        let count Symbol.declaration_count(s)
        var i 0
        while i < count
        {
            let d Symbol.declaration_at(s, i)
            var decl this.get_name_of_declaration(d)
            if this.is_unported()
                return
            if decl = null
                set decl: d
            this.error_on_node(decl, code)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        this.error_on_node(declaration_name, code)
    }

    ; getDeclarationName. Answers through out-parameters, and reports unported for
    ; the name forms this slice cannot spell — a name that comes from a LITERAL
    ; needs the literal's text, which this port does not store yet (ast.scaly's
    ; header lists it as the one omission waiting for its first reader; this is
    ; that reader, and the slice that binds a property is the slice that adds it).
    procedure get_declaration_name(this, n: ref[AstNode]?, out_name: ref[Slice[char]])
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)

        ; ★ Slice 29. Both names are UNPREFIXED — `export=` and `default` are two
        ; of the four InternalSymbolName constants the reference spells without
        ; the 0xFE byte, because they are names a program can actually resolve
        ; (§3.5bx). Getting this wrong is invisible until something PRODUCES one,
        ; which is what this slice does.
        if k = KindExportAssignment
        {
            if AstNode.is_export_equals_of(n)
            {
                let out_v Binder.internal_name_unprefixed(host, "export=")
                set out_name.data: out_v.data
                set out_name.length: out_v.length
            }
            else
            {
                let out_v Binder.internal_name_unprefixed(host, "default")
                set out_name.data: out_v.data
                set out_name.length: out_v.length
            }
            return
        }

        let name this.get_name_of_declaration(n)
        if this.is_unported()
            return
        if name <> null
        {
            ; ★★★ Slice 31. BEFORE the name's own kind is looked at: an AMBIENT
            ; module is named by the reference rather than by its name node —
            ; `global` answers the internal `global` and a string-named one answers
            ; the module name IN QUOTES, which is the same pair of bytes
            ; bindSourceFileAsExternalModule puts around a file name and the pair
            ; the checker's IsAmbientModuleSymbolName tests for. The order is
            ; load-bearing: reached through the StringLiteral arm below, `declare
            ; module "a"` would declare a symbol called `a`, which is a name a
            ; program can resolve and would collide with an ordinary declaration.
            if Binder.is_ambient_module(n)
            {
                if Binder.is_global_scope_augmentation(n)
                {
                    let out_v Binder.internal_name(host, "global")
                    set out_name.data: out_v.data
                    set out_name.length: out_v.length
                    return
                }
                let td AstNode.literal_text_of(name)
                let tl AstNode.literal_text_length_of(name)
                let buf host.allocate((tl + 2) as size_t, 1) as pointer[char]
                set *buf: "\"" as char
                var i 0
                while i < tl
                {
                    set *(buf + 1 + i): td[i]
                    set i: i + 1
                }
                set *(buf + 1 + tl): "\"" as char
                set out_name.data: buf
                set out_name.length: tl + 2
                return
            }

            let nk AstNode.kind_of(name)
            if nk = KindIdentifier
            {
                set out_name.data: AstNode.identifier_text_of(name).data
                set out_name.length: AstNode.identifier_text_length_of(name)
                return
            }
            ; ★★★ Slice 41. A PRIVATE identifier is named after the CLASS it sits
            ; in, not after itself: `GetSymbolNameForPrivateIdentifier(
            ; containingClass.Symbol(), name.Text())`. The id is what makes `#x` in
            ; two classes two symbols, and drawing it is what slices 31–40 deferred
            ; — see get_symbol_id for the decision and §3.5cl for what the numbers
            ; agree with.
            ;
            ; ★★ The NULL class is the reference's own arm and its comment is the
            ; reason to keep it: *"we can get here in cases where there is already a
            ; parse error"* — a `#x` outside any class parses, so the walk reaches a
            ; declaration whose containing class does not exist and the answer is
            ; `missing`. Without the arm this would be a null receiver two lines
            ; down; with it, the shape is ANSWERED, which is why the fixtures carry
            ; it at top level, in a function and in an object literal.
            if nk = KindPrivateIdentifier
            {
                let containing_class Binder.get_containing_class(n)
                if containing_class = null
                {
                    let out_v Binder.internal_name(host, "missing")
                    set out_name.data: out_v.data
                    set out_name.length: out_v.length
                    return
                }

                ; ★ Where the reference would nil-panic, the marker stands (§3.5bk):
                ; `containingClass.Symbol()` is read unguarded upstream, on the
                ; reasoning its comment states — a class-like node has been declared
                ; by the time its members are bound. A marker rather than a guess,
                ; so that a shape reaching it reports itself instead of minting
                ; `%FE#0@x` and comparing equal to nothing.
                let cs AstNode.symbol_of(containing_class)
                if cs = null
                {
                    this.record_unported("private-identifier-class-unbound", 0)
                    return
                }

                let id this.get_symbol_id(cs)
                let td AstNode.identifier_text_of(name)
                let tl AstNode.identifier_text_length_of(name)
                let out_v Binder.private_identifier_name(host, id, td)
                set out_name.data: out_v.data
                set out_name.length: out_v.length
                return
            }
            ; ★★ IsPropertyNameLiteral: an Identifier — answered above — or one of
            ; these three, which since slice 31 STORE their text and are therefore
            ; answerable. What the arm buys is a symbol named by the literal's
            ; DECODED value, so `{ "a-b": T }` and `{ 0: T }` declare `a-b` and `0`
            ; and an escape in the name resolves before it becomes a symbol.
            if nk = KindStringLiteral
            {
                this.literal_declaration_name(name, out_name)
                return
            }
            if nk = KindNumericLiteral
            {
                this.literal_declaration_name(name, out_name)
                return
            }
            if nk = KindNoSubstitutionTemplateLiteral
            {
                this.literal_declaration_name(name, out_name)
                return
            }

            ; ★★ A COMPUTED name whose expression is a literal is treated as that
            ; literal — `["a"]: T` declares `a` — and one whose expression is a
            ; SIGNED numeric literal keeps its sign, which is a name built out of
            ; two nodes (`-` and `1`). The third case, a computed name that is
            ; neither, PANICS in the reference: it cannot be reached, because
            ; has_dynamic_name has already sent every dynamic name to the anonymous
            ; `computed` symbol before this function is asked (slice 30). The marker
            ; stands where the panic does (§3.5bk).
            if nk = KindComputedPropertyName
            {
                let ne AstNode.expression_of(name)
                if Binder.is_string_or_numeric_literal_like(ne)
                {
                    this.literal_declaration_name(ne, out_name)
                    return
                }
                if AstNode.is_signed_numeric_literal(ne)
                {
                    let sign AstNode.prefix_unary_operator_kind(ne)
                    let operand AstNode.prefix_unary_operand_of(ne)
                    var td Slice[char]()
                    this.literal_declaration_name(operand, &td)
                    if this.is_unported()
                        return
                    let buf host.allocate((td.length + 1) as size_t, 1) as pointer[char]
                    ; scanner.TokenToString of a `+` or a `-`, which is the only
                    ; call this port makes of that function and the reason it is
                    ; not ported as a table: the two tokens is_signed_numeric_literal
                    ; admits are the whole domain here (parser.scaly says the same
                    ; about the ONE thing the reference needs it for).
                    if sign = KindMinusToken
                        set *buf: "-" as char
                    else
                        set *buf: "+" as char
                    var i 0
                    while i < td.length
                    {
                        set *(buf + 1 + i): td[i]
                        set i: i + 1
                    }
                    set out_name.data: buf
                    set out_name.length: td.length + 1
                    return
                }
                this.record_unported("declaration-name-computed", 0)
                return
            }
            ; ★★ Slice 35. A JSX NAMESPACED NAME is one of the three names the
            ; reference builds by CONCATENATION rather than by reading a slot
            ; (identifier_text_of's note names all three): `ns:attr` is the
            ; namespace's text, a colon, and the name's text. So an attribute
            ; written `xlink:href` declares the symbol `xlink:href` — a name a
            ; program cannot spell as an identifier, which is precisely why the
            ; reference does not route it through the internal-name prefix.
            ;
            ; ★ Both halves are Identifiers by grammar (parse_jsx_attribute_name
            ; builds nothing else), so the two texts are IdentifierData reads and
            ; neither can be a literal needing a decode.
            if nk = KindJsxNamespacedName
            {
                let ns AstNode.namespaced_namespace_of(name)
                let nm AstNode.namespaced_name_of(name)
                let nsd AstNode.identifier_text_of(ns)
                let nsl AstNode.identifier_text_length_of(ns)
                let nmd AstNode.identifier_text_of(nm)
                let nml AstNode.identifier_text_length_of(nm)
                let buf host.allocate((nsl + nml + 1) as size_t, 1) as pointer[char]
                var i 0
                while i < nsl
                {
                    set *(buf + i): nsd[i]
                    set i: i + 1
                }
                set *(buf + nsl): ":" as char
                set i: 0
                while i < nml
                {
                    set *(buf + nsl + 1 + i): nmd[i]
                    set i: i + 1
                }
                set out_name.data: buf
                set out_name.length: nsl + nml + 1
                return
            }
            let out_v Binder.internal_name(host, "missing")
            set out_name.data: out_v.data
            set out_name.length: out_v.length
            return
        }

        ; The nameless declarations, by kind. Every one of these belongs to an arm
        ; this slice has not ported, so reaching one is a defect in the arm set
        ; rather than a name this function cannot answer — but the answers are
        ; ported anyway, because they are one line each and the next slice needs
        ; them.
        if k = KindConstructor
        {
            let out_v Binder.internal_name(host, "constructor")
            set out_name.data: out_v.data
            set out_name.length: out_v.length
            return
        }
        if k = KindFunctionType
        {
            let out_v Binder.internal_name(host, "call")
            set out_name.data: out_v.data
            set out_name.length: out_v.length
            return
        }
        if k = KindCallSignature
        {
            let out_v Binder.internal_name(host, "call")
            set out_name.data: out_v.data
            set out_name.length: out_v.length
            return
        }
        if k = KindConstructorType
        {
            let out_v Binder.internal_name(host, "new")
            set out_name.data: out_v.data
            set out_name.length: out_v.length
            return
        }
        if k = KindConstructSignature
        {
            let out_v Binder.internal_name(host, "new")
            set out_name.data: out_v.data
            set out_name.length: out_v.length
            return
        }
        if k = KindIndexSignature
        {
            let out_v Binder.internal_name(host, "index")
            set out_name.data: out_v.data
            set out_name.length: out_v.length
            return
        }
        if k = KindExportDeclaration
        {
            let out_v Binder.internal_name(host, "export")
            set out_name.data: out_v.data
            set out_name.length: out_v.length
            return
        }
        if k = KindSourceFile
        {
            let out_v Binder.internal_name_unprefixed(host, "export=")
            set out_name.data: out_v.data
            set out_name.length: out_v.length
            return
        }
        if k = KindBinaryExpression
        {
            let out_v Binder.internal_name_unprefixed(host, "export=")
            set out_name.data: out_v.data
            set out_name.length: out_v.length
            return
        }
        let out_v Binder.internal_name(host, "missing")
        set out_name.data: out_v.data
        set out_name.length: out_v.length
    }

    ; The text of a literal used as a DECLARATION NAME.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE NUMERIC ARM STOPPED BEING A QUESTION IN SLICE 44, and where the
    ; answer came from is the point: nothing changed here except that this
    ; function got shorter. A numeric literal's `Text` is not its source spelling
    ; — the scanner ends `scanNumber` with `jsnum.FromString(tokenValue).String()`
    ; — so `class C { 0 = 1; 0.0 = 2 }` declares ONE member, and through slice 43
    ; this arm answered the canonical decimal spellings and reported
    ; `declaration-name-numeric` for every other one. The scanner answers all of
    ; them now (tscaly/jsnum.scaly), so the literal's stored text IS the
    ; reference's name and the arm is the same three lines every other literal
    ; kind takes.
    ;
    ; ★ A STRING or template literal needed none of this and still does not: our
    ; scanner's token value for those IS the decoded text, escapes resolved, which
    ; is exactly what the reference stores.
    ;
    ; ★ It keeps its `this` although the body no longer reads one: its five call
    ; sites are `this.`-qualified, and a this-LESS member reached through a
    ; receiver binds that receiver to its FIRST declared parameter (root
    ; CLAUDE.md's receiver-arity rule), which would silently shift every argument.
    procedure literal_declaration_name(this, name: ref[AstNode]?, out_name: ref[Slice[char]])
    {
        set out_name.data: AstNode.literal_text_of(name).data
        set out_name.length: AstNode.literal_text_length_of(name)
    }

    ; ast.IsAmbientModule: a module declaration whose name is a STRING, or a
    ; `global` augmentation. Ported rather than argued away — the argument would
    ; have been that no ModuleDeclaration can reach declare_module_member while its
    ; bind arm is unported, which is true today and is exactly the shape §3.5p
    ; charges interest on.
    function is_ambient_module(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) <> KindModuleDeclaration
            return false
        let name AstNode.name_of(n)
        if name <> null
        {
            if AstNode.kind_of(name) = KindStringLiteral
                return true
        }
        Binder.is_global_scope_augmentation(n)
    }

    ; ast.IsGlobalScopeAugmentation, which is the KEYWORD and not the name — it
    ; was a line inside is_ambient_module until slice 31 gave it a second caller
    ; (getDeclarationName, where a `global` block is named by the internal `global`
    ; rather than by its keyword's text).
    function is_global_scope_augmentation(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) <> KindModuleDeclaration
            return false
        AstNode.module_keyword_of(n) = KindGlobalKeyword
    }

    ; ast.IsImplicitlyExportedJSDocDeclaration — the second disjunct of
    ; hasExportModifier. A JSDoc `@typedef` in a module file is exported without
    ; anybody writing `export`, and so is a ModuleDeclaration the JSDoc reparser
    ; synthesized for a dotted `@typedef` name.
    ;
    ; ★ `IsExternalOrCommonJSModule(node.Parent)`, and slice 37 is the slice the
    ; note this replaces named: the commonJS indicator is set DURING the bind, by
    ; a `require` call or a `module.exports` assignment, and both of those arms
    ; are ported now — so the question is the DISJUNCTION and the answer moves
    ; while the walk runs.
    function is_implicitly_exported_jsdoc_declaration(this, n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
        if parent = null
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(parent) <> KindSourceFile
            return false
        if this.is_external_or_common_js_module() = false
            return false
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if k <> KindModuleDeclaration
            return false
        (AstNode.flags_of(n) & NodeFlagsReparsed) <> 0
    }

    ; declareModuleMember, all four arms (slice 28 — before it, the ALIAS arm and
    ; the EXPORTED arm reported themselves).
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE EXPORTED ARM IS WHY AN EXPORTED DECLARATION OWNS TWO SYMBOLS, and the
    ; reference's own comment gives both reasons: locals and exports of one name are
    ; mutually exclusive within a container (so the duplicate-identifier check sees
    ; them collide), and the checker resolves an identifier to the LOCAL while
    ; answering the EXPORT. The local carries only the ExportValue flag — not the
    ; declaration's own flags — which is what makes the pair visible in the dump:
    ; two symbols, one declaration each, joined by the `x` line.
    procedure declare_module_member(this, n: ref[AstNode], symbol_flags: int, symbol_excludes: int) returns ref[Symbol]?
    {
        var has_export_modifier: bool this.has_combined_modifier(n, ModifierFlagsExport)
        if has_export_modifier = false
            set has_export_modifier: this.is_implicitly_exported_jsdoc_declaration(n)

        if (symbol_flags & SymbolFlagsAlias) <> 0
        {
            ; An export specifier, and an `import x = require(…)` that is itself
            ; exported, declare into the container's EXPORTS; every other alias is a
            ; local.
            var into_exports false
            if AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindExportSpecifier
                set into_exports: true
            if AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
            {
                if has_export_modifier
                    set into_exports: true
            }
            if into_exports
            {
                let cs AstNode.symbol_of(container)
                return this.declare_symbol(Symbol.get_exports(host, cs as ref[Symbol]), cs, n, symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)
            }
            return this.declare_symbol(Binder.get_locals(host, container as ref[AstNode]), null, n, symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)
        }

        var exported: bool has_export_modifier
        if (AstNode.flags_of(container as ref[AstNode]) & NodeFlagsExportContext) <> 0
            set exported: true
        if Binder.is_ambient_module(n)
            set exported: false

        if exported
        {
            ; ★ The reference's first disjunct here is `!IsLocalsContainer(container)`
            ; and it is DEAD in the reference too, not just in this port: all three
            ; call sites of declareModuleMember pass a SourceFile or a
            ; ModuleDeclaration, and both of those embed LocalsContainerBase. It is
            ; left out with that argument rather than transcribed as a kind list
            ; nothing can exercise; the slice that gives declare_module_member a
            ; fourth caller is the slice that owes the list.
            ;
            ; What IS live is the second disjunct: an UNNAMED default export
            ; (`export default function () {}`) gets no local symbol at all, because
            ; there is no name to declare one under — getDeclarationName answers the
            ; internal `missing` name for it.
            var no_local false
            if this.has_syntactic_modifier(n, ModifierFlagsDefault)
            {
                var nd Slice[char]()
                this.get_declaration_name(n, &nd)
                if this.is_unported()
                    return null
                var mid Slice[char]()
                set mid: Binder.internal_name(host, "missing")
                if SymbolTable.names_equal(nd, mid)
                    set no_local: true
            }
            let cs AstNode.symbol_of(container)
            if no_local
                return this.declare_symbol(Symbol.get_exports(host, cs as ref[Symbol]), cs, n, symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)

            var export_kind 0
            if (symbol_flags & SymbolFlagsValue) <> 0
                set export_kind: SymbolFlagsExportValue
            let local this.declare_symbol(Binder.get_locals(host, container as ref[AstNode]), null, n, export_kind, symbol_excludes)
            let exported_symbol this.declare_symbol(Symbol.get_exports(host, cs as ref[Symbol]), cs, n, symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)
            if local <> null
                set local.export_symbol: exported_symbol
            ; `node.ExportableData().LocalSymbol = local`, ported in slice 65.
            ;
            ; ★★★ IT WAS REFUSED FOR TWO SLICES WITH A REASON THAT SURVEYED ONE
            ; PACKAGE. The note here said the only reader in the whole reference is
            ; nameresolver.go's GetLocalSymbolForExportDefault, so the slot would be
            ; a field nobody reads — §3.5be's rule, correctly applied to a survey of
            ; internal/binder and wrongly stated about the reference. The CHECKER
            ; reads it directly at two sites, and one of them is
            ; checkExportsOnMergedDeclarations' FIRST LINE. See AstNode.local_symbol.
            ;
            ; ★ No dump can see it, then or now, so its arrival is invisible to all
            ; five yardsticks: what measures it is the head that reads it.
            ;
            ; ★ Unconditional, as the reference's line is. Upstream reaches the slot
            ; through ExportableData(), which is a nil dereference — a PANIC — for a
            ; kind that carries no ExportableBase, so the write is total there too;
            ; every node here has the slot (§3.1's sum type puts the four binder
            ; slots on the NODE), so the same line needs no guard and inventing one
            ; would be a narrowing the reference does not make.
            set n.local_symbol: local
            return local
        }

        this.declare_symbol(Binder.get_locals(host, container as ref[AstNode]), null, n, symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)
    }

    ; declareClassMember. A STATIC member is an export of the class symbol and an
    ; instance member is a member of it — one question, two tables, and the class
    ; symbol is the parent either way (unlike the locals arms, which pass none).
    procedure declare_class_member(this, n: ref[AstNode], symbol_flags: int, symbol_excludes: int) returns ref[Symbol]?
    {
        let cs AstNode.symbol_of(container)
        if this.is_static(n)
            return this.declare_symbol(Symbol.get_exports(host, cs as ref[Symbol]), cs, n, symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)
        this.declare_symbol(Symbol.get_members(host, cs as ref[Symbol]), cs, n, symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)
    }

    ; declareSourceFileMember.
    procedure declare_source_file_member(this, n: ref[AstNode], symbol_flags: int, symbol_excludes: int) returns ref[Symbol]?
    {
        if is_external_module
            return this.declare_module_member(n, symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)
        this.declare_symbol(Binder.get_locals(host, file as ref[AstNode]), null, n, symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)
    }

    ; declareSymbolAndAddToSymbolTable. The container's KIND decides the table, and
    ; the arms this slice has not ported are the containers it cannot reach.
    procedure declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(this, n: ref[AstNode], symbol_flags: int, symbol_excludes: int) returns ref[Symbol]?
    {
        let ck AstNode.kind_of(container)

        ; The MODULE arm, slice 31 — and it is the SAME function the source-file
        ; arm reaches through for an external module (declareModuleMember, ported
        ; in slice 28). What differs is only which container's tables it writes,
        ; which is why the reference has the two arms and one body.
        if ck = KindModuleDeclaration
            return this.declare_module_member(n, symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)

        if ck = KindSourceFile
            return this.declare_source_file_member(n, symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)

        ; The CLASS arm. Slice 30 owns it because slice 30 is the first slice that
        ; can reach it: a class member's bind arm is the only caller.
        if ck = KindClassDeclaration
            return this.declare_class_member(n, symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)
        if ck = KindClassExpression
            return this.declare_class_member(n, symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)

        ; The ENUM arm: an enum member declares into the enum symbol's EXPORTS,
        ; not into its members — an enum has no instance side.
        if ck = KindEnumDeclaration
        {
            let es AstNode.symbol_of(container)
            return this.declare_symbol(Symbol.get_exports(host, es as ref[Symbol]), es, n, symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)
        }

        ; The MEMBERS arm, four kinds sharing one body. The case label was
        ; transcribed whole rather than split, because splitting it would put the
        ; same line in two places (§3.5n's argument, one level up: share a body
        ; only where the reference shares it) — and by slice 35 all four kinds
        ; genuinely reach it: the type literal and the mapped type in slice 30, the
        ; interface with them, the object literal in slice 32 and the JSX
        ; attributes object here. What stood here through slices 30–34 was a note
        ; naming the last two as unported, i.e. a claim with an expiry date that
        ; nothing but a reader makes come due.
        if Binder.container_declares_into_members(ck)
        {
            let ms AstNode.symbol_of(container)
            return this.declare_symbol(Symbol.get_members(host, ms as ref[Symbol]), ms, n, symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)
        }

        ; The locals arm: every function-like container plus the type alias and the
        ; mapped type. A parameter and a type parameter land here.
        if Binder.container_declares_into_locals(ck)
            return this.declare_symbol(Binder.get_locals(host, container as ref[AstNode]), null, n, symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)

        ; What is left of the reference's switch is nothing: every arm is above and
        ; the reference PANICS here, so the marker stands where the panic does
        ; (§3.5bk). Reaching it means GetContainerFlags called something a
        ; container that this switch does not know — a defect in one of the two
        ; lists rather than an unported construct.
        this.record_unported("declare-into-container", ck)
        null
    }

    function container_declares_into_locals(ck: int) returns bool
    {
        if ck = KindFunctionType
            return true
        if ck = KindConstructorType
            return true
        if ck = KindCallSignature
            return true
        if ck = KindConstructSignature
            return true
        if ck = KindIndexSignature
            return true
        if ck = KindMethodDeclaration
            return true
        if ck = KindMethodSignature
            return true
        if ck = KindConstructor
            return true
        if ck = KindGetAccessor
            return true
        if ck = KindSetAccessor
            return true
        if ck = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return true
        if ck = KindFunctionExpression
            return true
        if ck = KindArrowFunction
            return true
        if ck = KindClassStaticBlockDeclaration
            return true
        if ck = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if ck = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if ck = KindMappedType
            return true
        false
    }

    function container_declares_into_members(ck: int) returns bool
    {
        if ck = KindTypeLiteral
            return true
        if ck = KindObjectLiteralExpression
            return true
        if ck = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            return true
        if ck = KindJsxAttributes
            return true
        false
    }

    ; bindBlockScopedDeclaration.
    procedure bind_block_scoped_declaration(this, n: ref[AstNode]?, symbol_flags: int, symbol_excludes: int)
    {
        let bk AstNode.kind_of(block_scope_container)
        if bk = KindModuleDeclaration
        {
            this.declare_module_member(n as ref[AstNode], symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)
            return
        }
        if bk = KindSourceFile
        {
            ; `IsExternalOrCommonJSModule(b.container.AsSourceFile())` — the
            ; DISJUNCTION (slice 37), and the container is the file here by the
            ; arm above.
            if this.is_external_or_common_js_module()
            {
                this.declare_module_member(n as ref[AstNode], symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)
                return
            }
        }
        this.declare_symbol(Binder.get_locals(host, block_scope_container as ref[AstNode]), null, n, symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)
    }

    ; bindAnonymousDeclaration: a symbol with a name of the binder's own choosing
    ; and no table.
    procedure bind_anonymous_declaration(this, n: ref[AstNode]?, symbol_flags: int, name_data: Slice[char])
    {
        let s this.new_symbol(symbol_flags, name_data)
        var member false
        if (symbol_flags & SymbolFlagsEnumMember) <> 0
            set member: true
        if (symbol_flags & SymbolFlagsClassMember) <> 0
            set member: true
        if member
            set s.parent: container.symbol
        this.add_declaration_to_symbol(s, n as ref[AstNode], symbol_flags)
    }

    ; ── the arms ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    ; bindSourceFileIfExternalModule. setExportContextFlag first, then the module
    ; symbol — and the module symbol needs a name built from the FILE NAME, which
    ; is bindSourceFileAsExternalModule's whole content.
    procedure bind_source_file_if_external_module(this)
    {
        this.set_export_context_flag(file as ref[AstNode])
        ; `IsExternalOrCommonJSModule` — the DISJUNCTION (slice 37). It is asked
        ; from bind()'s SourceFile arm, i.e. before any child has been walked, so
        ; the second disjunct is still null at every call this port can make; it
        ; is written as the reference writes it because the site is what decides
        ; that, not the reachability.
        if this.is_external_or_common_js_module()
        {
            this.bind_source_file_as_external_module()
            return
        }

        ; ★★★ THE JSON ARM, and leaving it out was a WRONG ANSWER rather than a
        ; gap — which is what the yardstick was built to catch and did, on its first
        ; run (slice 27): a JSON document is bound as an external module too (its
        ; symbol is the quoted file name) plus a second symbol standing for
        ; `module.exports = {}`, and this port answered `f 0 0` for six units with
        ; rc 0. An arm that is MISSING from a switch produces a well-formed dump; an
        ; arm that REPORTS produces a column entry. The difference is the whole
        ; discipline.
        ;
        ; ★ `IsJsonSourceFile` asks the file's SCRIPT KIND upstream; here the
        ; equivalent is the JsonFile context flag, which the parser stamps on every
        ; node of a JSON parse (parser.scaly's initializeState arm).
        ;
        ; ★★ The save-and-RESTORE of the file's symbol is not defensive coding, it
        ; is the mechanism: declare_symbol ends in add_declaration_to_symbol, which
        ; writes `n.symbol` — and the node here IS the file, so declaring the
        ; property symbol would otherwise leave the file owning the `module.exports`
        ; symbol instead of the module one. The reference saves it for exactly that
        ; reason and the dump would show it (the `n` line for the file names its
        ; symbol).
        if (AstNode.flags_of(file as ref[AstNode]) & NodeFlagsJsonFile) <> 0
        {
            this.bind_source_file_as_external_module()
            let original_symbol AstNode.symbol_of(file)
            if original_symbol <> null
            {
                this.declare_symbol(Symbol.get_exports(host, original_symbol), original_symbol, file, SymbolFlagsProperty, SymbolFlagsAll)
                set file.symbol: original_symbol
            }
        }
    }

    ; bindSourceFileAsExternalModule. One line upstream, and the whole of it is the
    ; NAME:
    ;
    ;     b.bindAnonymousDeclaration(b.file.AsNode(), SymbolFlagsValueModule,
    ;                                "\"" + tspath.RemoveFileExtension(b.file.FileName()) + "\"")
    ;
    ; The quotes are part of the symbol's name — that is how the checker tells a
    ; module symbol from a declared one (`IsAmbientModuleSymbolName` is a test for
    ; exactly this pair of bytes) — and the extension is removed from the WHOLE
    ; path, not from the base name, so `a/b/c.ts` becomes `"a/b/c"`.
    procedure bind_source_file_as_external_module(this)
    {
        let len remove_file_extension(file_name)
        let buf host.allocate((len + 2) as size_t, 1) as pointer[char]
        set *buf: "\"" as char
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            set *(buf + 1 + i): file_name[i]
            set i: i + 1
        }
        set *(buf + 1 + len): "\"" as char
        this.bind_anonymous_declaration(file, SymbolFlagsValueModule, Slice[char](len + 2, buf))
    }

    ; ── slice 37: the CommonJS module indicator, and the arms that set it ────
    ;
    ; ast.IsExternalOrCommonJSModule. `ExternalModuleIndicator != nil ||
    ; CommonJSModuleIndicator != nil`, and the two halves are asked SEPARATELY
    ; everywhere: of the ten sites in binder.go, six ask the external half alone
    ; (three of them to pick which of three strict-mode messages to report) and
    ; four ask the disjunction. The field's own note has the split; what matters
    ; at a call site is that this one MOVES during the walk and the other cannot.
    function is_external_or_common_js_module(this) returns bool
    {
        if is_external_module
            return true
        common_js_module_indicator <> null
    }

    ; ast.IsExternalModule and the CommonJS indicator, as the two halves the
    ; disjunction above already reads — asked SEPARATELY because slice 64's
    ; ast.IsEffectiveExternalModule combines them with a MODULE-KIND test between
    ; them (`external || (kindIsCommonJSContaining && indicator != nil)`), which is
    ; a different disjunction and not one this file can spell: the module kind is
    ; the checker's field.
    ;
    ; ★ Two readers, not one accessor with a flag: the field note above says the
    ; two halves are asked separately at six of the reference's ten sites, and a
    ; combined reader would be a third spelling of a question the reference asks in
    ; exactly two shapes.
    function is_external_module_of(this) returns bool
        is_external_module

    ; `file.IsDeclarationFile` — one of the three disjuncts collectModuleReferences
    ; uses to decide whether an ambient module declaration was DECLARED.
    function is_declaration_file_of(this) returns bool
        is_declaration_file

    function common_js_module_indicator_of(this) returns ref[AstNode]?
        common_js_module_indicator

    ; setCommonJSModuleIndicator. Its answer is the GUARD of the three assignment
    ; arms below — `module.exports = x` in a file that is already an ES module
    ; declares nothing — and its side effect is that the file becomes a module
    ; symbol, named after its path, exactly as an `import` would have made it one.
    ;
    ; ★ `b.file.ExternalModuleIndicator != nil && != b.file.AsNode()`: the second
    ; conjunct singles out the FORCE arm of getExternalModuleIndicator, the only
    ; arm that answers the file itself, and this port has no compiler options —
    ; parser.scaly's external_module_indicator carries the same argument for the
    ; same arm, and the reference's own oracle zeroes the option struct, so it is
    ; unreachable on BOTH sides of every yardstick. What is left is
    ; `is_external_module`.
    procedure set_common_js_module_indicator(this, n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if is_external_module
            return false
        if common_js_module_indicator = null
        {
            set common_js_module_indicator: n
            ; `if b.file.ExternalModuleIndicator == nil` — which the return above
            ; has already guaranteed, so the module symbol is made here without a
            ; second test rather than under one that cannot be false.
            this.bind_source_file_as_external_module()
        }
        true
    }

    ; bindModuleExportsAssignment — JSDeclarationKindModuleExports, i.e.
    ; `module.exports = x` where x is not `exports`. The symbol is declared into
    ; the FILE symbol's exports under the name getDeclarationName gives a
    ; BinaryExpression, which is the unprefixed `export=`: a CommonJS default
    ; export and a TypeScript `export =` are the same symbol, which is the whole
    ; point of naming them alike.
    ;
    ; ★ EXCLUDES is 0 rather than a flag set, so a second `module.exports = …`
    ; merges instead of reporting a duplicate.
    procedure bind_module_exports_assignment(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if this.set_common_js_module_indicator(n) = false
            return
        let cs AstNode.symbol_of(file)
        if cs = null
            return
        var flags: int SymbolFlagsProperty
        if Binder.expression_is_alias(AstNode.binary_right_of(n))
            set flags: SymbolFlagsAlias
        let s this.declare_symbol(Symbol.get_exports(host, cs), cs, n, flags, 0)
        if this.is_unported()
            return
        if s = null
            return
        Binder.set_value_declaration(s, n)
    }

    ; bindExportsOrObjectDefineProperty — two kinds through one arm:
    ; `exports.a = x` / `module.exports.a = x` (ExportsProperty) and
    ; `Object.defineProperty(exports, "a", …)` (ObjectDefinePropertyExports). The
    ; name is the PROPERTY's, which get_name_of_declaration answers for both
    ; shapes, so the arm itself does not have to tell them apart — except in the
    ; alias test, which reads a binary expression's right operand and therefore
    ; asks the kind first.
    ;
    ; ★ The symbol is a FUNCTION-SCOPED VARIABLE rather than a property, with the
    ; matching excludes — which do NOT contain FunctionScopedVariable, so two
    ; `exports.a = …` for one name merge exactly as two `var a` do. What the
    ; excludes buy is the collision with a CLASS, an enum or a value module of
    ; that name, and the arm above buys none of that: its excludes are 0.
    procedure bind_exports_or_object_define_property(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if this.set_common_js_module_indicator(n) = false
            return
        let cs AstNode.symbol_of(file)
        if cs = null
            return
        var flags: int SymbolFlagsFunctionScopedVariable
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindBinaryExpression
        {
            if Binder.expression_is_alias(AstNode.binary_right_of(n))
                set flags: SymbolFlagsAlias
        }
        this.declare_symbol(Symbol.get_exports(host, cs), cs, n, flags, SymbolFlagsFunctionScopedVariableExcludes)
    }

    ; ── `this.a = x` (slice 42) ─────────────────────────────────────────────

    ; bindThisPropertyAssignment. The fourth assignment-declaration kind, and the
    ; one that is a thing of its own: the other three declare into a FILE's or an
    ; entity's exports, this one declares into the CLASS the assignment's `this`
    ; belongs to.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THREE GUARDS BEFORE ANY WORK, AND EACH ONE ANSWERS A DIFFERENT QUESTION.
    ; JavaScript only, because in TypeScript a class property is declared and this
    ; would double it. Not a PRIVATE name, because `this.#x = 1` in a JS file has
    ; nothing to declare — the private member's own declaration already did it, and
    ; slice 41 is what made that name exist at all. And `thisContainer` must be
    ; live, which is the register the deferred pass does NOT restore (see
    ; bind_deferred_expando_assignments): here it is, because this arm runs during
    ; the walk.
    ;
    ; ★★ The `else if` is the reference's PANIC — *"!!! constructor functions"* —
    ; and the marker stands where it stands (§3.5bk). A plain function's `this.a =
    ; x` is what upstream has not implemented: getThisClassAndSymbolTable answers
    ; nothing for the two function kinds DELIBERATELY, so those two fall out
    ; silently and every other kind with no class table is the unhandled case.
    procedure bind_this_property_assignment(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if Parser.is_in_js_file(n) = false
            return

        let left AstNode.binary_left_of(n)
        if AstNode.kind_of(left) = KindPropertyAccessExpression
        {
            if AstNode.kind_of(AstNode.name_of(left)) = KindPrivateIdentifier
                return
        }
        if this_container = null
            return

        var class_symbol: ref[Symbol]? null
        let table this.get_this_class_and_symbol_table(&class_symbol)
        if table <> null
        {
            ; ★ Both arms pass isReplaceableByMethod TRUE — this IS the
            ; constructor-property rule's only caller — and they differ in the NAME
            ; and in one flag: a dynamically named assignment gets the anonymous
            ; `computed` name and NO Assignment flag, plus its node remembered on
            ; the class symbol for the checker to bind late.
            if this.has_dynamic_name(n)
            {
                this.declare_symbol_ex(table, class_symbol, n, SymbolFlagsProperty, SymbolFlagsNone, true, true)
                this.add_late_bound_assignment_declaration_to_symbol(n, class_symbol)
            }
            else
                this.declare_symbol_ex(table, class_symbol, n, SymbolFlagsProperty | SymbolFlagsAssignment, SymbolFlagsNone, true, false)
            return
        }

        let tk AstNode.kind_of(this_container)
        if tk = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return
        if tk = KindFunctionExpression
            return
        this.record_unported("this-property-constructor-function", tk)
    }

    ; ── the deferred expando pass (slice 38) ────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The other two assignment-declaration kinds — `f.a = x` (Property) and
    ; `Object.defineProperty(f, "a", …)` (ObjectDefinePropertyValue) — and they are
    ; a PASS rather than an arm, which §3.5l makes a slice of its own. The arm
    ; below only REMEMBERS; everything that decides anything runs after the walk.

    ; bindExpandoPropertyAssignment, whole: append the node and the two container
    ; registers.
    procedure bind_expando_property_assignment(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        expando_assignments.add(ExpandoAssignmentInfo(n, container, block_scope_container))
    }

    ; bindDeferredExpandoAssignments.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT RESTORES TWO OF THE THREE CONTAINER REGISTERS, AND THE THIRD IS NULL
    ; BY THEN — which is not an oversight to correct but a fact to port, because it
    ; decides an answer. `b.thisContainer` is saved and restored by bindContainer,
    ; so when the walk of the SourceFile returns it is back to its initial nil; the
    ; deferred pass never writes it. So getThisClassAndSymbolTable, asked from
    ; lookup_entity during this pass, answers (nil, nil) at every entry — which
    ; makes lookup_entity's `this` branch answer null for every `this.a.b = x` in
    ; the corpus. Both functions are ported as the reference writes them, and the
    ; control battery records the verdict rather than the code hiding it.
    procedure bind_deferred_expando_assignments(this)
    {
        let len expando_assignments.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            if this.is_unported()
                return
            let info expando_assignments.at(i)
            set container: info.container
            set block_scope_container: info.block_scope_container
            this.bind_deferred_expando_assignment(info.node)
            set i: i + 1
        }
    }

    ; bindDeferredExpandoAssignment. Find the symbol of the thing being extended,
    ; and declare the property into its EXPORTS.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE `existing == nil || existing.Flags&Assignment != 0` TEST IS THE
    ; REFERENCE'S OWN COMMENT — "we declare expandos only when there are no
    ; non-expando declarations for that name" — and it is what makes
    ; `function f() {}  f.a = 1` different from `namespace f { export const a = 1 }
    ; f.a = 2`: the second already has a real `a` in f's exports, so the expando is
    ; dropped rather than merged. Two expandos of one name DO merge, because both
    ; carry the Assignment flag.
    ;
    ; ★ get_declaration_name is asked here and again inside declare_symbol, which
    ; is one call more than the reference makes and the same answer both times —
    ; the function reads nothing but the node.
    procedure bind_deferred_expando_assignment(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let parent Binder.get_parent_of_property_assignment(n)
        if parent = null
            return
        var symbol this.lookup_entity(parent, block_scope_container)
        if this.is_unported()
            return
        if symbol = null
        {
            set symbol: this.lookup_entity(parent, container)
            if this.is_unported()
                return
        }
        set symbol: Binder.get_initializer_symbol(symbol)
        if symbol = null
            return

        if this.has_dynamic_name(n)
        {
            var nd Slice[char]()
            set nd: Binder.internal_name(host, "computed")
            this.bind_anonymous_declaration(n, SymbolFlagsProperty | SymbolFlagsAssignment, nd)
            this.add_late_bound_assignment_declaration_to_symbol(n, symbol)
            return
        }
        if this.is_unported()
            return

        let exports Symbol.get_exports(host, symbol)
        var dd Slice[char]()
        this.get_declaration_name(n, &dd)
        if this.is_unported()
            return
        let existing SymbolTable.get(exports, dd)
        var declares true
        if existing <> null
        {
            if (Symbol.flags_of(existing) & SymbolFlagsAssignment) = 0
                set declares: false
        }
        if declares
            this.declare_symbol(exports, symbol, n, SymbolFlagsProperty | SymbolFlagsAssignment, SymbolFlagsPropertyExcludes)
    }

    ; getParentOfPropertyAssignment: the expression the property hangs off — the
    ; `f` of `f.a = x` and the first ARGUMENT of an `Object.defineProperty` call.
    ;
    ; ★ The tail answers null where the reference PANICS (§3.5bk), and it is
    ; unreachable by construction rather than tolerated: the only two callers are
    ; the two `bind` arms, one per kind. The `< 1` test on the argument list is the
    ; same kind of safety — only IsBindableObjectDefinePropertyCall answers that
    ; kind and it requires exactly three arguments.
    function get_parent_of_property_assignment(n: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindBinaryExpression
            return AstNode.expression_of(AstNode.binary_left_of(n))
        if k = KindCallExpression
        {
            let args AstNode.call_arguments_of(n)
            if args = null
                return null
            if args.get_length() < 1
                return null
            return args[0]
        }
        null
    }

    ; lookupEntity: resolve a dotted name against a container, one hop at a time,
    ; where every hop but the first must be an EXPORT of the previous hop's symbol.
    ;
    ; ★★ The recursion is not a plain name walk: each intermediate symbol goes
    ; through get_initializer_symbol, so `a.b.c = 1` only resolves `a.b` when `a`
    ; is itself expando-extensible AND `b` is one of its expando exports. That is
    ; the difference between this and the checker's resolution, and it is why the
    ; function lives in the binder.
    ;
    ; ★ Its ONLY caller is the deferred pass — in the reference too — which is what
    ; makes the `this` branch's verdict a fact about the pass rather than about the
    ; shape (see bind_deferred_expando_assignments).
    procedure lookup_entity(this, n: ref[AstNode]?, container_node: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Symbol]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        if container_node = null
            return null
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindIdentifier
            return Binder.lookup_name(AstNode.identifier_text_of(n), container_node)

        let inner AstNode.expression_of(n)
        if inner = null
            return null
        if AstNode.kind_of(inner) = KindThisKeyword
        {
            var cs: ref[Symbol]? null
            let tbl this.get_this_class_and_symbol_table(&cs)
            if tbl = null
                return null
            let tnm Parser.get_element_or_property_access_name(n)
            if tnm = null
                return null
            var tnd Slice[char]()
            this.entity_access_name_text(tnm, &tnd)
            if this.is_unported()
                return null
            return SymbolTable.get(tbl, tnd)
        }

        let base Binder.get_initializer_symbol(this.lookup_entity(inner, container_node))
        if this.is_unported()
            return null
        if base = null
            return null
        if Symbol.exports_of(base) = null
            return null
        let nm Parser.get_element_or_property_access_name(n)
        if nm = null
            return null
        var nd Slice[char]()
        this.entity_access_name_text(nm, &nd)
        if this.is_unported()
            return null
        SymbolTable.get(Symbol.exports_of(base), nd)
    }

    ; `name.Text()` for the node GetElementOrPropertyAccessName answers: an
    ; IDENTIFIER (a property access) or a string/numeric/template LITERAL (an
    ; element access).
    ;
    ; ★★ It goes through literal_declaration_name rather than reading the literal's
    ; text directly, and that is what makes a lookup and a declaration agree by
    ; construction: get_declaration_name takes the same route, including its
    ; non-canonical-numeric report, so `a[01] = 1` cannot resolve against a name no
    ; declaration could have produced.
    procedure entity_access_name_text(this, name: ref[AstNode], out_name: ref[Slice[char]])
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(name) = KindIdentifier
        {
            set out_name.data: AstNode.identifier_text_of(name).data
            set out_name.length: AstNode.identifier_text_length_of(name)
            return
        }
        this.literal_declaration_name(name, out_name)
    }

    ; lookupName: the container's own LOCALS first, then — if the container is a
    ; declaration with a symbol — that symbol's EXPORTS.
    ;
    ; ★ `core.OrElse(local.ExportSymbol, local)`: a local that has been exported
    ; answers its EXPORT symbol, because that is the one the export table shares
    ; and the one an expando must extend.
    ;
    ; ★★ Upstream guards each half on the container having the relevant BASE
    ; embedded — `LocalsContainerData() != nil`, `DeclarationData() != nil` — and
    ; this port has both slots on every node (ast.scaly's own note on the
    ; divergence). The behaviour is the same because only a locals container is
    ; ever given a locals table and only a declaration is ever given a symbol, so
    ; the null test on the SLOT answers what upstream's null test on the BASE does.
    function lookup_name(name_data: Slice[char], container_node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Symbol]?
    {
        let locals AstNode.locals_of(container_node)
        if locals <> null
        {
            let local SymbolTable.get(locals, name_data)
            if local <> null
            {
                let ex Symbol.export_symbol_of(local)
                if ex <> null
                    return ex
                return local
            }
        }
        let cs AstNode.symbol_of(container_node)
        if cs = null
            return null
        SymbolTable.get(Symbol.exports_of(cs), name_data)
    }

    ; getInitializerSymbol — the gate that decides whether a name can carry expando
    ; declarations at all, and it asks the symbol's VALUE DECLARATION, not its
    ; flags. The reference's own comment is the specification: a previously
    ; declared function, a const (or JS) variable initialized with a function
    ; expression or arrow, a JS class expression, and a JS empty object literal
    ; without a type annotation.
    ;
    ; ★★ For the first arm the answer is the symbol ITSELF; for the other two it is
    ; the INITIALIZER's symbol — the anonymous symbol bindFunctionExpression or
    ; bindObjectLiteralExpression made — because that is where the function's own
    ; exports table lives. So `const f = () => {}` puts `f.a` on a symbol nothing
    ; is named after.
    ;
    ; ★ Go's `switch { case … }` takes the FIRST matching condition and falls out
    ; of the switch entirely when none matches — so a class declaration in a
    ; TypeScript file, or a non-const TypeScript variable, answers nil rather than
    ; being retried by a later arm. The `return null` inside each arm is that.
    function get_initializer_symbol(s: ref[Symbol]?) returns ref[Symbol]?
    {
        if s = null
            return null
        let d Symbol.value_declaration_of(s)
        if d = null
            return null
        let k AstNode.kind_of(d)
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return s
        if k = KindClassDeclaration
        {
            if Parser.is_in_js_file(d)
                return s
            return null
        }
        if k = KindVariableDeclaration
        {
            var extensible false
            if (AstNode.flags_of((AstNode.parent_node_of(d)) as ref[AstNode]) & NodeFlagsConst) <> 0
                set extensible: true
            if Parser.is_in_js_file(d)
                set extensible: true
            if extensible = false
                return null
            let iv AstNode.initializer_of(d)
            if Binder.is_expando_initializer(d, iv)
                return AstNode.symbol_of(iv)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindBinaryExpression
        {
            if Parser.is_in_js_file(d) = false
                return null
            let rhs AstNode.binary_right_of(d)
            if Binder.is_expando_initializer(d, rhs)
                return AstNode.symbol_of(rhs)
            return null
        }
        null
    }

    ; ast.IsExpandoInitializer. The function/arrow arms hold in EVERY file; the
    ; class-expression and empty-object-literal arms are JavaScript only.
    ;
    ; ★ `declaration.Type() == nil` guards the object-literal arm alone: an
    ; annotated `const x = {}` is a real type and not an expando host.
    function is_expando_initializer(declaration: ref[AstNode], initializer: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if initializer = null
            return false
        let k AstNode.kind_of(initializer)
        if k = KindFunctionExpression
            return true
        if k = KindArrowFunction
            return true
        if Parser.is_in_js_file(initializer) = false
            return false
        if k = KindClassExpression
            return true
        if k <> KindObjectLiteralExpression
            return false
        let props AstNode.properties_of(initializer)
        if props <> null
        {
            if props.get_length() <> 0
                return false
        }
        AstNode.type_of(declaration) = null
    }

    ; addLateBoundAssignmentDeclarationToSymbol: a dynamically-named expando
    ; (`f[k] = 1`) is filed under ONE internal `assignment` symbol in the host's
    ; exports, which collects every such node as a declaration.
    ;
    ; ★★ It does NOT go through add_declaration_to_symbol, and each difference is
    ; observable in the dump: the append is PLAIN rather than
    ; append-if-unique, no symbol flags are or'ed in (the symbol's flags stay 0),
    ; and `n.symbol` is NOT written — the node's own symbol is the anonymous
    ; `computed` one bind_anonymous_declaration just made.
    procedure add_late_bound_assignment_declaration_to_symbol(this, n: ref[AstNode], s: ref[Symbol]?)
    {
        let exports Symbol.get_exports(host, s as ref[Symbol])
        var nd Slice[char]()
        set nd: Binder.internal_name(host, "assignment")
        var a SymbolTable.get(exports, nd)
        if a = null
        {
            set a: this.new_symbol(SymbolFlagsNone, nd)
            SymbolTable.set_entry(host, exports, nd, a)
        }
        if a.declarations = null
        {
            let list &Array[ref[AstNode]?]^host()
            set a.declarations: list
        }
        a.declarations.add(n)
    }

    ; getThisClassAndSymbolTable: for a `this.x` assignment inside a class member,
    ; the CLASS's symbol and the table the member belongs in — exports for a static
    ; member, members for an instance one. The two function arms are upstream's own
    ; `!!! constructor functions`, i.e. empty, so a `this.x` in a plain function
    ; answers nothing.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ITS ONLY CALLER TODAY IS lookup_entity, WHICH RUNS IN THE DEFERRED PASS,
    ; WHERE `this_container` IS ALWAYS NULL — so it always answers null and the
    ; first line is the whole function in practice. It is ported whole anyway
    ; rather than left as a marker, because it is not a stand-in: this is what the
    ; reference computes, and the slice that binds `this.a = x` (kind 3) calls it
    ; from bindThisPropertyAssignment, where the register IS live.
    procedure get_this_class_and_symbol_table(this, out_class: ref[ref[Symbol]?]) returns ref[SymbolTable]?
    {
        set out_class: null
        if this_container = null
            return null
        let k AstNode.kind_of(this_container)
        var in_class false
        if k = KindConstructor
            set in_class: true
        if k = KindPropertyDeclaration
            set in_class: true
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            set in_class: true
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            set in_class: true
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            set in_class: true
        if k = KindClassStaticBlockDeclaration
            set in_class: true
        if in_class = false
            return null
        let cs AstNode.symbol_of(AstNode.parent_node_of(this_container))
        set out_class: cs
        if cs = null
            return null
        if this.is_static(this_container)
            return Symbol.get_exports(host, cs)
        Symbol.get_members(host, cs)
    }

    ; bindCallExpression. The whole arm is one question — does this file contain a
    ; `require(...)` call — and the answer makes it a CommonJS module.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference's guard is `b.file.CommonJSModuleIndicator == nil &&
    ; IsRequireCall(node, false)`, and the first conjunct is a pure optimisation:
    ; setCommonJSModuleIndicator's own null test makes the second call a no-op.
    ; Transcribed anyway, because it is the reference's comment ("we can skip this
    ; check if we've already seen the module indicator") rather than an accident.
    procedure bind_call_expression(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if common_js_module_indicator <> null
            return
        if Parser.is_require_call(n, false) = false
            return
        this.set_common_js_module_indicator(n)
    }

    ; declareCommonJSVariable. `module` and `exports` become function-scoped
    ; variables of the FILE, declared on the file NODE, once each — and `module`
    ; additionally gets a member called `exports`, so that `module.exports` in a
    ; CommonJS file resolves through a symbol rather than through nothing.
    ;
    ; ★★ It does NOT go through add_declaration_to_symbol, and the difference is
    ; observable: that procedure writes `n.symbol`, and the node here is the FILE,
    ; whose symbol is the module symbol. So the declaration list is built by hand,
    ; exactly as upstream's newSingleDeclaration does.
    ;
    ; ★ Upstream SHARES one declarations slice between the two symbols; a copy of
    ; a one-element list is the same list to every reader in this port, since
    ; nothing appends to either afterwards.
    procedure declare_common_js_variable(this, name: Slice[char])
    {
        let locals Binder.get_locals(host, file as ref[AstNode])
        var nd Slice[char]()
        set nd: Binder.internal_name_unprefixed(host, name)
        if SymbolTable.get(locals, nd) <> null
            return
        let s this.new_symbol(SymbolFlagsFunctionScopedVariable | SymbolFlagsModuleExports, nd)
        let a &Array[ref[AstNode]?]^host()
        a.add(file)
        set s.declarations: a
        set s.value_declaration: file
        var md Slice[char]()
        set md: Binder.internal_name_unprefixed(host, "module")
        if SymbolTable.names_equal(nd, md)
        {
            var ed Slice[char]()
            set ed: Binder.internal_name_unprefixed(host, "exports")
            let ep this.new_symbol(SymbolFlagsModuleExports | SymbolFlagsProperty, ed)
            let b2 &Array[ref[AstNode]?]^host()
            b2.add(file)
            set ep.declarations: b2
            set ep.value_declaration: file
            set ep.parent: s
            SymbolTable.set_entry(host, Symbol.get_members(host, s), ed, ep)
        }
        SymbolTable.set_entry(host, locals, nd, s)
    }

    ; ── slice 29: the four arms with a body of their own ────────────────────

    ; bindNamespaceExportDeclaration. `export as namespace X` names the UMD global
    ; of a declaration file, and three of the reference's four arms are
    ; DIAGNOSTICS. The `switch { case … }` is a first-match chain, so a wrong
    ; placement is a wrong error number rather than a missing one — transcribed in
    ; the reference's order, and the modifier report is NOT part of the chain (it
    ; is a separate `if` above it, so `export declare as namespace X` at top level
    ; of a module .d.ts reports 1184 and still declares the symbol).
    procedure bind_namespace_export_declaration(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if AstNode.modifiers_of(n) <> null
            this.error_on_node(n, DiagModifiers_cannot_appear_here)

        if AstNode.kind_of(AstNode.parent_node_of(n)) <> KindSourceFile
        {
            this.error_on_node(n, DiagGlobal_module_exports_may_only_appear_at_top_level)
            return
        }

        ; ★ `node.Parent.AsSourceFile()` is `b.file` by the arm above, so both
        ; questions below are the binder's own cached ones rather than a second
        ; walk: the parent IS the file this bind is about.
        if is_external_module = false
        {
            this.error_on_node(n, DiagGlobal_module_exports_may_only_appear_in_module_files)
            return
        }
        if is_declaration_file = false
        {
            this.error_on_node(n, DiagGlobal_module_exports_may_only_appear_in_declaration_files)
            return
        }

        let fs AstNode.symbol_of(file)
        this.declare_symbol(this.get_global_exports(), fs, n, SymbolFlagsAlias, SymbolFlagsAliasExcludes)
    }

    ; ast.GetSymbolTable(&b.file.GlobalExports) — made on first use, like a
    ; symbol's members and exports tables and for the same reason (whether the
    ; table exists at all is a fact about the file).
    procedure get_global_exports(this) returns ref[SymbolTable]
    {
        if global_exports = null
            set global_exports: SymbolTable.create(host)
        global_exports as ref[SymbolTable]
    }

    ; bindImportClause. The clause declares a symbol only for the DEFAULT import:
    ; `import d, { a } from "m"` has a name and `import { a } from "m"` has none,
    ; and the named bindings declare themselves through their own arms.
    procedure bind_import_clause(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if AstNode.name_of(n) <> null
            this.declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(n, SymbolFlagsAlias, SymbolFlagsAliasExcludes)
    }

    ; bindExportDeclaration. Three shapes and each declares something different:
    ; `export * from "m"` is collected under the container's ONE `__export` star
    ; symbol, `export * as ns from "m"` declares an ALIAS named after the
    ; namespace export, and `export { a } from "m"` declares nothing here — its
    ; specifiers do it themselves.
    ;
    ; ★ The first arm is the `export *` written where there is no container symbol
    ; to hang it on (inside a block), and its name comes from getDeclarationName,
    ; which answers the internal `export` for this kind — that arm has been in
    ; get_declaration_name since slice 27 and this is its first reader.
    procedure bind_export_declaration(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let cs AstNode.symbol_of(container)
        if cs = null
        {
            var nd Slice[char]()
            this.get_declaration_name(n, &nd)
            if this.is_unported()
                return
            this.bind_anonymous_declaration(n, SymbolFlagsExportStar, nd)
            return
        }

        let clause AstNode.export_clause_of(n)
        if clause = null
        {
            this.declare_symbol(Symbol.get_exports(host, cs), cs, n, SymbolFlagsExportStar, SymbolFlagsNone)
            return
        }
        if AstNode.kind_of(clause) = KindNamespaceExport
            this.declare_symbol(Symbol.get_exports(host, cs), cs, clause, SymbolFlagsAlias, SymbolFlagsAliasExcludes)
    }

    ; bindExportAssignment. `export = x` and `export default x`, which are one
    ; node kind apart only in a boolean — and the boolean decides both the symbol
    ; NAME (`export=` against `default`, and neither carries the internal prefix)
    ; and whether the symbol is forced to have a value declaration.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference's first condition is `container.Symbol() == nil &&
    ; IsExportAssignment(node)`, and the second conjunct is DEAD in the reference
    ; too: bindExportAssignment has exactly one call site and it is the
    ; KindExportAssignment arm. Left out with that argument rather than
    ; transcribed as a test that cannot be false — the same treatment slice 28
    ; gave `!IsLocalsContainer(container)`.
    procedure bind_export_assignment(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let cs AstNode.symbol_of(container)
        if cs = null
        {
            var nd Slice[char]()
            this.get_declaration_name(n, &nd)
            if this.is_unported()
                return
            this.bind_anonymous_declaration(n, SymbolFlagsValue, nd)
            return
        }

        ; ★ `export default x` where x is a NAME is an alias for that name; where
        ; it is any other expression it is a property of the module. The
        ; distinction is what upstream's comment calls out: you may not
        ; `export default` twice, but `export default function f() {}` and
        ; `export default interface I {}` are declarations rather than assignments
        ; and go through their own arms.
        var flags: int SymbolFlagsProperty
        if Binder.expression_is_alias(AstNode.expression_of(n))
            set flags: SymbolFlagsAlias
        let s this.declare_symbol(Symbol.get_exports(host, cs), cs, n, flags, SymbolFlagsAll)
        if this.is_unported()
            return
        if s = null
            return
        if AstNode.is_export_equals_of(n)
            Binder.set_value_declaration(s, n)
    }

    ; ast.ExpressionIsAlias. `IsEntityNameExpression(node) || IsClassExpression(node)`.
    function expression_is_alias(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if Binder.is_entity_name_expression(n)
            return true
        if n = null
            return false
        AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindClassExpression
    }

    ; ast.IsEntityNameExpression, i.e. IsEntityNameExpressionEx with allowJS
    ; FALSE.
    ;
    ; ★ It used to be a SECOND COPY of the recursion here, carrying the note that
    ; "the slice that gives this function a JS caller owns" the two disjuncts the
    ; flag enables. Slice 33 is that slice — GetAssignmentDeclarationKind asks
    ; both readings — so the copy is gone and the flag is a parameter of the one
    ; in parser.scaly. The claim expired exactly as written and the grep that
    ; found it was for the function's own name.
    function is_entity_name_expression(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
        Parser.is_entity_name_expression_ex(n, false)

    ; setExportContextFlag. An AMBIENT declaration container with no export
    ; declarations of its own is an export context: everything in it is exported
    ; implicitly. The flag is CLEARED otherwise, which matters for a re-bind and is
    ; ported as written.
    procedure set_export_context_flag(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        var ambient false
        if (AstNode.flags_of(n) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
            set ambient: true
        if ambient
        {
            if this.has_export_declarations(n) = false
            {
                set n.flags: AstNode.flags_of(n) | NodeFlagsExportContext
                return
            }
        }
        if (AstNode.flags_of(n) & NodeFlagsExportContext) <> 0
            set n.flags: AstNode.flags_of(n) - NodeFlagsExportContext
    }

    function has_export_declarations(this, n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        var statements: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]? null
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindSourceFile
            set statements: AstNode.statements_of(n)
        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
        {
            let body AstNode.body_of(n)
            if body <> null
            {
                if AstNode.kind_of(body) = KindModuleBlock
                    set statements: AstNode.statements_of(body)
            }
        }
        if statements = null
            return false
        let len statements.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            let sk AstNode.kind_of(statements[i])
            if sk = KindExportDeclaration
                return true
            if sk = KindExportAssignment
                return true
            set i: i + 1
        }
        false
    }

    ; bindEnumDeclaration. The two arms differ in BOTH arguments, and the
    ; excludes are the interesting half: a RegularEnum merges with another regular
    ; enum and with a module, a ConstEnum with neither — so `const enum E {}`
    ; twice is a duplicate and `enum E {}` twice is not.
    procedure bind_enum_declaration(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        ; ast.IsEnumConst, the COMBINED modifier flags — the same question
        ; getModuleInstanceState asks of the same node.
        if this.has_combined_modifier(n, ModifierFlagsConst)
            this.bind_block_scoped_declaration(n, SymbolFlagsConstEnum, SymbolFlagsConstEnumExcludes)
        else
            this.bind_block_scoped_declaration(n, SymbolFlagsRegularEnum, SymbolFlagsRegularEnumExcludes)
    }

    ; ── slice 31: the MODULE DECLARATION ────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; bindModuleDeclaration. One node kind, three spellings (`namespace A`,
    ; `module A`, `declare module "a"`) and TWO different questions asked of them:
    ; an AMBIENT module is a value module by definition and may carry a pattern,
    ; while an ordinary one has to be WALKED to find out whether it is
    ; instantiated at all — a namespace holding nothing but types declares a
    ; NamespaceModule symbol, which is a different symbol with different excludes.
    procedure bind_module_declaration(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.set_export_context_flag(n)

        if Binder.is_ambient_module(n)
        {
            ; ★ Not part of the chain below: the report happens and the symbol is
            ; still declared, the same shape bindNamespaceExportDeclaration's
            ; modifier report has (slice 29).
            if this.has_syntactic_modifier(n, ModifierFlagsExport)
                this.error_on_first_token(n, DiagX_export_modifier_cannot_be_applied_to_ambient_modules_and_module_augmentations_since_they_are_always_visible)

            ; A module AUGMENTATION — an ambient module declared where it can only
            ; be adding to another one — takes the ordinary route, because whether
            ; it is instantiated is still a question about its body. Everything
            ; else ambient is a ValueModule outright.
            if this.is_module_augmentation_external(n)
            {
                this.declare_module_symbol(n)
                return
            }

            let name AstNode.name_of(n)
            this.declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(n, SymbolFlagsValueModule, SymbolFlagsValueModuleExcludes)
            if this.is_unported()
                return
            if name = null
                return
            if AstNode.kind_of(name) <> KindStringLiteral
                return

            ; ★★★ `core.TryParsePattern` + `Pattern.IsValid`, and what the pair
            ; DECIDES here is one diagnostic. Upstream's structure is a parse into
            ; {Text, StarIndex} followed by two tests: invalid — which for the zero
            ; value TryParsePattern answers on a second star means `StarIndex 0 <
            ; len("") == 0` is false — reports 5061, and a valid pattern with a
            ; star is APPENDED to file.PatternAmbientModules.
            ;
            ; The predicate is ported as the question it answers (§3.5ag: compute
            ; WHETHER, not WHICH) and the equivalence is not asserted but derived:
            ; TryParsePattern answers a valid Pattern for a text with no star
            ; (StarIndex -1) and for a text with exactly one (StarIndex < len), and
            ; the ZERO value for two or more — whose only reading is invalid. So
            ; "at most one asterisk" is the whole of it, and there is no third
            ; case for a shorter spelling to lose.
            ;
            ; ★ The APPEND is NOT ported, and this is §3.5be rather than laziness:
            ; PatternAmbientModules is read by the checker's module resolution —
            ; `tryFindAmbientModule` — and by nothing that produces a symbol, a
            ; span or a diagnostic. No dump can see it, so a stored one could not
            ; be checked. What IS observable is exactly what stays: the symbol, and
            ; the report for a pattern that has two stars.
            if Binder.pattern_has_at_most_one_star(AstNode.literal_text_of(name)) = false
                this.error_on_first_token(name, DiagPattern_0_can_have_at_most_one_Asterisk_character)
            return
        }

        let state this.declare_module_symbol(n)
        if this.is_unported()
            return
        if state = ModuleInstanceStateNonInstantiated
            return

        let symbol AstNode.symbol_of(n)
        if symbol = null
            return

        ; ★★ The const-enum-only bit, whose three conjuncts are three different
        ; kinds of statement: the symbol as it stands NOW must carry none of
        ; Function/Class/RegularEnum (a merge with any of those makes the module a
        ; real value), THIS declaration's own state must be ConstEnumOnly, and the
        ; answer must not have been settled negatively by an EARLIER declaration of
        ; the same merged symbol — which is what the side set remembers. The
        ; reference's comment says "namespaces will already recalculate", and this
        ; is where they do it.
        var const_enum_only true
        if (Symbol.flags_of(symbol) & (SymbolFlagsFunction | SymbolFlagsClass | SymbolFlagsRegularEnum)) <> 0
            set const_enum_only: false
        if state <> ModuleInstanceStateConstEnumOnly
            set const_enum_only: false
        if this.symbol_is_not_const_enum_only(symbol)
            set const_enum_only: false

        if const_enum_only
        {
            set symbol.flags: Symbol.flags_of(symbol) | SymbolFlagsConstEnumOnlyModule
            return
        }
        Binder.clear_const_enum_only_module(symbol)
        this.add_not_const_enum_only(symbol)
    }

    ; declareModuleSymbol. The state decides BOTH arguments, and they are not two
    ; spellings of one flag: a NamespaceModule excludes NOTHING
    ; (SymbolFlagsNamespaceModuleExcludes is 0), so a type-only namespace merges
    ; with anything, while a ValueModule excludes every other value.
    procedure declare_module_symbol(this, n: ref[AstNode]) returns int
    {
        let state this.get_module_instance_state(n)
        if this.is_unported()
            return state
        if state = ModuleInstanceStateNonInstantiated
            this.declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(n, SymbolFlagsNamespaceModule, SymbolFlagsNamespaceModuleExcludes)
        else
            this.declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(n, SymbolFlagsValueModule, SymbolFlagsValueModuleExcludes)
        state
    }

    ; ast.IsModuleAugmentationExternal. An ambient module is an AUGMENTATION of
    ; another module when it stands where it cannot be declaring one of its own:
    ; at the top level of a file that is itself an external module, or inside an
    ; ambient module at the top level of a file that is not.
    ;
    ; ★ `IsExternalModule(sourceFile)` is `ExternalModuleIndicator != nil`, and the
    ; binder's cached `is_external_module` answers IsExternalOrCommonJSModule —
    ; one disjunct wider. The two coincide in this port and the reason is measured
    ; rather than assumed (slice 30): CommonJSModuleIndicator is written only by
    ; setCommonJSModuleIndicator, whose callers are the JS expando arms, and the
    ; PARSER never writes it. The slice that ports those arms owes the second
    ; field.
    function is_module_augmentation_external(this, n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        let p AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
        if p = null
            return false
        let pk AstNode.kind_of(p)
        if pk = KindSourceFile
            return is_external_module
        if pk <> KindModuleBlock
            return false
        let gp AstNode.parent_node_of(p)
        if gp = null
            return false
        if Binder.is_ambient_module(gp) = false
            return false
        let ggp AstNode.parent_node_of(gp)
        if ggp = null
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(ggp) <> KindSourceFile
            return false
        is_external_module = false
    }

    ; core.TryParsePattern + Pattern.IsValid, folded into the question its one
    ; caller asks. See bind_module_declaration for the derivation.
    function pattern_has_at_most_one_star(d: Slice[char]) returns bool
    {
        if d.data = null
            return true
        var stars 0
        var i 0
        while i < d.length
        {
            if (d[i] as int) = ("*" as char) as int
                set stars: stars + 1
            set i: i + 1
        }
        stars < 2
    }

    ; b.notConstEnumOnlyModules.Has / .Add.
    function symbol_is_not_const_enum_only(this, s: ref[Symbol]) returns bool
    {
        var l not_const_enum_only_modules
        while l <> null
        {
            if l.symbol = s
                return true
            set l: l.next
        }
        false
    }

    procedure add_not_const_enum_only(this, s: ref[Symbol])
    {
        if this.symbol_is_not_const_enum_only(s)
            return
        let l &SymbolLink^host(s, not_const_enum_only_modules)
        set not_const_enum_only_modules: l
    }

    ; `symbol.Flags &^= SymbolFlagsConstEnumOnlyModule`. Scaly has no AND-NOT and
    ; no unary complement, so the bit is cleared by XOR under a test — which is the
    ; same value for the one bit and does not depend on the width of an int.
    function clear_const_enum_only_module(s: ref[Symbol])
    {
        if (Symbol.flags_of(s) & SymbolFlagsConstEnumOnlyModule) <> 0
            set s.flags: Symbol.flags_of(s) xor SymbolFlagsConstEnumOnlyModule
    }

    ; ── ast.GetModuleInstanceState ──────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ The first ported function here that answers a question about a node by
    ; WALKING THE SUBTREE BELOW IT, and every awkward part of it follows from
    ; WHEN it is called: from bindModuleDeclaration, i.e. before the body has been
    ; bound. So the body's Parent pointers are not set yet, which is what the
    ; `ancestors` stack stands in for, and an export specifier's target may be a
    ; statement of an ENCLOSING scope, which is why the walk can leave the module
    ; entirely.
    ;
    ; ★ It lives on the Binder although upstream keeps it in ast/utilities.go,
    ; because its two dependencies do: the modifier questions are methods here
    ; (they read the modifier NODES rather than a cached flag list) and the
    ; unreachable-case marker needs the reporting channel.
    function get_module_instance_state(this, n: ref[AstNode]) returns int
    {
        let v &VisitedSet^host(null)
        this.module_instance_state(n, null, v)
    }

    ; getModuleInstanceState. A module with NO body — `declare module "a";` — is
    ; instantiated, because there is nothing in it to prove otherwise.
    function module_instance_state(this, n: ref[AstNode]?, ancestors: ref[AncestorLink]?, visited: ref[VisitedSet]) returns int
    {
        let body AstNode.body_of(n)
        if body = null
            return ModuleInstanceStateInstantiated
        this.module_instance_state_cached(body, this.push_ancestor(ancestors, n), visited)
    }

    ; getModuleInstanceStateCached, and the Unknown entry written BEFORE the
    ; worker runs is the cycle guard: `export { x }` naming a declaration that
    ; re-enters this node answers NonInstantiated rather than recursing.
    function module_instance_state_cached(this, n: ref[AstNode]?, ancestors: ref[AncestorLink], visited: ref[VisitedSet]) returns int
    {
        var l visited.head
        while l <> null
        {
            if l.node = n
            {
                if l.state <> ModuleInstanceStateUnknown
                    return l.state
                return ModuleInstanceStateNonInstantiated
            }
            set l: l.next
        }
        let e &VisitedLink^host(n, ModuleInstanceStateUnknown, visited.head)
        set visited.head: e
        let result this.module_instance_state_worker(n, ancestors, visited)
        set e.state: result
        result
    }

    ; getModuleInstanceStateWorker. A `switch` whose arms FALL THROUGH to the
    ; final `return Instantiated` when their own test fails — the enum arm and the
    ; import arms both do — so each is an `if` that returns only on its own
    ; condition and the default is the last line.
    function module_instance_state_worker(this, n: ref[AstNode]?, ancestors: ref[AncestorLink], visited: ref[VisitedSet]) returns int
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)

        ; A module is uninstantiated if it contains only these.
        if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            return ModuleInstanceStateNonInstantiated
        if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
            return ModuleInstanceStateNonInstantiated
        if k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
            return ModuleInstanceStateNonInstantiated

        ; ast.IsEnumConst is GetCombinedModifierFlags, not the syntactic one: a
        ; `const enum` written inside a `declare` block carries the modifier on
        ; itself, but the combined form is what upstream asks and it is the same
        ; function that answers `declare` for a variable declaration.
        if k = KindEnumDeclaration
        {
            if this.has_combined_modifier(n, ModifierFlagsConst)
                return ModuleInstanceStateConstEnumOnly
        }

        var is_import false
        if k = KindImportDeclaration
            set is_import: true
        if k = KindJSImportDeclaration
            set is_import: true
        if k = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
            set is_import: true
        if is_import
        {
            if this.has_syntactic_modifier(n, ModifierFlagsExport) = false
                return ModuleInstanceStateNonInstantiated
        }

        ; ★★ `export { a, b }` with NO module specifier is the only export that
        ; can be uninstantiated, because its specifiers name declarations of THIS
        ; file — so each one has to be resolved against the scopes above it.
        ; `export { a } from "m"` is a re-export of something this file cannot see
        ; and is instantiated by the last line.
        if k = KindExportDeclaration
        {
            let clause AstNode.export_clause_of(n)
            var named false
            if AstNode.module_specifier_of(n) = null
            {
                if clause <> null
                {
                    if AstNode.kind_of(clause) = KindNamedExports
                        set named: true
                }
            }
            if named
            {
                var state ModuleInstanceStateNonInstantiated
                let a2 this.push_ancestor(this.push_ancestor(ancestors, n), clause)
                let elements AstNode.elements_of(clause)
                if elements <> null
                {
                    let len elements.get_length() as int
                    var i 0
                    while i < len
                    {
                        let ss this.module_instance_state_for_alias_target(elements[i], a2, visited)
                        if this.is_unported()
                            return state
                        ; The MAXIMUM, and ConstEnumOnly is above Instantiated in
                        ; it — see the enum's own comment. Only Instantiated stops
                        ; the loop.
                        if ss > state
                            set state: ss
                        if state = ModuleInstanceStateInstantiated
                            return state
                        set i: i + 1
                    }
                }
                return state
            }
        }

        ; The body itself: ForEachChild over the statements, and the callback's
        ; return value is a STOP flag — NonInstantiated contributes nothing and
        ; keeps going, ConstEnumOnly raises the answer and keeps going, and
        ; Instantiated ends the walk.
        if k = KindModuleBlock
        {
            var state ModuleInstanceStateNonInstantiated
            let a2 this.push_ancestor(ancestors, n)
            var i 0
            var stop false
            while stop = false
            {
                let c AstNode.child_at(n as ref[AstNode], i)
                if c = null
                    set stop: true
                else
                {
                    let cs this.module_instance_state_cached(c, a2, visited)
                    if this.is_unported()
                        set stop: true
                    if cs = ModuleInstanceStateConstEnumOnly
                        set state: ModuleInstanceStateConstEnumOnly
                    if cs = ModuleInstanceStateInstantiated
                    {
                        set state: ModuleInstanceStateInstantiated
                        set stop: true
                    }
                    ; The reference PANICS on any other value. It cannot be
                    ; reached — the cached form answers Unknown for nothing, a
                    ; cycle included — so the marker stands where the panic does
                    ; (§3.5bk) rather than being silently absorbed.
                    if cs = ModuleInstanceStateUnknown
                    {
                        this.record_unported("module-instance-state-unknown", 0)
                        set stop: true
                    }
                    set i: i + 1
                }
            }
            return state
        }

        ; A nested module: the same question one level down, and the ancestors
        ; stack is what makes the nesting answerable before the bind reaches it.
        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
            return this.module_instance_state(n, ancestors, visited)

        ModuleInstanceStateInstantiated
    }

    ; getModuleInstanceStateForAliasTarget. An export specifier names a
    ; declaration by NAME, so this is a lexical lookup done by hand: walk out
    ; through the virtual parents, and in every Block, ModuleBlock or SourceFile
    ; on the way, look for a statement of that name.
    ;
    ; ★ `export { "x" }` — a string-named specifier — is invalid syntax and
    ; answers Instantiated without looking anything up, which is upstream's own
    ; comment. That arm is why this function does not need a literal's text.
    function module_instance_state_for_alias_target(this, n: ref[AstNode]?, ancestors: ref[AncestorLink], visited: ref[VisitedSet]) returns int
    {
        var name AstNode.property_name_of(n)
        if name = null
            set name: AstNode.name_of(n)
        if name = null
            return ModuleInstanceStateInstantiated
        if AstNode.kind_of(name) <> KindIdentifier
            return ModuleInstanceStateInstantiated

        ; popAncestor, written TWICE — as the loop's init and as its post step,
        ; which is how upstream's `for ancestors, p := pop(…); p != nil; ancestors,
        ; p = pop(…)` spells it. It answers the virtual parent while the stack has
        ; one and the real Parent afterwards, and once the stack is empty it stays
        ; empty, so the walk continues up the real chain.
        var a: ref[AncestorLink]? ancestors
        var cur n
        var p: ref[AstNode]? null
        if a <> null
        {
            set p: a.node
            set a: a.prev
        }
        else
            set p: AstNode.parent_node_of(cur)

        while p <> null
        {
            var is_scope false
            let pk AstNode.kind_of(p)
            if pk = KindBlock
                set is_scope: true
            if pk = KindModuleBlock
                set is_scope: true
            if pk = KindSourceFile
                set is_scope: true

            if is_scope
            {
                var found ModuleInstanceStateUnknown
                let sa this.push_ancestor(a, p)
                let statements AstNode.statements_of(p)
                if statements <> null
                {
                    let len statements.get_length() as int
                    var i 0
                    while i < len
                    {
                        let st statements[i]
                        if Binder.node_has_name(st, name)
                        {
                            let s2 this.module_instance_state_cached(st, sa, visited)
                            if this.is_unported()
                                return ModuleInstanceStateInstantiated
                            if found = ModuleInstanceStateUnknown
                                set found: s2
                            else
                            {
                                if s2 > found
                                    set found: s2
                            }
                            if found = ModuleInstanceStateInstantiated
                                return found
                            ; ★ Upstream's own comment: a re-export of an import
                            ; alias is AMBIGUOUS, so it counts as instantiated —
                            ; consistent with `export import x = mod.x` being
                            ; treated that way. It sits AFTER the early return, so
                            ; it raises the answer for the rest of the list
                            ; without ending the walk here.
                            if AstNode.kind_of(st) = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
                                set found: ModuleInstanceStateInstantiated
                        }
                        set i: i + 1
                    }
                }
                if found <> ModuleInstanceStateUnknown
                    return found
            }

            set cur: p as ref[AstNode]
            if a <> null
            {
                set p: a.node
                set a: a.prev
            }
            else
                set p: AstNode.parent_node_of(cur)
        }

        ; Couldn't locate it — assume it could refer to a value.
        ModuleInstanceStateInstantiated
    }

    ; ast.NodeHasName. The declaration's own name slot — NOT
    ; GetNameOfDeclaration, so an ExportAssignment named by its expression does
    ; not answer here — plus the one nameless statement that can still declare the
    ; name: a variable statement, through its declarations.
    function node_has_name(st: ref[AstNode]?, id: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if st = null
            return false
        let nm AstNode.name_of(st)
        if nm <> null
        {
            if AstNode.kind_of(nm) <> KindIdentifier
                return false
            return AstNode.identifier_texts_equal(nm, id)
        }
        if AstNode.kind_of(st) <> KindVariableStatement
            return false
        let declarations AstNode.variable_declarations_of(AstNode.variable_statement_list_of(st))
        if declarations = null
            return false
        let len declarations.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            if Binder.node_has_name(declarations[i], id)
                return true
            set i: i + 1
        }
        false
    }

    ; pushAncestor.
    function push_ancestor(this, ancestors: ref[AncestorLink]?, parent: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AncestorLink]
    {
        let l &AncestorLink^host(parent, ancestors)
        l
    }

    ; bindVariableDeclarationOrBindingElement.
    procedure bind_variable_declaration_or_binding_element(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_strict_mode_eval_or_arguments(n, AstNode.name_of(n))
        let name AstNode.name_of(n)
        if name = null
            return
        if Binder.is_binding_pattern(name)
            return

        ; A `var x = require("m")` in a JavaScript file is an ALIAS. Ported in
        ; slice 37 with the `require` call itself — the predicate's note has why
        ; the two had to move together.
        if this.is_variable_declaration_initialized_to_require(n)
        {
            this.declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(n, SymbolFlagsAlias, SymbolFlagsAliasExcludes)
            return
        }
        if this.is_block_or_catch_scoped(n)
        {
            this.bind_block_scoped_declaration(n, SymbolFlagsBlockScopedVariable, SymbolFlagsBlockScopedVariableExcludes)
            return
        }
        if Binder.is_part_of_parameter_declaration(n)
        {
            this.declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(n, SymbolFlagsFunctionScopedVariable, SymbolFlagsParameterExcludes)
            return
        }
        this.declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(n, SymbolFlagsFunctionScopedVariable, SymbolFlagsFunctionScopedVariableExcludes)
    }

    ; `slices.Index(node.Parent.Parameters(), node)`. Pointer identity, which is
    ; what the reference compares too — §3.10's divergence is about node IDs, not
    ; about what a node IS.
    function parameter_index(n: ref[AstNode]) returns int
    {
        let params AstNode.parameters_of(AstNode.parent_node_of(n))
        if params = null
            return 0 - 1
        var i 0
        let count params.get_length() as int
        while i < count
        {
            if params[i] = n
                return i
            set i: i + 1
        }
        0 - 1
    }

    ; `"__" + strconv.Itoa(index)` — see the arm's comment for why the underscores
    ; are ASCII. The digit count is measured before the buffer is taken because the
    ; digits are written most-significant-LAST, so the length has to be known first.
    function anonymous_parameter_name(host: ref[Page], index: int) returns Slice[char]
    {
        var digits 1
        var rest index / 10
        while rest > 0
        {
            set digits: digits + 1
            set rest: rest / 10
        }
        let buf host.allocate((digits + 2) as size_t, 1) as pointer[char]
        set *buf: "_" as char
        set *(buf + 1): "_" as char
        var value index
        var i digits
        while i > 0
        {
            set *(buf + 1 + i): ((("0" as char) as int) + (value % 10)) as char
            set value: value / 10
            set i: i - 1
        }
        Slice[char](digits + 2, buf)
    }

    ; bindParameter. Both arms, since slice 40.
    ;
    ; ── slice 40: the binding-pattern arm ───────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; `({a}: T) => …` has no name to be known by, so the reference gives it one of
    ; its own: `bindAnonymousDeclaration(node, FunctionScopedVariable, "__" +
    ; strconv.Itoa(index))`, the index being the parameter's POSITION in its
    ; parent's parameter list. The position is the whole content of the name — it
    ; is the only thing that tells two destructured parameters of one signature
    ; apart — which is why the helper reads the parent's list rather than counting
    ; anything the walk could have carried.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE FALL-THROUGH IS THE POINT, and slice 39 wrote the warning at the
    ; `return` this replaces: the reference's property test sits AFTER the if/else,
    ; so `constructor(public {a}: T)` declares the class property too. Keeping the
    ; return would have lost it silently.
    ;
    ; ★★ AND THE SHAPE DOES NOT OCCUR IN THE CORPUS — measured, not assumed. Slice
    ; 39's note said its four units "prove the two arms meet in real code", which
    ; holds of the FILE and not of the parameter: a grep for a parameter-property
    ; modifier immediately followed by `{` or `[` over all 13 195 cases answers ONE
    ; hit, and that one is a `readonly [...]` tuple TYPE. Control c12 agrees from
    ; the other side — removing this fall-through is RED on exactly ONE unit, the
    ; fixture written for it. **The fixture is the whole witness**, which is the
    ; argument for writing one rather than trusting a corpus to contain the shape.
    ;
    ; ★★ AND WHAT THAT PROPERTY IS NAMED IS NOT `__0`: the anonymous name belongs
    ; to the PARAMETER symbol alone, while declare_symbol asks
    ; get_declaration_name of the same node and gets a BINDING PATTERN, which is
    ; not a property-name literal — so the name is the internal `missing` one and
    ; the symbol lands in no table (the path declare_symbol's own missing arm
    ; describes). One node, two symbols, and the second one nameless.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE TWO UNDERSCORES ARE ASCII, not the 0xFE prefix `internal_name`
    ; builds, and the reference writes them as a literal rather than through
    ; `InternalSymbolNamePrefix`. So this name is SPELLABLE: a source `__0` really
    ; can collide with it, and that is the reference's behaviour rather than an
    ; oversight to be tidied — the same argument the four unprefixed constants
    ; carry at internal_name's own comment.
    ;
    ; ★★ `slices.Index` answers -1 when the node is not in its parent's list, which
    ; upstream cannot happen — a Parameter's parent is the signature that holds it.
    ; Here it can, for one reason only: `parameters_of` could be narrower than the
    ; reference's `Node.Parameters()`, and a kind it has no arm for answers null.
    ; That is a port gap and reports as one. The guard is MEASURED rather than
    ; defensive: control c18 replaces it with the negative index it catches, and
    ; all eighteen units carrying a destructured parameter then answer `__/` —
    ; `"0" + (-1)` — where c11, the same negative index WITH the guard, parks them
    ; honestly as unported. It is what turned this slice's own accessor gap into a
    ; named report on the arm's first run instead of a diff to be puzzled over.
    ;
    ; ── slice 39: the parameter PROPERTY ────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; "If this is a property-parameter, then also declare the property symbol into
    ; the containing class" — the reference's own comment, and `also` is the whole
    ; shape of the arm: the parameter symbol is not replaced and not moved, a
    ; SECOND symbol is declared into a SECOND table out of the SAME node. One
    ; declaration owning two symbols is unique to this arm in the whole binder.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT IS declareSymbol AND NOT declareSymbolAndAddToSymbolTable, and that is
    ; what makes the arm reachable at all: the container registers are never
    ; consulted, so the table and the parent symbol are both read off the TREE
    ; (`node.Parent.Parent`) rather than off `container` — which for a
    ; constructor's parameter is the CONSTRUCTOR. Routing it through the container
    ; function would land the property in the constructor's locals beside the
    ; parameter, in a table no member lookup reaches.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE ORDER IS OBSERVABLE, AND IT IS THE ONLY OBSERVABLE OF IT: both
    ; declarations go through addDeclarationToSymbol, which writes `node.symbol` —
    ; so the parameter node ends up owning the PROPERTY's symbol, and the dump's
    ; walk line for a parameter property names a symbol that lives in the class's
    ; members table. Declaring the two the other way round is a well-formed wrong
    ; answer, which is what the order control measures.
    ;
    ; ★ `node.Parent.Parent`: the parameter's parent is the constructor — which
    ; is_parameter_property_declaration has just established — and the
    ; constructor's parent is the class-like declaration. The null test below
    ; stands where the reference PANICS (§3.5bk): it hands
    ; `classDeclaration.Symbol()` to GetMembers, which dereferences it, and
    ; bindClassLikeDeclaration has already either set that slot or reported.
    ;
    ; ★ The flags are `Property | IfElse(decl.QuestionToken != nil, Optional,
    ; None)`. The question mark is the PARAMETER's own —
    ; `constructor(public a?: number)` — and it is the one thing the property
    ; symbol carries that the parameter symbol cannot: a parameter's optionality
    ; is a node, a property's is a symbol flag.
    procedure bind_parameter(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if (AstNode.flags_of(n) & NodeFlagsAmbient) = 0
            this.check_strict_mode_eval_or_arguments(n, AstNode.name_of(n))
        let name AstNode.name_of(n)
        var pattern false
        if name <> null
            set pattern: Binder.is_binding_pattern(name)
        if pattern
        {
            let index Binder.parameter_index(n)
            if index < 0
            {
                this.record_unported("parameter-index-not-found", 0)
                return
            }
            var nd Slice[char]()
            set nd: Binder.anonymous_parameter_name(host, index)
            this.bind_anonymous_declaration(n, SymbolFlagsFunctionScopedVariable, nd)
        }
        else
            this.declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(n, SymbolFlagsFunctionScopedVariable, SymbolFlagsParameterExcludes)
        if this.is_unported()
            return
        if this.is_parameter_property_declaration(n) = false
            return
        let class_declaration AstNode.parent_node_of(AstNode.parent_node_of(n))
        let cs AstNode.symbol_of(class_declaration)
        if cs = null
        {
            this.record_unported("parameter-property-class-symbol", 0)
            return
        }
        var flags: int SymbolFlagsProperty
        if AstNode.parameter_question_token_of(n) <> null
            set flags: flags | SymbolFlagsOptional
        this.declare_symbol(Symbol.get_members(host, cs), cs, n, flags, SymbolFlagsPropertyExcludes)
    }

    ; bindFunctionDeclaration. The emitFlags half — HasAsyncFunctions, which the
    ; TRANSFORMS read — is a node flag on the SourceFile that no dump here can see;
    ; the check the reference does before it is ported.
    procedure bind_function_declaration(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_strict_mode_function_name(n)
        this.bind_block_scoped_declaration(n, SymbolFlagsFunction, SymbolFlagsFunctionExcludes)
    }

    ; ── slice 34: the two function-like arms ────────────────────────────────

    ; bindFunctionExpression, which serves the FunctionExpression and the ARROW
    ; alike — one case label upstream, and the arm's whole shape is the question
    ; "does this node have a name to be known by".
    ;
    ; ★ The emitFlags half (HasAsyncFunctions) is left out for the reason
    ; bind_function_declaration leaves it out: it is a node flag on the SourceFile
    ; that the transforms read and no dump here can see. setFlowNode is the flow
    ; half, which this port does not build at all (see the header).
    ;
    ; ★★ THE NAME TEST IS ON THE KIND, not on the slot, and here that is a
    ; TRANSCRIPTION rather than a gate. `IsFunctionExpression(node) &&
    ; node.AsFunctionExpression().Name() != nil` is one condition upstream because
    ; only a function expression HAS a name slot — an arrow never does — and
    ; reading it through the union arm is what makes that a compile-time fact
    ; there. This port's name_of has no ArrowFunction arm, so it answers null for
    ; one and the guarded block is skipped whether the kind is tested or not:
    ; slice 34's c16 is UNGATED, *indistinguishable*. The test stays because a
    ; reader looking for the reference's condition has to find it, and because the
    ; day name_of grows an arrow arm it is the line that keeps
    ; checkStrictModeFunctionName — the arm's only diagnostic — off an arrow.
    procedure bind_function_expression(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        ; ★★ THE SIXTH FLOW-SLOT WRITE, and the one no reading of `bind`'s switch
        ; finds: a function expression and an arrow are given the flow that
        ; REACHES them, so that the checker can ask what a captured variable's
        ; type was at the point the closure was made. It is here rather than in
        ; the switch because upstream puts it here.
        set n.flow_node: current_flow
        var nd Slice[char]()
        var named false
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindFunctionExpression
        {
            let name AstNode.name_of(n)
            if name <> null
            {
                this.check_strict_mode_function_name(n)

                ; ★ The reference reads `name.Text()`, which for a function
                ; expression can only ever be an identifier —
                ; parse_function_expression takes its name from
                ; parse_binding_identifier and the grammar admits nothing else, so
                ; this marker is unreachable by construction. It stands where the
                ; reference would have no text either (§3.5bk), the same way the
                ; class expression's does, rather than guessing at a name.
                if AstNode.kind_of(name) <> KindIdentifier
                {
                    this.record_unported("function-expression-name", AstNode.kind_of(name))
                    return
                }
                set nd: AstNode.identifier_text_of(name)
                set named: true
            }
        }

        ; ★ InternalSymbolNameFunction, and it is PREFIXED — `%FEfunction`, like the
        ; class expression's `class` and the type literal's `type`. The four
        ; UNPREFIXED constants are the ones §3.5bx names — `export=`, `default`,
        ; `this`, `module.exports` — because a program can really resolve those and
        ; they must stay spellable. An unnamed function expression is not one of
        ; them, so the prefix is what keeps it unspellable.
        if named = false
            set nd: Binder.internal_name(host, "function")

        this.bind_anonymous_declaration(n, SymbolFlagsFunction, nd)
    }

    ; bindFunctionOrConstructorType. The only arm in the binder that builds TWO
    ; symbols for one node and wires one into the other's table by hand — and the
    ; reference's comment says why: `<...>(...) => T` must produce the symbol
    ; `{ <...>(...): T }` would, so the signature symbol becomes the sole member of
    ; an anonymous type literal symbol and the rest of the system cannot tell the
    ; short form from the long one.
    ;
    ; ★★★ NEITHER SYMBOL IS DECLARED INTO A TABLE, so this is the one arm that
    ; reaches new_symbol and add_declaration_to_symbol without going through
    ; declare_symbol — no duplicate report, no excludes, no classifiable name. The
    ; SECOND add_declaration_to_symbol is therefore what the node's symbol slot
    ; ends up holding (it overwrites the first), which is the type literal; the
    ; signature symbol is reachable only through that members table. Both orders
    ; are transcribed rather than reasoned about, because the slot is dumped.
    ;
    ; ★ Upstream makes the members table EAGERLY here (`make(ast.SymbolTable)`)
    ; where every other producer goes through ast.GetMembers. It makes no
    ; difference: the very next line puts an entry in it, so a lazily created table
    ; is created by that entry and has the same one member either way. Whether a
    ; symbol has a members table AT ALL is observable (§the locals note in
    ; bind_container) — a table that always gets its entry is not one of those
    ; cases.
    procedure bind_function_or_constructor_type(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        var nd Slice[char]()
        this.get_declaration_name(n, &nd)
        if this.is_unported()
            return
        let s this.new_symbol(SymbolFlagsSignature, nd)
        this.add_declaration_to_symbol(s, n, SymbolFlagsSignature)

        var td Slice[char]()
        set td: Binder.internal_name(host, "type")
        let tls this.new_symbol(SymbolFlagsTypeLiteral, td)
        this.add_declaration_to_symbol(tls, n, SymbolFlagsTypeLiteral)
        let members Symbol.get_members(host, tls)
        SymbolTable.set_entry(host, members, s.name, s)
    }

    ; bindTypeParameter. Two arms, and they do not write to the same kind of table:
    ; a type parameter of a SIGNATURE declares into its container the ordinary way,
    ; while `infer U` declares into the LOCALS OF THE CONDITIONAL TYPE whose
    ; `extends` clause it sits in — a node that is not a container at all (it is
    ; absent from GetContainerFlags), so that table is made here, by this
    ; declaration, and by nothing else in the binder.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE CONTAINER IS NOT THE NEAREST LOCALS CONTAINER, which is the wrong
    ; answer that suggests itself and that a one-line fixture would not tell apart:
    ; in `T extends (x: infer U) => void ? U : never` the FunctionType between the
    ; two IS a locals container — it holds `x` — and `U` goes past it into the
    ; conditional type's own table. Asked of the oracle before the arm was written
    ; (§3.5bt's habit), and `binder_infer_type_function.ts` keeps the answer.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE ELSE ARM IS NOT A FALLBACK BUT A SECOND RULE. A null container — an
    ; `infer` that is nowhere in an extends clause, which parses and is a CHECKER
    ; error rather than a syntactic one — takes bindAnonymousDeclaration, and the
    ; difference is observable twice over: the symbol lands in NO table, and the
    ; file's CLASSIFIABLE names do not learn its name, because that list is written
    ; by declareSymbolEx and a type parameter is classifiable. So the two arms
    ; differ in the dump by a table entry and by a `c` line, and a port that used
    ; one arm for both would be caught by either.
    procedure bind_type_parameter(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let p AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
        if p <> null
        {
            if AstNode.kind_of(p) = KindInferType
            {
                let c Binder.get_infer_type_container(p)
                if c <> null
                {
                    this.declare_symbol(Binder.get_locals(host, c), null, n, SymbolFlagsTypeParameter, SymbolFlagsTypeParameterExcludes)
                    return
                }
                var nd Slice[char]()
                this.get_declaration_name(n, &nd)
                if this.is_unported()
                    return
                this.bind_anonymous_declaration(n, SymbolFlagsTypeParameter, nd)
                return
            }
        }
        this.declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(n, SymbolFlagsTypeParameter, SymbolFlagsTypeParameterExcludes)
    }

    ; getInferTypeContainer: the ancestor that IS a conditional type's extendsType,
    ; answered as that conditional type.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE PREDICATE ASKS ABOUT THE PARENT AND THE WALK DOES NOT STOP AT THE
    ; FIRST CONDITIONAL TYPE, and that pair is the whole content of the function.
    ; `T extends (infer A extends infer B ? 1 : 2) ? A : B` puts `infer A` in the
    ; INNER conditional's CHECK position — not a match, so the walk carries on and
    ; lands on the OUTER one, whose extends clause that inner conditional is —
    ; while `infer B` is the inner one's extendsType and stops there. Two
    ; conditionals, two locals tables, one nesting, and that is what tells this
    ; apart from *the nearest conditional ancestor*.
    ;
    ; ★ Written as the loop rather than as FindAncestor plus a callback: upstream's
    ; FindAncestor is a two-line walk over Parent and the callback IS this
    ; predicate, so the loop is the transcription and not a simplification of one —
    ; the same answer §3.5e gives one level up, where a func-value parameter becomes
    ; a flag because the callees are static.
    function get_infer_type_container(n: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        var node: ref[AstNode]? n
        while node <> null
        {
            let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
            if parent <> null
            {
                if AstNode.kind_of(parent) = KindConditionalType
                {
                    if AstNode.conditional_extends_type_of(parent) = node
                        return parent
                }
            }
            set node: parent
        }
        null
    }

    ; bindClassLikeDeclaration. Two kinds, and they differ in HOW the class gets
    ; its name — a declaration declares one, an expression may have none — but
    ; agree on the tail, which is the synthesized `prototype`.
    procedure bind_class_like_declaration(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindClassDeclaration
            this.bind_block_scoped_declaration(n, SymbolFlagsClass, SymbolFlagsClassExcludes)
        if k = KindClassExpression
        {
            let name AstNode.name_of(n)
            var nd Slice[char]()
            if name = null
                set nd: Binder.internal_name(host, "class")
            else
            {
                ; ★ The reference reads `name.Text()` here, which for a class
                ; expression can only ever be an identifier — the grammar admits no
                ; other name for one. Anything else would be a name this port has no
                ; text for (§3.5bc), so it reports rather than guessing.
                if AstNode.kind_of(name) <> KindIdentifier
                {
                    this.record_unported("class-expression-name", AstNode.kind_of(name))
                    return
                }
                set nd: AstNode.identifier_text_of(name)
                ; classifiableNames.Add. A class DECLARATION reaches the same table
                ; through declareSymbol's SymbolFlagsClassifiable test; a class
                ; expression does not go through declareSymbol at all, which is why
                ; this one line is explicit in the reference too.
                SymbolTable.set_entry(host, classifiable_names, nd, null)
            }
            this.bind_anonymous_declaration(n, SymbolFlagsClass, nd)
        }
        if this.is_unported()
            return

        ; TypeScript 1.0 spec (April 2014): 8.4 — every class automatically
        ; contains a static property member named `prototype`, and it is an error to
        ; declare one explicitly. The reference's own comment says why the check is
        ; HERE and not in the checker: this class may be MERGING into a module, and
        ; the module might export a variable called `prototype`.
        ;
        ; ★ The name is UNPREFIXED, and it is not one of the four internal names
        ; §3.5bx lists — it is a plain string in the reference, because `prototype`
        ; is a name a program really can resolve. Passing it through the prefixing
        ; helper would make the symbol unspellable and the dump would say `%FE…`.
        let symbol AstNode.symbol_of(n)
        if symbol = null
        {
            ; Unreachable: every path above either declares the symbol (which sets
            ; the slot) or reports. A marker rather than a silent return, so that a
            ; future arm which breaks the invariant says so.
            this.record_unported("class-symbol-missing", 0)
            return
        }
        var pd Slice[char]()
        set pd: Binder.internal_name_unprefixed(host, "prototype")
        let ps this.new_symbol(SymbolFlagsProperty | SymbolFlagsPrototype, pd)
        let exports Symbol.get_exports(host, symbol)
        let existing SymbolTable.get(exports, pd)
        ;
        ; ★★★ AND THE DUPLICATE REPORT WAS UNREACHABLE UNTIL SLICE 31, which is
        ; worth keeping because the obvious fixture does NOT trigger it. A
        ; `static prototype` member does not collide with the synthesized symbol:
        ; the class is bound BEFORE its members, and Property is not in
        ; PropertyExcludes, so the member MERGES into the prototype symbol and no
        ; diagnostic is raised — what TypeScript says about `static prototype` is
        ; TS2699, from the CHECKER. The only producer of a pre-existing `prototype`
        ; export is the case the reference's comment names — a class MERGING into a
        ; namespace that exports one — so the fixture has to be a namespace, and
        ; the module slice is where it became writable.
        if existing <> null
        {
            ; The reference indexes Declarations[0] with no guard, so an existing
            ; `prototype` export carrying no declaration would PANIC there. It
            ; cannot be the synthesized symbol — a class symbol is bound once, and
            ; two class declarations of one name do not merge (ClassExcludes forbids
            ; it) — so the only producer has a declaration. The marker stands where
            ; the panic does (§3.5bk).
            if Symbol.declaration_count(existing) = 0
                this.record_unported("prototype-export-without-declaration", 0)
            else
                this.error_on_node(Symbol.declaration_at(existing, 0), DiagDuplicate_identifier_0)
        }
        SymbolTable.set_entry(host, exports, pd, ps)
        set ps.parent: symbol
    }

    ; The TypeLiteral / MappedType arm of bind: bindAnonymousDeclaration with the
    ; type-literal flag and the internal `type` name. One helper rather than two
    ; copies of the three lines the name costs.
    procedure bind_type_literal(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        var nd Slice[char]()
        set nd: Binder.internal_name(host, "type")
        this.bind_anonymous_declaration(n, SymbolFlagsTypeLiteral, nd)
    }

    ; The ObjectLiteralExpression arm of bind: bindAnonymousDeclaration again,
    ; with the object-literal flag and the internal `object` name. The reference
    ; writes it inline; here it is the same three lines bind_type_literal costs,
    ; and keeping the two arms the same shape is what makes the pair legible —
    ; both are anonymous containers whose members table is the only thing anyone
    ; can name them by.
    procedure bind_object_literal_expression(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        var nd Slice[char]()
        set nd: Binder.internal_name(host, "object")
        this.bind_anonymous_declaration(n, SymbolFlagsObjectLiteral, nd)
    }

    ; ── slice 35: the JSX attributes object ─────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; bindJsxAttributes is bind_object_literal_expression with a different name,
    ; and the reference writes it as its own function for the same reason it writes
    ; bindAnonymousDeclaration's other three callers separately: the NAME is the
    ; content. `%FEjsxAttributes` is what the checker looks the attributes object
    ; up as when it resolves `<div {...props}/>` against a component's props type.
    procedure bind_jsx_attributes(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        var nd Slice[char]()
        set nd: Binder.internal_name(host, "jsxAttributes")
        this.bind_anonymous_declaration(n, SymbolFlagsObjectLiteral, nd)
    }

    ; bindJsxAttribute. Its body is one call whose two arguments are the ONLY
    ; arguments its single call site passes — and it is ported as the reference's
    ; two-parameter function rather than as its body, because §3.5aq's rule is that
    ; the wrapper's signature is the documentation of what its default is, and
    ; porting a wrapper as its wrappee deletes the only thing the wrapper carried.
    procedure bind_jsx_attribute(this, n: ref[AstNode], symbol_flags: int, symbol_excludes: int)
        this.declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(n, symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)

    ; bindPropertyWorker. An AUTO-ACCESSOR (`accessor x = 1`) is an accessor and
    ; not a property, which changes both the flag and what it collides with.
    procedure bind_property_worker(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        var includes: int SymbolFlagsProperty
        var excludes: int SymbolFlagsPropertyExcludes
        if this.is_auto_accessor_property_declaration(n)
        {
            set includes: SymbolFlagsAccessor
            set excludes: SymbolFlagsAccessorExcludes
        }
        this.bind_property_or_method_or_accessor(n, includes | Binder.optional_symbol_flag_for_node(n), excludes)
    }

    ; The MethodDeclaration / MethodSignature arm. The reference writes its
    ; excludes as an IfElse inside the call; it is a local here because a method of
    ; an OBJECT LITERAL excludes every value rather than just the methods — two
    ; methods of one name in an object literal are a duplicate, in a class they are
    ; overloads.
    procedure bind_method(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        var excludes: int SymbolFlagsMethodExcludes
        if Binder.is_object_literal_method(n)
            set excludes: SymbolFlagsValue
        this.bind_property_or_method_or_accessor(n, SymbolFlagsMethod | Binder.optional_symbol_flag_for_node(n), excludes)
    }

    ; bindPropertyOrMethodOrAccessor. Two of the reference's four statements are
    ; not ported and each says why at its line; what remains is the choice between
    ; an anonymous symbol and a named one, which is the whole content for this
    ; port.
    procedure bind_property_or_method_or_accessor(this, n: ref[AstNode], symbol_flags: int, symbol_excludes: int)
    {
        ; The emitFlags/HasAsyncFunctions bit is a SourceFile node flag that no dump
        ; here can see — the same argument bind_function_declaration makes at its
        ; own line.
        ;
        ; ★★ THE SEVENTH AND LAST FLOW-SLOT WRITE (slice 92), and it is the
        ; narrowest: only a method or accessor of an OBJECT LITERAL or a CLASS
        ; EXPRESSION gets one, because only those are contextually typed and only
        ; there does the flow at the point of definition mean anything.
        if current_flow <> null
        {
            if Binder.is_object_literal_or_class_expression_method_or_accessor(n)
                set n.flow_node: current_flow
        }
        let dynamic this.has_dynamic_name(n)
        if this.is_unported()
            return
        if dynamic
        {
            var nd Slice[char]()
            set nd: Binder.internal_name(host, "computed")
            this.bind_anonymous_declaration(n, symbol_flags, nd)
            return
        }
        this.declare_symbol_and_add_to_symbol_table(n, symbol_flags, symbol_excludes)
    }

    ; ── the four member predicates ──────────────────────────────────────────

    ; ast.IsStatic. The static block is static by definition and carries no
    ; modifier; everything else has to be a class ELEMENT first, so a `static`
    ; modifier that the parser accepted somewhere else declares nothing static.
    function is_static(this, n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindClassStaticBlockDeclaration
            return true
        if Binder.is_class_element(n) = false
            return false
        this.has_syntactic_modifier(n, ModifierFlagsStatic)
    }

    ; getOptionalSymbolFlagForNode. The POSTFIX slot also holds a `!` (a definite
    ; assignment assertion), which is why the reference tests the token's kind
    ; rather than its presence — ast.scaly's question_token_of carries the same
    ; note for the same reason.
    function optional_symbol_flag_for_node(n: ref[AstNode]) returns int
    {
        let pt AstNode.postfix_token_of(n)
        if pt = null
            return SymbolFlagsNone
        if AstNode.kind_of(pt) = KindQuestionToken
            return SymbolFlagsOptional
        SymbolFlagsNone
    }

    function is_object_literal_method(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) <> KindMethodDeclaration
            return false
        let p AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
        if p = null
            return false
        AstNode.kind_of(p) = KindObjectLiteralExpression
    }

    function is_auto_accessor_property_declaration(this, n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) <> KindPropertyDeclaration
            return false
        this.has_syntactic_modifier(n, ModifierFlagsAccessor)
    }

    ; ── the dynamic-name question ───────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ast.HasDynamicName, and it is the reason a computed property name does not
    ; simply report: `[Symbol.iterator]() {}` gets an ANONYMOUS symbol under the
    ; internal `computed` name, so it never asks getDeclarationName for text this
    ; port does not have. A computed name whose expression IS a literal (`["a"]: T`,
    ; `[-1]: T`) is a static name and does reach getDeclarationName — where it
    ; reports, for the same reason a quoted member name does.
    function has_dynamic_name(this, n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let name this.get_name_of_declaration(n)
        if this.is_unported()
            return false
        if name = null
            return false
        this.is_dynamic_name(name)
    }

    function is_dynamic_name(this, name: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(name)
        if k = KindElementAccessExpression
        {
            ; ★★★ THE CLAIM THAT STOOD HERE EXPIRED WITH SLICE 38, exactly as §3.5p
            ; says such a claim does — and it named the wrong half of its own
            ; condition. It said the arm was unreachable because
            ; get_name_of_declaration reports for all five assignment kinds; slice
            ; 37 ported those five, and what makes THIS arm live is narrower: an
            ; element access is the name only when GetElementOrPropertyAccessName
            ; could not answer, i.e. when the subscript is not a literal — `f[k] = 1`
            ; — which is precisely the shape the deferred pass sends to the
            ; late-bound `assignment` symbol. A literal subscript never gets here,
            ; because the name is then the LITERAL and the two tests below answer on
            ; it.
            let arg Parser.skip_parentheses(AstNode.element_access_argument_of(name))
            if Binder.is_string_or_numeric_literal_like(arg)
                return false
            if AstNode.is_signed_numeric_literal(arg)
                return false
            return true
        }
        if k <> KindComputedPropertyName
            return false
        let expr AstNode.expression_of(name)
        if Binder.is_string_or_numeric_literal_like(expr)
            return false
        if AstNode.is_signed_numeric_literal(expr)
            return false
        true
    }

    function is_string_or_numeric_literal_like(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindStringLiteral
            return true
        if k = KindNoSubstitutionTemplateLiteral
            return true
        k = KindNumericLiteral
    }

    ; bindCommonJSTypeExports. An external module that declares `export = x` folds
    ; every OTHER export that is a type or a namespace into x's own exports, so
    ; `export = C` beside `export interface I` makes I reachable as `C.I`.
    ;
    ; ★ Reachable for the first time in this slice, and that is the whole reason it
    ; is ported here: it needs an `export=` symbol (slice 29) AND a second export
    ; carrying SymbolFlagsType or SymbolFlagsNamespace — an interface, a class or a
    ; type alias, i.e. exactly what slice 30 adds.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference iterates a Go map, so the ORDER it visits the exports in is
    ; random — and the result does not depend on it: every qualifying symbol is
    ; filed under its own name and the flag it sets is idempotent. That is why this
    ; needs no normalisation, unlike the dump's tables.
    procedure bind_common_js_type_exports(this, module_symbol: ref[Symbol]?)
    {
        if module_symbol = null
            return
        let module_exports Symbol.exports_of(module_symbol)
        if module_exports = null
            return
        var ed Slice[char]()
        set ed: Binder.internal_name_unprefixed(host, "export=")
        let export_equals SymbolTable.get(module_exports, ed)
        if export_equals = null
            return
        let len SymbolTable.length(module_exports)
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            let nd SymbolTable.entry_name_at(module_exports, i)
            let nl SymbolTable.entry_name_len_at(module_exports, i)
            if SymbolTable.names_equal(nd, ed) = false
            {
                let sym SymbolTable.entry_symbol_at(module_exports, i)
                if sym <> null
                {
                    if (Symbol.flags_of(sym) & (SymbolFlagsType | SymbolFlagsNamespace)) <> 0
                    {
                        SymbolTable.set_entry(host, Symbol.get_exports(host, export_equals), nd, sym)
                        set export_equals.flags: Symbol.flags_of(export_equals) | SymbolFlagsNamespaceModule
                    }
                }
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
    }

    ; ── the strict-mode and contextual checks ───────────────────────────────

    ; checkContextualIdentifier. A future reserved word, `await` or `yield` used as
    ; a plain identifier is reported HERE rather than by the parser, which is why
    ; the B section carries codes the D section never does.
    procedure check_contextual_identifier(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if file_has_no_parse_errors = false
            return
        if (AstNode.flags_of(n) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
            return
        if (AstNode.flags_of(n) & NodeFlagsJSDoc) <> 0
            return
        if Binder.is_identifier_name(n)
            return
        let original get_identifier_token(AstNode.identifier_text_of(n))
        if original = KindIdentifier
            return
        if original >= KindFirstFutureReservedWord
        {
            if original <= KindLastFutureReservedWord
            {
                this.error_on_node(n, this.strict_mode_identifier_code(n))
                return
            }
        }
        if original = KindAwaitKeyword
        {
            if is_external_module
            {
                if Binder.is_in_top_level_context(n)
                {
                    this.error_on_node(n, DiagIdentifier_expected_0_is_a_reserved_word_at_the_top_level_of_a_module)
                    return
                }
            }
            if (AstNode.flags_of(n) & NodeFlagsAwaitContext) <> 0
                this.error_on_node(n, DiagIdentifier_expected_0_is_a_reserved_word_that_cannot_be_used_here)
            return
        }
        if original = KindYieldKeyword
        {
            if (AstNode.flags_of(n) & NodeFlagsYieldContext) <> 0
                this.error_on_node(n, DiagIdentifier_expected_0_is_a_reserved_word_that_cannot_be_used_here)
        }
    }

    ; getStrictModeIdentifierMessage: three messages, and which one you get says
    ; WHY the binder thinks the code is in strict mode.
    function strict_mode_identifier_code(this, n: ref[AstNode]) returns int
    {
        if Binder.get_containing_class(n) <> null
            return DiagIdentifier_expected_0_is_a_reserved_word_in_strict_mode_Class_definitions_are_automatically_in_strict_mode
        if is_external_module
            return DiagIdentifier_expected_0_is_a_reserved_word_in_strict_mode_Modules_are_automatically_in_strict_mode
        DiagIdentifier_expected_0_is_a_reserved_word_in_strict_mode
    }

    ; checkStrictModeEvalOrArguments, and it has NO strict-mode test — which reads
    ; like a missing gate and is not one. The reference's function is
    ; `if name != nil && isEvalOrArgumentsIdentifier(name)`, its callers gate only on
    ; NodeFlagsAmbient, and the message it picks (TS1100/1215/2332) merely SAYS
    ; "in strict mode". Measured on `tests/fixtures/binder_eval_parameter.ts`, which
    ; carries no `"use strict"` and is not a module: the reference reports TS1100 at
    ; the parameter, and so does this port. Fidelity is to the reference.
    procedure check_strict_mode_eval_or_arguments(this, context: ref[AstNode], name: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        if name = null
            return
        if AstNode.kind_of(name) <> KindIdentifier
            return
        var is_eval_or_arguments false
        if AstNode.text_is(name, "eval")
            set is_eval_or_arguments: true
        if AstNode.text_is(name, "arguments")
            set is_eval_or_arguments: true
        if is_eval_or_arguments = false
            return
        this.error_on_node(name, this.strict_mode_eval_or_arguments_code(context))
    }

    function strict_mode_eval_or_arguments_code(this, n: ref[AstNode]) returns int
    {
        if Binder.get_containing_class(n) <> null
            return DiagCode_contained_in_a_class_is_evaluated_in_JavaScript_s_strict_mode_which_does_not_allow_this_use_of_0_For_more_information_see_https_Colon_Slash_Slashdeveloper_mozilla_org_Slashen_US_Slashdocs_SlashWeb_SlashJavaScript_SlashReference_SlashStrict_mode
        if is_external_module
            return DiagInvalid_use_of_0_Modules_are_automatically_in_strict_mode
        DiagInvalid_use_of_0_in_strict_mode
    }

    procedure check_strict_mode_function_name(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if (AstNode.flags_of(n) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
            return
        this.check_strict_mode_eval_or_arguments(n, AstNode.name_of(n))
    }

    ; ── slice 35: the six strict-mode grammar checks and the private name ────
    ;
    ; Each is the reference's function of the same name, and each is two or three
    ; lines. The group's one interesting property is stated at their bind arms:
    ; not one of them asks whether the code is in strict mode, because tsgo's
    ; binder has no such state.

    ; checkStrictModeCatchClause. The name is the catch VARIABLE's, and `catch { }`
    ; — a catch with no binding at all, which is valid since ES2019 — is the null
    ; this has to test.
    procedure check_strict_mode_catch_clause(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let vd AstNode.catch_variable_declaration_of(n)
        if vd = null
            return
        this.check_strict_mode_eval_or_arguments(n, AstNode.name_of(vd))
    }

    ; checkStrictModeDeleteExpression. The one check of the six that is not about
    ; `eval`/`arguments`: ANY bare identifier operand is the error, and the
    ; diagnostic sits on the OPERAND rather than on the `delete`.
    procedure check_strict_mode_delete_expression(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let e AstNode.expression_of(n)
        if e = null
            return
        if AstNode.kind_of(e) <> KindIdentifier
            return
        this.error_on_node(e, DiagX_delete_cannot_be_called_on_an_identifier_in_strict_mode)
    }

    ; checkStrictModePostfixUnaryExpression / …PrefixUnaryExpression. The pair is
    ; asymmetric and the asymmetry is the reference's: `x++` and `x--` are the only
    ; postfix operators there are, so the postfix arm needs no operator test, while
    ; the prefix arm shares its node kind with `!`, `~`, `+` and `-` and therefore
    ; asks.
    procedure check_strict_mode_postfix_unary_expression(this, n: ref[AstNode])
        this.check_strict_mode_eval_or_arguments(n, AstNode.postfix_unary_operand_of(n))

    procedure check_strict_mode_prefix_unary_expression(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let op AstNode.prefix_unary_operator_kind(n)
        if op = KindPlusPlusToken
        {
            this.check_strict_mode_eval_or_arguments(n, AstNode.prefix_unary_operand_of(n))
            return
        }
        if op = KindMinusMinusToken
            this.check_strict_mode_eval_or_arguments(n, AstNode.prefix_unary_operand_of(n))
    }

    ; checkStrictModeWithStatement. Unconditional, and reported on the FIRST TOKEN
    ; — the `with` keyword — rather than on the statement, which is why
    ; error_on_first_token exists.
    procedure check_strict_mode_with_statement(this, n: ref[AstNode])
        this.error_on_first_token(n, DiagX_with_statements_are_not_allowed_in_strict_mode)

    ; checkStrictModeLabeledStatement. A label may not sit in front of a
    ; DECLARATION — `l: function f() {}`, `l: class C {}`, `l: var v` — and the
    ; span is the LABEL's first token, not the statement's.
    procedure check_strict_mode_labeled_statement(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let st AstNode.statement_of(n)
        if st = null
            return
        var bad Binder.is_declaration_statement(st)
        if AstNode.kind_of(st) = KindVariableStatement
            set bad: true
        if bad
            this.error_on_first_token(AstNode.label_name_of(n), DiagA_label_is_not_allowed_here)
    }

    ; checkPrivateIdentifier. `#constructor` is the one private name the language
    ; reserves, and the check is on the TEXT — which our scanner stores WITH the
    ; leading `#`, because scan_identifier is entered there with a prefix length of
    ; 1 (scanner.scaly says so at its own line).
    ;
    ; ★ The parse-error guard is the same one check_contextual_identifier carries:
    ; a file that already has a syntactic diagnostic gets none of these.
    procedure check_private_identifier(this, n: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if AstNode.text_is(n, "#constructor") = false
            return
        if file_has_no_parse_errors = false
            return
        this.error_on_node(n, DiagX_constructor_is_a_reserved_word)
    }

    ; ── the ast utilities the arms above need ───────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; Each is the reference's function of the same name, ported at the size its
    ; callers here need and no smaller — §3.5br's lesson is that an accessor
    ; narrowed to today's callers costs more the second time.

    function is_binding_pattern(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindObjectBindingPattern
            return true
        if k = KindArrayBindingPattern
            return true
        false
    }

    ; isDeclarationStatementKind, ported whole. Its one caller here is the labeled
    ; statement's check, which also tests KindVariableStatement separately — the
    ; reference does the same (IsDeclarationStatement || IsVariableStatement),
    ; because a variable statement is a STATEMENT that declares and is deliberately
    ; not on this list.
    ;
    ; ★ KindMissingDeclaration is on it. That is not a defensive entry: the parser
    ; builds one for a modifier list with nothing after it (`export;`), and a label
    ; in front of that is exactly the shape the diagnostic is for.
    function is_declaration_statement(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindMissingDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindClassDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindEnumDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindImportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindJSImportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindExportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindExportAssignment
            return true
        if k = KindNamespaceExportDeclaration
            return true
        false
    }

    ; GetRootDeclaration: up through the binding elements to the declaration that
    ; owns them. A BindingElement's parent is the PATTERN and its grandparent the
    ; declaration, which is the reference's `node.Parent.Parent`.
    function get_root_declaration(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        var node n
        while AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindBindingElement
        {
            let p AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
            if p = null
                return node
            let g AstNode.parent_node_of(p)
            if g = null
                return node
            set node: g as ref[AstNode]
        }
        node
    }

    function is_part_of_parameter_declaration(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
        AstNode.kind_of(Binder.get_root_declaration(n)) = KindParameter

    ; GetCombinedNodeFlags / GetCombinedModifierFlags share one shape upstream (a
    ; generic over the flag getter): from the root declaration up through the
    ; declaration list and the statement, OR-ing at each level. Two functions here,
    ; because Scaly has no function-valued parameter for this and a delegate would
    ; make the callee unknown at exactly the place §3.1 refuses to.
    function combined_node_flags(n: ref[AstNode]) returns int
    {
        var node Binder.get_root_declaration(n)
        var flags AstNode.flags_of(node as ref[AstNode])
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindVariableDeclaration
            set node: AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        if node <> null
        {
            if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindVariableDeclarationList
            {
                set flags: flags | AstNode.flags_of(node)
                set node: AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
            }
        }
        if node <> null
        {
            if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindVariableStatement
                set flags: flags | AstNode.flags_of(node)
        }
        flags
    }

    ; GetCombinedModifierFlags, the VALUE — upstream's function, of which
    ; has_combined_modifier below is the one-flag test every caller so far wanted.
    ;
    ; ★ Slice 65 needed the flags themselves: getEffectiveDeclarationFlags ORs Export
    ; and Ambient INTO the combined value and masks the result with its caller's
    ; flagsToCheck, so a boolean answer cannot serve it. The walk is unchanged and
    ; the predicate now reads it, which is why this is a refactor rather than a
    ; second copy — a second copy is how the two answers drift.
    function combined_modifier_flags(this, n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        var node Binder.get_root_declaration(n)
        var flags this.modifier_flags(node)
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindVariableDeclaration
            set node: AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        if node <> null
        {
            if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindVariableDeclarationList
            {
                set flags: flags | this.modifier_flags(node)
                set node: AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
            }
        }
        if node <> null
        {
            if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindVariableStatement
                set flags: flags | this.modifier_flags(node)
        }
        flags
    }

    function has_combined_modifier(this, n: ref[AstNode]?, flag: int) returns bool
        (this.combined_modifier_flags(n) & flag) <> 0

    ; Node.ModifierFlags. Upstream caches the value on the ModifierList when the
    ; parser builds it; this port stores the modifier NODES, so the OR is computed
    ; here — the same OR, since modifiersToFlags is what fills the cache.
    function modifier_flags(this, n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return ModifierFlagsNone
        let mods AstNode.modifiers_of(n)
        if mods = null
            return ModifierFlagsNone
        var flags: int ModifierFlagsNone
        let len mods.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            set flags: flags | AstNode.modifier_to_flag(AstNode.kind_of(mods[i]))
            set i: i + 1
        }
        flags
    }

    function has_syntactic_modifier(this, n: ref[AstNode]?, flag: int) returns bool
        (this.modifier_flags(n) & flag) <> 0

    ; ast.GetFunctionFlags. It is HERE rather than in ast.scaly because its async
    ; arm asks has_syntactic_modifier, which this port keeps on the Binder (the
    ; reference computes the modifier flags in the parser and caches them on the
    ; node; here they are ORed on demand, see modifier_flags).
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE FIRST TEST IS A LAYOUT QUESTION, and it is asked of the KIND for
    ; DeclarationKinds.scaly's reason with the opposite outcome: `node.BodyData()`
    ; is non-nil exactly for the eight structs that embed BodyBase —
    ; FunctionDeclaration, FunctionExpression, ArrowFunction, MethodDeclaration,
    ; ConstructorDeclaration, GetAccessor, SetAccessor and ModuleDeclaration —
    ; which is EXACTLY the set AstNode.body_of answers for, so `body_of` cannot
    ; stand in for the test (a null body is a valid answer for all eight and is
    ; what sets Invalid three lines down) but the set itself needs no table. A
    ; ninth kind gaining a body would have to be added to both.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference's `fallthrough` from the three generator-capable kinds into
    ; the ArrowFunction case is why the async test covers FOUR kinds and the
    ; asterisk test three; written as two tests rather than as a switch, because a
    ; fallthrough has no spelling here and nesting the second inside the first
    ; would lose the arrow.
    function get_function_flags(this, n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return FunctionFlagsInvalid
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if Binder.kind_has_body_data(k) = false
            return FunctionFlagsInvalid
        var flags: int FunctionFlagsNormal
        var generator_capable false
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            set generator_capable: true
        if k = KindFunctionExpression
            set generator_capable: true
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            set generator_capable: true
        if generator_capable
        {
            if AstNode.asterisk_token_of(n) <> null
                set flags: flags | FunctionFlagsGenerator
        }
        var async_capable generator_capable
        if k = KindArrowFunction
            set async_capable: true
        if async_capable
        {
            if this.has_syntactic_modifier(n, ModifierFlagsAsync)
                set flags: flags | FunctionFlagsAsync
        }
        if AstNode.body_of(n) = null
            set flags: flags | FunctionFlagsInvalid
        flags
    }

    ; The eight kinds whose data struct embeds BodyBase — see get_function_flags
    ; for why this is a layout question answered on the kind.
    function kind_has_body_data(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindFunctionExpression
            return true
        if k = KindArrowFunction
            return true
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindConstructor
            return true
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
            return true
        false
    }

    ; A byte of the SOURCE by absolute position — the scanner already answers it
    ; (Scanner.byte_at), and the binder holds the scanner for the same reason it
    ; holds skip_trivia_at: the TEXT lives there.
    ;
    ; ★ Its one caller is is_use_strict_prologue_directive, which compares the
    ; RAW text of a string literal rather than its cooked value; see there for why
    ; the raw form is the question.
    function byte_at(this, p: int) returns int
        trivia.byte_at(p)

    ; isUseStrictPrologueDirective, with the string comparison written out.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS THE RAW TEXT, INCLUDING THE QUOTES, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
    ; The reference reads GetSourceTextOfNodeFromSourceFile and compares against
    ; `"use strict"` and `'use strict'`, with its own comment saying why: *the node
    ; text must be exactly "use strict" or 'use strict'. It is not ok for the string
    ; to contain unicode escapes (as per ES5).* So the literal's COOKED value — what
    ; literal_text_of answers — is the wrong question: `"use\u0020strict"` cooks to
    ; the same ten characters and is not a directive. Twelve bytes, matching quotes
    ; at both ends, `use strict` in between.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE `ReparserTransformedLiteral` ARM OF GetTextOfNodeFromSourceText IS
    ; SKIPPED, and it is a NO-OP here rather than a gap: that arm re-adds the quotes
    ; a reparsed literal's span does not carry, and a node carrying the flag is one
    ; the JSDoc reparser synthesized — which cannot be a statement of a function
    ; BODY, the only list this is asked about. NodeIsMissing is likewise not asked:
    ; a missing node has pos = end, i.e. length 0, which fails the length test.
    function is_use_strict_prologue_directive(this, node: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        let e AstNode.expression_of(node)
        if e = null
            return false
        let start this.skip_trivia_at(AstNode.pos_of(e))
        if AstNode.end_of(e) - start <> 12
            return false
        let quote this.byte_at(start)
        if quote <> 0x22
        {
            if quote <> 0x27
                return false
        }
        if this.byte_at(start + 11) <> quote
            return false
        if this.byte_at(start + 1) <> 0x75      ; u
            return false
        if this.byte_at(start + 2) <> 0x73      ; s
            return false
        if this.byte_at(start + 3) <> 0x65      ; e
            return false
        if this.byte_at(start + 4) <> 0x20      ; space
            return false
        if this.byte_at(start + 5) <> 0x73      ; s
            return false
        if this.byte_at(start + 6) <> 0x74      ; t
            return false
        if this.byte_at(start + 7) <> 0x72      ; r
            return false
        if this.byte_at(start + 8) <> 0x69      ; i
            return false
        if this.byte_at(start + 9) <> 0x63      ; c
            return false
        if this.byte_at(start + 10) <> 0x74     ; t
            return false
        true
    }

    ; binder.FindUseStrictPrologue, whole. Exported upstream and called from
    ; exactly ONE place — checkGrammarForUseStrictSimpleParameterList — which is
    ; why it lives in binder.go without a binder in sight; here it is a method
    ; because the two questions it delegates (the trivia skip and the text) are
    ; the binder's.
    ;
    ; ★ ast.IsPrologueDirective is inlined: an ExpressionStatement whose expression
    ; is a StringLiteral. The `else return nil` is the whole of the directive
    ; PROLOGUE rule — the run of directives ends at the first statement that is not
    ; one, and a `"use strict"` after real code is not a directive.
    function find_use_strict_prologue(this, statements: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if statements = null
            return null
        var i 0
        let n AstNode.list_count(statements)
        while i < n
        {
            let statement AstNode.child_in_list(statements, i)
            set i: i + 1
            if AstNode.kind_of(statement) <> KindExpressionStatement
                return null
            let e AstNode.expression_of(statement)
            if e = null
                return null
            if AstNode.kind_of(e) <> KindStringLiteral
                return null
            if this.is_use_strict_prologue_directive(statement)
                return statement
        }
        null
    }

    ; IsBlockOrCatchScoped.
    function is_block_or_catch_scoped(this, n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if (Binder.combined_node_flags(n) & NodeFlagsBlockScoped) <> 0
            return true
        Binder.is_catch_clause_variable_declaration_or_binding_element(n)
    }

    function is_catch_clause_variable_declaration_or_binding_element(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let root Binder.get_root_declaration(n)
        if AstNode.kind_of(root) <> KindVariableDeclaration
            return false
        let p AstNode.parent_node_of(root)
        if p = null
            return false
        AstNode.kind_of(p) = KindCatchClause
    }

    ; IsIdentifierName: is this identifier a NAME rather than a reference? The
    ; answer is entirely about the parent, which is the second reader that makes
    ; Parent load-bearing in this slice.
    function is_identifier_name(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let p AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
        if p = null
            return false
        let pk AstNode.kind_of(p)
        if Binder.parent_kind_names_by_name_slot(pk)
            return AstNode.name_of(p) = n
        if pk = KindQualifiedName
            return AstNode.qualified_right_of(p) = n
        if pk = KindBindingElement
            return AstNode.property_name_of(p) = n
        if pk = KindImportSpecifier
            return AstNode.property_name_of(p) = n
        if pk = KindExportSpecifier
            return true
        if pk = KindJsxAttribute
            return true
        if pk = KindJsxSelfClosingElement
            return true
        if pk = KindJsxOpeningElement
            return true
        if pk = KindJsxClosingElement
            return true
        false
    }

    function parent_kind_names_by_name_slot(pk: int) returns bool
    {
        if pk = KindPropertyDeclaration
            return true
        if pk = KindPropertySignature
            return true
        if pk = KindMethodDeclaration
            return true
        if pk = KindMethodSignature
            return true
        if pk = KindGetAccessor
            return true
        if pk = KindSetAccessor
            return true
        if pk = KindEnumMember
            return true
        if pk = KindPropertyAssignment
            return true
        if pk = KindPropertyAccessExpression
            return true
        false
    }

    ; GetContainingClass: the nearest class-like ancestor, by walking the parent
    ; chain. Its two callers are the strict-mode messages, which say WHY the binder
    ; thinks the code is in strict mode — a class body always is.
    function get_containing_class(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        var p AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
        while p <> null
        {
            let k AstNode.kind_of(p)
            if k = KindClassDeclaration
                return p
            if k = KindClassExpression
                return p
            set p: AstNode.parent_node_of(p)
        }
        null
    }

    ; IsInTopLevelContext: whether the node's THIS CONTAINER is the source file.
    ;
    ; ★ The first step is not decoration: a class or function DECLARATION's own name
    ; is a binding in the SURROUNDING scope, so the walk starts one level up for it.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ITS UNREACHABILITY CLAIM EXPIRED WITH SLICE 28 and this is the paragraph
    ; that used to carry it: slice 27 ported this properly rather than
    ; approximating it, noting that no fixture could fail because its only caller
    ; sits behind `if is_external_module` and external modules were unported. They
    ; are ported now, and the caller RUNS — `binder_module_await_identifier.ts`
    ; reports TS1262 at the `await` binding through exactly this walk, which is
    ; what makes the whole GetThisContainer list below a checked transcription
    ; instead of a careful one (§3.5p, paid in the direction it usually is: the
    ; slice that lifts a condition owns every comment resting on it).
    function is_in_top_level_context(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        var node n
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindIdentifier
        {
            let p AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
            if p <> null
            {
                let pk AstNode.kind_of(p)
                var is_named_decl false
                if pk = KindFunctionDeclaration
                    set is_named_decl: true
                if pk = KindClassDeclaration
                    set is_named_decl: true
                if is_named_decl
                {
                    if AstNode.name_of(p) = node
                        set node: p as ref[AstNode]
                }
            }
        }
        let c Binder.get_this_container(node, true, false)
        if c = null
            return false
        AstNode.kind_of(c) = KindSourceFile
    }

    ; ast.GetThisContainer, whole, WITH ITS TWO FLAGS — and until slice 97 they
    ; were not parameters but the arguments of the one caller this file had, which
    ; the note that stood here spelled out in as many words: *the two arguments its
    ; only caller here passes, which is why the arrow arm returns and the
    ; computed-name arm only skips*.
    ;
    ; ★★★ A FLAG FROZEN AT A CALLER'S ARGUMENT IS INVISIBLE UNTIL A SECOND CALLER
    ; ARRIVES, AND THEN IT IS NOT A GAP BUT A REWRITE. checkThisExpression asks this
    ; walk FOUR times with three different flag pairs — `(true, true)` at the head,
    ; `(false, !thisInComputedPropertyName)` for the arrow hop,
    ; `(!capturedByArrowFunction, false)` for the computed-name hop and
    ; `(false, false)` from tryGetThisTypeAtEx — so the specialised form answers the
    ; right question for exactly one of them. ★The `includeClassComputedPropertyName`
    ; arm had no body at all here, because with the flag false there is nothing to
    ; return; it is the arm that makes `[this.x]: 1` in a CLASS report.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference carries this function TWICE — `ast.GetThisContainer` and the
    ; checker's own `c.getThisContainer` — and they are the same code, the arrow arm
    ; spelled once as `if include { return }` and once as `if !include { continue };
    ; fallthrough`. One copy here, and it lives in the binder because the binder's
    ; caller came first.
    ;
    ; ★★★ A CLASS IS NOT A THIS CONTAINER in this function, and an approximation
    ; that treated it as one — the first draft of this file did — answers a
    ; different question. The kinds that stop the walk are the reference's own list:
    ; the function-likes, the signatures, a property declaration or signature, a
    ; module or enum declaration, a class STATIC BLOCK, and the source file.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference PANICS on a null parent; this answers null and the caller
    ; reads that as "not the source file", which is the same answer for every tree
    ; the parser can actually build (only a detached node has no parent chain).
    function get_this_container(n: ref[AstNode], include_arrow_functions: bool, include_class_computed_property_name: bool) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        var node: ref[AstNode]? n
        while true
        {
            set node: AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
            if node = null
                return null
            let k AstNode.kind_of(node)

            ; A computed property name is skipped together with the member it names
            ; (`node.Parent.Parent`), because `this` inside it belongs to the scope
            ; AROUND the class — UNLESS the caller asked for the class case, where
            ; the reference's own comment is the specification: *a computed property
            ; name in a class needs to be a this container so that we can error on
            ; it*.
            if k = KindComputedPropertyName
            {
                let p AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
                if p = null
                    return null
                let g AstNode.parent_node_of(p)
                if include_class_computed_property_name
                {
                    if Parser.is_class_like_node(g)
                        return node
                }
                set node: g
                if node = null
                    return null
            }
            else
            {
                ; A decorator is evaluated OUTSIDE the body it decorates, so the
                ; walk jumps over the class element — or, for a parameter
                ; decorator, over the parameter and its element both.
                if k = KindDecorator
                {
                    let p AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
                    if p <> null
                    {
                        if AstNode.kind_of(p) = KindParameter
                        {
                            let g AstNode.parent_node_of(p)
                            if g <> null
                            {
                                if Binder.is_class_element(g)
                                    set node: g
                            }
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            if Binder.is_class_element(p)
                                set node: p
                        }
                    }
                }
                else
                {
                    if k = KindArrowFunction
                    {
                        if include_arrow_functions
                            return node
                    }
                    if Binder.is_this_container_kind(k)
                        return node
                }
            }
        }
        null
    }

    function is_this_container_kind(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindFunctionExpression
            return true
        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindClassStaticBlockDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindPropertyDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindPropertySignature
            return true
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindMethodSignature
            return true
        if k = KindConstructor
            return true
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindCallSignature
            return true
        if k = KindConstructSignature
            return true
        if k = KindIndexSignature
            return true
        if k = KindEnumDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindSourceFile
            return true
        false
    }

    ; ast.IsClassElement.
    function is_class_element(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindConstructor
            return true
        if k = KindPropertyDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindIndexSignature
            return true
        if k = KindClassStaticBlockDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindSemicolonClassElement
            return true
        false
    }

    ; ast.IsVariableDeclarationInitializedToRequire —
    ; isVariableDeclarationInitializedWithRequireHelper with allowAccessedRequire
    ; FALSE, which is the only reading any caller in this port has (the TRUE one
    ; belongs to GetModuleSpecifierOfBareOrAccessedRequire, a checker function).
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT WAS A STAND-IN ANSWERING `false` UNCONDITIONALLY, and slice 37 is
    ; what made the wrong answer visible rather than what introduced it: the two
    ; units that carry `const a = require("./a")` were reported for the
    ; `commonjs-require` marker, so their whole dump was the marker and the alias
    ; symbol nobody declared could not be seen. Porting the require call turned
    ; them into a MISMATCH — a `a` bound as a FunctionScopedVariable with a value
    ; declaration where the reference has an Alias with none, plus a classifiable
    ; name the reference does not have. §3.5be from the other end: **a stand-in
    ; guarded by another marker expires when that marker does**, and the guard is
    ; not visible from the stand-in's own line.
    ;
    ; ★ The `allowAccessedRequire` parameter is NOT kept: §3.5ar asks for the
    ; reference's SIGNATURE where a future caller will need it, and the future
    ; caller here is the CHECKER, which this port has no plan for — where the
    ; reference has two exported wrappers over one helper, this keeps the one
    ; wrapper anything binds through.
    function is_variable_declaration_initialized_to_require(this, n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        var d: ref[AstNode]? n
        ; A BINDING ELEMENT asks about the variable declaration it belongs to —
        ; Parent is the binding pattern, Parent.Parent the declaration — so
        ; `const { a } = require("m")` makes every element an alias.
        if AstNode.kind_of(d) = KindBindingElement
        {
            let bp AstNode.parent_node_of(d)
            if bp = null
                return false
            set d: AstNode.parent_node_of(bp)
            if d = null
                return false
        }
        if Parser.is_in_js_file(d) = false
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(d) <> KindVariableDeclaration
            return false
        ; `init` is a reserved word here, so the local is spelled out.
        let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(d)
        if initializer = null
            return false
        ; `node.Parent.Parent.ModifierFlags()` — the declaration LIST's parent,
        ; i.e. the variable statement, since that is where `export` is written.
        let list AstNode.parent_node_of(d)
        if list = null
            return false
        let stmt AstNode.parent_node_of(list)
        if this.has_syntactic_modifier(stmt, ModifierFlagsExport)
            return false
        if AstNode.type_of(d) <> null
            return false
        ; ★ TRUE here, unlike the binder's own `require` arm: an aliasing
        ; declaration needs a module SPECIFIER it can resolve, while the CommonJS
        ; indicator only needs the call to exist.
        Parser.is_require_call(initializer, true)
    }

    ; ast.IsParameterPropertyDeclaration: a parameter carrying an ACCESSIBILITY,
    ; `readonly` or `override` modifier, inside a CONSTRUCTOR — the shape that
    ; declares a class property and a parameter in one breath. Both halves are
    ; needed: without the modifier test every constructor parameter would declare a
    ; property, and without the parent test a modifier on any parameter would.
    ;
    ; ★ Slice 39: the reference's THIRD conjunct is `IsParameterDeclaration(node)`,
    ; a kind test, and it is written out here although the only caller is
    ; bind_parameter's Parameter arm — the argument bind_function_expression makes
    ; for keeping its own indistinguishable kind test: a reader looking for the
    ; reference's condition has to find all of it, and a predicate narrowed to
    ; today's callers is what the next slice re-invents wrongly (§3.5br, §3.5by).
    function is_parameter_property_declaration(this, n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) <> KindParameter
            return false
        let p AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
        if p = null
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(p) <> KindConstructor
            return false
        let f this.modifier_flags(n)
        if (f & ModifierFlagsPublic) <> 0
            return true
        if (f & ModifierFlagsPrivate) <> 0
            return true
        if (f & ModifierFlagsProtected) <> 0
            return true
        if (f & ModifierFlagsReadonly) <> 0
            return true
        if (f & ModifierFlagsOverride) <> 0
            return true
        false
    }
}
